direct.github.com/Ehsan-hash) in\n[#&#8203;1561](https://redirect.github.com/jdx/usage/pull/1561)\n\n##### 🛡️ Security\n\n- replace prettier with oxfmt by\n[@&#8203;jdx](https://redirect.github.com/jdx) in\n[#&#8203;1560](https://redirect.github.com/jdx/usage/pull/1560)\n\n##### 🔍 Other Changes\n\n- **(release)** shorten minimum release age to 2 days by\n[@&#8203;jdx](https://redirect.github.com/jdx) in\n[52b1b78](https://redirect.github.com/jdx/usage/commit/52b1b7874590f2e6d5becaa86d0395647dce7991)\n- pin perf-runner image by tag and stop automerging it by\n[@&#8203;jdx](https://redirect.github.com/jdx) in\n[#&#8203;1562](https://redirect.github.com/jdx/usage/pull/1562)\n\n##### New Contributors\n\n- [@&#8203;Ehsan-hash](https://redirect.github.com/Ehsan-hash) made\ntheir first contribution in\n[#&#8203;1561](https://redirect.github.com/jdx/usage/pull/1561)\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Configuration\n\n📅 **Schedule**: (in timezone America/Chicago)\n\n- Branch creation\n  - At any time (no schedule defined)\n- Automerge\n  - At any time (no schedule defined)\n\n🚦 **Automerge**: Enabled.\n\n♻ **Rebasing**: Whenever PR becomes conflicted, or you tick the\nrebase/retry checkbox.\n\n👻 **Immortal**: This PR will be recreated if closed unmerged. Get\n[config\nhelp](https://redirect.github.com/renovatebot/renovate/discussions) if\nthat's undesired.\n\n---\n\n- [ ] <!-- rebase-check -->If you want to rebase/retry this PR, check\nthis box\n\n---\n\nThis PR was generated by [Mend Renovate](https://mend.io/renovate/).\nView the [repository job\nlog](https://developer.mend.io/github/jdx/mr-boxington).\n\n<!--renovate-debug:eyJjcmVhdGVkSW5WZXIiOiI0NC4xNjQuMSIsInVwZGF0ZWRJblZlciI6IjQ0LjE2NC4xIiwidGFyZ2V0QnJhbmNoIjoibWFpbiIsImxhYmVscyI6W119-->\n\nCo-authored-by: renovate[bot] <29139614+renovate[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-17T12:34:53Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a/comments","author":{"login":"renovate[bot]","id":29139614,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90MjkxMzk2MTQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/2740?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/renovate","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"1725bbe70a76ac3f1e2fc72bf1420ae376e9399c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1725bbe70a76ac3f1e2fc72bf1420ae376e9399c","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/1725bbe70a76ac3f1e2fc72bf1420ae376e9399c"}]},{"sha":"62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDYyNjcwZjA0OWRlOWM4YzI3N2E0MThjZjliM2UwMTg0NWViMzVmZmE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-17T14:42:27Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-17T14:42:27Z"},"message":"test(seed): run the access-time seeding test on Windows volumes that track access times (#730)\n\n`target_seed::tests::only_units_the_donors_last_build_read_are_copied`\nfails on Windows whenever the volume records last-access times. The OS\nerror is 5 (`ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED`); the message below is localized:\n\n```\ncalled `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Os { code: 5, kind: PermissionDenied, message: \"アクセスが拒否されました。\" }\n```\n\nThe test backdates the stale unit's `fingerprint` directory with\n`File::open(dir).set_modified(..)`. Windows opens a directory only with\n`FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS`, and setting times needs\n`FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES`, which a read-only open does not request. On\nWindows the test now opens the directory with both; other platforms keep\nthe plain open.\n\nCI does not show the failure because the test returns early when\n`access_times_tracked` reports that the volume does not update access\ntimes, which appears to be the case on the `windows-latest` runner. On\nthis Windows 11 machine `fsutil behavior query disablelastaccess`\nreports `DisableLastAccess = 2 (System Managed, Last Access Time Updates\nENABLED)`, so the test runs past that check and fails.\n\nValidation on Windows 11 with Rust 1.99.1 and last-access updates\nenabled:\n\n- before: `mbx test -p mbx --lib target_seed` → 19 passed, 1 failed (the\nerror above)\n- after: `mbx test -p mbx --lib target_seed` → 20 passed\n\nOnly the test changes; mbx behavior is unchanged.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Tests**\n* Improved cross-platform reliability of a test that checks stale unit\ndetection.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7c9c64f4bfbc3d98f387a7a1df834f7efe1866f3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/7c9c64f4bfbc3d98f387a7a1df834f7efe1866f3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq04lTCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7zYQAEHbUtekTIbvCyWFdqedo9qt\nTWzQo+YycMnpYL3pe+AEwtUWQznHCq47tIiMCXwzbURvGzkYxO684MbDvnRUI5O1\nx91AeBMxHow1IasyNZI740stHrfILzZ34eRGI4EApJbAn58MvSLZ5YXjXzHmieU9\nBL+2rw1PKPUjUEEyNNUnIbuu36aFRs5QNjayOBFy/FgT6VHF5/fy8MYVRdRT7/rs\nt2J3BsCB095sNtV3Gsfy5ptmU9N3EGT6hKkJI1iFstJa0+mb/JdbZRpokAqtWwbF\nZtbfKDMbIX63TfFx1u5MyKpb2/iP8dO7GxZHWHtebFvvoFOA1VMnkeZAYapFW+/N\n/uJVOfvuBy6XkUGsXCTfEwbj24r030GtTClwqGwH7QZsJWBlMx3iw3d2+aYA6qJb\ngtT9t5U4LW651VdtZU239zbLVI9h3bzHloAlQJLMwUo7buy+t0AM808Brw50UY5v\nJKSxVXiGxadryIP+QEudPDIVNSQ79PCCB07DDfDvnYKXI+x8cL4eRzzJO8ihosdp\nDyRHDQ1eeEqZpx1qf6lk8ChGuwjbeHSUy1f5s5dCu9sc//+Qj8sHkPhQIWMFnBQt\nfMG6qoLcnMxkRyYX/C7290q7s9hcrVis82pmdJZiq8IzPzniqW8MuwS+BHDhretp\nhjzD91EYcY0unzhzRMjk\n=85SG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7c9c64f4bfbc3d98f387a7a1df834f7efe1866f3\nparent 2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792248147 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792248147 -0500\n\ntest(seed): run the access-time seeding test on Windows volumes that track access times (#730)\n\n`target_seed::tests::only_units_the_donors_last_build_read_are_copied`\nfails on Windows whenever the volume records last-access times. The OS\nerror is 5 (`ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED`); the message below is localized:\n\n```\ncalled `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Os { code: 5, kind: PermissionDenied, message: \"アクセスが拒否されました。\" }\n```\n\nThe test backdates the stale unit's `fingerprint` directory with\n`File::open(dir).set_modified(..)`. Windows opens a directory only with\n`FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS`, and setting times needs\n`FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES`, which a read-only open does not request. On\nWindows the test now opens the directory with both; other platforms keep\nthe plain open.\n\nCI does not show the failure because the test returns early when\n`access_times_tracked` reports that the volume does not update access\ntimes, which appears to be the case on the `windows-latest` runner. On\nthis Windows 11 machine `fsutil behavior query disablelastaccess`\nreports `DisableLastAccess = 2 (System Managed, Last Access Time Updates\nENABLED)`, so the test runs past that check and fails.\n\nValidation on Windows 11 with Rust 1.99.1 and last-access updates\nenabled:\n\n- before: `mbx test -p mbx --lib target_seed` → 19 passed, 1 failed (the\nerror above)\n- after: `mbx test -p mbx --lib target_seed` → 20 passed\n\nOnly the test changes; mbx behavior is unchanged.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Tests**\n* Improved cross-platform reliability of a test that checks stale unit\ndetection.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-17T14:42:27Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2791d50164783a0a5562bca848b47c93fe71392a"}]},{"sha":"790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDc5MDIwNGM3NTAwYmFjMzk4YTlhNDkyMjUwZGMzMjVhMDc3YzkxN2I","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-17T16:14:52Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-17T16:14:52Z"},"message":"fix(cli): let cargo clean -p run when a managed target link dangles (#732)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/181\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nAutomatic collection removes a managed target directory but cannot\nremove the `target` link inside the checkout, so the link is left\ndangling until the next build places the directory again. In that state\n`cargo clean -p <package>` through the Cargo shim failed, because Cargo\ncannot create a directory through a dangling link:\n\n```\n$ cargo clean -p codegen-rebuild\nerror: failed to create directory `…/target`\n\nCaused by:\n  Not a directory (os error 20)\n```\n\nNow, before a `cargo clean` runs, the shim removes the checkout's\n`target` link if it points at that checkout's own managed directory and\nthe directory is gone. `cargo clean -p` then reports `Removed 0 files`\nand exits 0, and the next `cargo build` places and links the managed\ntarget again. A link to anywhere else, a link whose directory still\nexists, and a view that a running command holds are left alone.\n\nI checked this by building a small crate with a managed target, deleting\nthe managed directory and its record, and running `cargo clean -p`.\nPlain Cargo fails with the error above. Through the shim it succeeds,\nand a following build restores the link.\n\nFixes #731\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Medium Risk**\n> Changes symlink removal and shim passthrough behavior around managed\ntargets; safeguards limit edits to dangling own-view links, but\nfilesystem races and lease checks are still sensitive to build tooling.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes **`cargo clean`** (including **`cargo clean -p`**) failing when\nautomatic collection removed a managed target directory but left the\ncheckout’s **`target`** symlink pointing at the missing view—Cargo then\nerrors trying to create a directory through a dangling link.\n> \n> The Cargo shim now runs **best-effort cleanup before `cargo clean`**\non passthrough paths (inside an mbx session or proxy passthrough), via\nnew **`drop_dangling_managed_link`**. Shared logic lives in\n**`remove_dangling_link`**, which removes the link only when it points\nat **this checkout’s** managed view, that view is **gone**, and no\ncommand holds the view lease; other symlinks and active views are\nuntouched. **`remove_workspace`** reuses the same helper when there is\nno view record. Failures are logged and do not block Cargo.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n1934d18cefef7b239e62991b84ed33a4df8d19b7. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* `cargo clean` now removes a dangling managed `target` link when safe,\nincluding inside an mbx session or in passthrough mode.\n* Links are preserved if the managed target exists, the link points\nelsewhere, or the target is in use. Cleanup errors do not prevent Cargo\nfrom running.\n* Workspace cleanup removes dangling managed target links even when no\nworkspace record exists.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"53e8ed686fc0d5e5e4c9482e12341c767fd57501","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/53e8ed686fc0d5e5e4c9482e12341c767fd57501"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq0578CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3h4QADbTAW1PVgXTUokX7Blwqwtg\nunuVl2fH5tHomfAKDCCAcM3NBIzLyY9ie1wWo3R9bM3vt2/04n1zMWLUT5fgbHw6\n7rk0IQl6mQjS379oeV0LxmjcjBYhZQc+CMvwzcuZm5PvBQNVL+BtDsZ5iOfs7yv4\n2OIL/vs4duDGWUdEbm3cKLdC5OzbjsEKRqFjEG51qe34XAzbIXKorw6GXSc497T7\nzATynXvY837XW4gORCz15dZSeUcVeioubYzVTCZFHKyPw07fIDdbOXuUKPZgjACt\np5Meps6sbqfoX+JaJfhb99ebijzBu4RVEJxVKHfSKWkcfZ0q6lu7yUun6v9E8Pu3\nPrJJ6LyVQ1yaJYk+iFzR5oLJXelkAaAtdH3BwsN/yppSSk5OJgyEKyp0va20cQG9\nOzFNV8cE1Pur+OEZaMxxiLT94a8Qmn6o3XUJ5dMNRDBV7ANbs4NOLOxiFzgCOXZf\nOi6Dq/O2y3YafBLgDJZVHxxiHzfHkrY4/QihoLh3WfzmYw8PLUc6y07XNmyNy9R4\nwEFpedsJKNmLpC3o+hyNLEahjHyJqVk6bfSN9ITWLxhF/pjcMAGjJ/q0jSaqkwxg\nhLrlxeGTbLhjP08CcqdFoRsODAuMnzO7V2Nke94EB/LfGKQyr/rmSFyAcDZPBXnT\n2foEhFdq8zr7Ky4xsqVC\n=tClu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 53e8ed686fc0d5e5e4c9482e12341c767fd57501\nparent 62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792253692 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792253692 -0500\n\nfix(cli): let cargo clean -p run when a managed target link dangles (#732)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/181\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nAutomatic collection removes a managed target directory but cannot\nremove the `target` link inside the checkout, so the link is left\ndangling until the next build places the directory again. In that state\n`cargo clean -p <package>` through the Cargo shim failed, because Cargo\ncannot create a directory through a dangling link:\n\n```\n$ cargo clean -p codegen-rebuild\nerror: failed to create directory `…/target`\n\nCaused by:\n  Not a directory (os error 20)\n```\n\nNow, before a `cargo clean` runs, the shim removes the checkout's\n`target` link if it points at that checkout's own managed directory and\nthe directory is gone. `cargo clean -p` then reports `Removed 0 files`\nand exits 0, and the next `cargo build` places and links the managed\ntarget again. A link to anywhere else, a link whose directory still\nexists, and a view that a running command holds are left alone.\n\nI checked this by building a small crate with a managed target, deleting\nthe managed directory and its record, and running `cargo clean -p`.\nPlain Cargo fails with the error above. Through the shim it succeeds,\nand a following build restores the link.\n\nFixes #731\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Medium Risk**\n> Changes symlink removal and shim passthrough behavior around managed\ntargets; safeguards limit edits to dangling own-view links, but\nfilesystem races and lease checks are still sensitive to build tooling.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes **`cargo clean`** (including **`cargo clean -p`**) failing when\nautomatic collection removed a managed target directory but left the\ncheckout’s **`target`** symlink pointing at the missing view—Cargo then\nerrors trying to create a directory through a dangling link.\n> \n> The Cargo shim now runs **best-effort cleanup before `cargo clean`**\non passthrough paths (inside an mbx session or proxy passthrough), via\nnew **`drop_dangling_managed_link`**. Shared logic lives in\n**`remove_dangling_link`**, which removes the link only when it points\nat **this checkout’s** managed view, that view is **gone**, and no\ncommand holds the view lease; other symlinks and active views are\nuntouched. **`remove_workspace`** reuses the same helper when there is\nno view record. Failures are logged and do not block Cargo.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n1934d18cefef7b239e62991b84ed33a4df8d19b7. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* `cargo clean` now removes a dangling managed `target` link when safe,\nincluding inside an mbx session or in passthrough mode.\n* Links are preserved if the managed target exists, the link points\nelsewhere, or the target is in use. Cleanup errors do not prevent Cargo\nfrom running.\n* Workspace cleanup removes dangling managed target links even when no\nworkspace record exists.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-17T16:14:52Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/62670f049de9c8c277a418cf9b3e01845eb35ffa"}]},{"sha":"4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDQ0OTJhZDBkYTQ3YjA1NTcwNDRlODE0M2E3YjlmNmU0ZjllNTdiMzg","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-17T17:03:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-17T17:03:59Z"},"message":"chore: fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows (#733)\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`\nfails on Windows. CI runs clippy only on Linux, where `#[cfg]` removes\nthe code that triggers these lints, and Linux CI uses Rust 1.94 while\nthe `windows-latest` runner ships 1.99. This PR makes clippy pass on\nWindows with Rust 1.99 and adds a clippy step to the Windows `test` job\nin `ci.yml`, as pitchfork already does, so Windows-only lints fail CI\ninstead of reaching users.\n\nThe findings fall into three groups:\n\n- **Items only Unix uses.** Test imports (`resolve_in_path`,\n`resolve_on_path`, `symlink_shim`, `ShimLease`, `first_in_path`), the\n`SentinelLedger` test helper, and `Donor::respellings` are used only by\n`#[cfg(unix)]` code, so they are now `#[cfg(unix)]` as well. The fields\nof `target_seed::Links` are read only when Unix copies a symbolic link.\nOn Windows a unit holding one is left for the build, so the struct\nallows `dead_code` there.\n- **`clippy::permissions_set_readonly_false`.** The lint warns that\n`set_readonly(false)` makes a Unix file world-writable. The four calls\nare in `#[cfg(windows)]` or `#[cfg(not(unix))]` code, where they clear\nonly the read-only attribute, so each call is allowed in place with a\ncomment.\n- **`clippy::chunks_exact_to_as_chunks` (new in Rust 1.99).** The\ntest-history reader in `scheduler.rs` and the UTF-16 reader for the\nWindows Cargo shim's `mbx-target` file now use `as_chunks`, which is\nstable since Rust 1.88, within the 1.91 MSRV. Behavior is unchanged: the\ntest-history reader still ignores a partial trailing record, and the\nshim target reader still rejects an odd byte count.\n\nNo user-visible behavior changes.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Windows 11, Rust 1.99.1: `mbx clippy --workspace --all-features\n--all-targets -- -D warnings` and the same with `--release` both exit 0.\nBefore this change, both fail with the findings above. `mbx fmt --all\n--check` passes. `mbx test -p mbx --lib` (849 passed) and `mbx test -p\nmbx-cache-cc -p mbx-cache-core --lib` pass, including the\n`nextest_history_*` and `setup_*` tests that cover the two `as_chunks`\nrewrites.\n- Linux (Debian container), Rust 1.94.1, the minor version Linux CI\nuses: `cargo fmt --all --check` and `cargo clippy --workspace\n--all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` exit 0, and `cargo test -p\nmbx --lib` passes (938 passed).\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Windows setup now rejects target data with an incomplete trailing byte\ninstead of attempting to decode it.\n* **Chores**\n* Expanded Windows lint checks and adjusted platform-specific build and\ntest coverage.\n* Clarified how read-only permissions are handled on Windows and other\nnon-Unix platforms.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"3f1155f6c5d62b9f5d2fcfb3345c2e13dfac6dd2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/3f1155f6c5d62b9f5d2fcfb3345c2e13dfac6dd2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq06p/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAArk0QAJPIW+kjOQq9VB7+zmoFDly5\n5sYu+qmTq+KWk2m/gslqxz0CZsVqge8jgJ9L3Z5ayS1SyWLejvyup1zMslo8nt5s\nhHFW7TAiRCOocMKDRxWlqwkN5VdOSf3S3he+nZ8nmBsSdfk1msTDOYpEEHDI2gbV\nAK10o/Pp/rXY6g5wjnapl4BjeDTqb8ATuB/KMViaE6sxdmQyVJOxdQUk27kqw3JO\nIVdXXh6X4Hf+MMJml+pGmJX+oj/+Ta7rgRC9ES0PAKD5Y0sOJfKVbGKFQcp3ngN+\n327qbB0FqRBq4f4cOkAQX22s54y3WIs9OQSoMlnGNoFcEu94G6WLrc136RrqTdbx\nMpfOtYIIvCbCNJ7I2jLUTms4RivNBjQAlYw6EvblvB8yW/3wQFe0FuZx8dp+RmV/\nSEqPl++CUyyx6HoknXh5p90uh7uH2Xm9Fse0mo9r6Sy8sZoHSDIUX1nMLH/uH4pq\nZoMws+mULKkWkEkBd031usaYJEaqZmghB8YSkkKo/pBuHJ36qmqqCVLbhyx/ohe9\n4N6Tra54FquDF+w4xRcUgIWMmorrc0XTxsOKyx1ksPHoXvJ+6/zfYBUwlQ2If0j5\ns5eesODUSRjjTNJOOKsR5UJLx52lqeHYUAVAtyHNGnM/IPcKoPyDdT9tCvy7NRsF\ny7+mV2gIfYe5eI9QZFTJ\n=nO8D\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 3f1155f6c5d62b9f5d2fcfb3345c2e13dfac6dd2\nparent 790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792256639 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792256639 -0500\n\nchore: fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows (#733)\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`\nfails on Windows. CI runs clippy only on Linux, where `#[cfg]` removes\nthe code that triggers these lints, and Linux CI uses Rust 1.94 while\nthe `windows-latest` runner ships 1.99. This PR makes clippy pass on\nWindows with Rust 1.99 and adds a clippy step to the Windows `test` job\nin `ci.yml`, as pitchfork already does, so Windows-only lints fail CI\ninstead of reaching users.\n\nThe findings fall into three groups:\n\n- **Items only Unix uses.** Test imports (`resolve_in_path`,\n`resolve_on_path`, `symlink_shim`, `ShimLease`, `first_in_path`), the\n`SentinelLedger` test helper, and `Donor::respellings` are used only by\n`#[cfg(unix)]` code, so they are now `#[cfg(unix)]` as well. The fields\nof `target_seed::Links` are read only when Unix copies a symbolic link.\nOn Windows a unit holding one is left for the build, so the struct\nallows `dead_code` there.\n- **`clippy::permissions_set_readonly_false`.** The lint warns that\n`set_readonly(false)` makes a Unix file world-writable. The four calls\nare in `#[cfg(windows)]` or `#[cfg(not(unix))]` code, where they clear\nonly the read-only attribute, so each call is allowed in place with a\ncomment.\n- **`clippy::chunks_exact_to_as_chunks` (new in Rust 1.99).** The\ntest-history reader in `scheduler.rs` and the UTF-16 reader for the\nWindows Cargo shim's `mbx-target` file now use `as_chunks`, which is\nstable since Rust 1.88, within the 1.91 MSRV. Behavior is unchanged: the\ntest-history reader still ignores a partial trailing record, and the\nshim target reader still rejects an odd byte count.\n\nNo user-visible behavior changes.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Windows 11, Rust 1.99.1: `mbx clippy --workspace --all-features\n--all-targets -- -D warnings` and the same with `--release` both exit 0.\nBefore this change, both fail with the findings above. `mbx fmt --all\n--check` passes. `mbx test -p mbx --lib` (849 passed) and `mbx test -p\nmbx-cache-cc -p mbx-cache-core --lib` pass, including the\n`nextest_history_*` and `setup_*` tests that cover the two `as_chunks`\nrewrites.\n- Linux (Debian container), Rust 1.94.1, the minor version Linux CI\nuses: `cargo fmt --all --check` and `cargo clippy --workspace\n--all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` exit 0, and `cargo test -p\nmbx --lib` passes (938 passed).\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Windows setup now rejects target data with an incomplete trailing byte\ninstead of attempting to decode it.\n* **Chores**\n* Expanded Windows lint checks and adjusted platform-specific build and\ntest coverage.\n* Clarified how read-only permissions are handled on Windows and other\nnon-Unix platforms.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-17T17:03:59Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/790204c7500bac398a9a492250dc325a077c917b"}]},{"sha":"7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDdkOTc3OTZhNWUxZTk3MDE5ZWI5MTgyMjIyZmE2YzE1NDlmMzEyYWM","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-17T19:03:31Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-17T19:03:31Z"},"message":"fix(cc): identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text (#735)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/182\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nFixes #734.\n\nOn some installs a valid `cl.exe` made mbx bypass the C/C++ cache with\n`could not establish compiler identity: <compiler> did not report an\nMSVC compiler identity`. mbx required the literal English banner\n`Microsoft (R) C/C++ Optimizing Compiler`, which is localized and can be\nreworded.\n\nmbx now preprocesses a tiny source with `cl /nologo /EP` and reads\n`_MSC_VER`, `_MSC_FULL_VER` and `_MSC_BUILD`. A compiler that leaves\nthose macros undefined is not treated as MSVC, and one that defines\n`__clang__` (clang-cl) is rejected. The cache key now carries those\nversion numbers, so it no longer depends on banner wording or locale.\n\nValidation: unit tests for the macro parser (CRLF output, undefined\nmacros, clang-cl) pass on Linux. The `cl.exe` invocation itself was not\nrun on Windows.\n\nNot included: target metadata and content fingerprints of toolchain\nbinaries in the key, also suggested in the issue. The target still comes\nfrom `VSCMD_ARG_TGT_ARCH`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Medium Risk**\n> Changes Windows C/C++ cache identity and classification logic;\nincorrect parsing would bypass caching or split keys, though behavior is\ncovered by new unit and optional real-`cl` tests.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> **MSVC compiler identity no longer depends on the English `cl.exe`\nbanner**, which was causing cache bypass on localized or differently\nworded installs (fixes #734).\n> \n> Identity is now obtained by preprocessing a tiny source with\n**`/nologo /EP`**, parsing `_MSC_VER`, `_MSC_FULL_VER`, `_MSC_BUILD`,\nand target-arch macros into a stable `mbx-msvc-identity …` string.\n**`clang-cl` is excluded** when `__clang__` is defined, and\n**`CcCompilerFamily::classify`** treats MSVC only when the probe starts\nwith that prefix (replacing `/Bv` plus banner substring matching). Cache\nkeys therefore include version numbers and **ARCH** (x64 vs ARM64, etc.)\ninstead of banner text.\n> \n> Tests cover macro parsing (CRLF, missing macros, clang-cl, arch\nseparation) and a Windows integration test that runs the real `cl` probe\nwhen available.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n7b85c54e4e39ba4476bcb5d6a560279ac2adf4a6. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Improved Microsoft Visual C++ compiler detection by using\ncompiler-provided version information instead of banner text, making\ndetection less dependent on output wording.\n* Clang-cl is no longer classified as MSVC. Missing or unavailable\nversion information is reported as an unavailable compiler identity.\n* Compiler identities include detected target architecture,\ndistinguishing x64 and ARM64 builds.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"bdacb9abea14f50b6af0d057037947364e029b49","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/bdacb9abea14f50b6af0d057037947364e029b49"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq08aDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0qwQAD03GRHP5Qd3dLf6XbbH4J67\nQK0T9jyMaild0tc65cJ6HEQyipMbZPTl2mWZ8Sw56Ao+W4aXPPwTgyg4BugslH1C\nFF1qLO25P4ccX/s56lUbwSoruNkasntbIAC0LsKbioXUYC39AJuDjBjNdn7/dyuH\nPHIsfuyrJ9xKQBHBuIjO2nN4pLkTkTqnNxVVyuN/O/HWRbiGGGD3SMIO8OK0Hc7q\n5PyWFJN9NRjyyuhvGaW+NWQsC8iyvqD7/s0v4/qdjeL9bpw35UjSxrFL2QlpaMWE\ncO0pkO80kdfwyuZLRwYRrOM3RblpQ6SMwz0ZVEOFC5UqfhP8tQrviIE58yTkX14R\nvD/qlkBVaVWZ6uLB2IMM+rcwVmE/jtqTXcD1eoo7UZcM2MhiIZVMktu3VWTyMMMm\nAFv8bJRFhmoFYZiEDQrnHerw/UGQXnRF4VH8/qT4q2a3cIPqGwPg1qI/6kLxuC6M\nWq9KnN69/kY3G+T5Rx4FqWbfLfUEla7CnWMSdXeBSsbInB+sfaTjqNcNnr28OeRG\n7gU3u71cNT3OMLfY1TNmNkULFqeJzX2jD4QNxT9iAamETi3cmoI8aH9xpKCfSwJ4\nGwn6BtpQtyADaU3Nk39lF9nyXcnJOyZ6uJ5VR8QYECEMgmhs75wPI15QpEjVBwGf\nLOSTvoahdDQH/wh9OuC7\n=GWnB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree bdacb9abea14f50b6af0d057037947364e029b49\nparent 4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792263811 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792263811 -0500\n\nfix(cc): identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text (#735)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/182\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nFixes #734.\n\nOn some installs a valid `cl.exe` made mbx bypass the C/C++ cache with\n`could not establish compiler identity: <compiler> did not report an\nMSVC compiler identity`. mbx required the literal English banner\n`Microsoft (R) C/C++ Optimizing Compiler`, which is localized and can be\nreworded.\n\nmbx now preprocesses a tiny source with `cl /nologo /EP` and reads\n`_MSC_VER`, `_MSC_FULL_VER` and `_MSC_BUILD`. A compiler that leaves\nthose macros undefined is not treated as MSVC, and one that defines\n`__clang__` (clang-cl) is rejected. The cache key now carries those\nversion numbers, so it no longer depends on banner wording or locale.\n\nValidation: unit tests for the macro parser (CRLF output, undefined\nmacros, clang-cl) pass on Linux. The `cl.exe` invocation itself was not\nrun on Windows.\n\nNot included: target metadata and content fingerprints of toolchain\nbinaries in the key, also suggested in the issue. The target still comes\nfrom `VSCMD_ARG_TGT_ARCH`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Medium Risk**\n> Changes Windows C/C++ cache identity and classification logic;\nincorrect parsing would bypass caching or split keys, though behavior is\ncovered by new unit and optional real-`cl` tests.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> **MSVC compiler identity no longer depends on the English `cl.exe`\nbanner**, which was causing cache bypass on localized or differently\nworded installs (fixes #734).\n> \n> Identity is now obtained by preprocessing a tiny source with\n**`/nologo /EP`**, parsing `_MSC_VER`, `_MSC_FULL_VER`, `_MSC_BUILD`,\nand target-arch macros into a stable `mbx-msvc-identity …` string.\n**`clang-cl` is excluded** when `__clang__` is defined, and\n**`CcCompilerFamily::classify`** treats MSVC only when the probe starts\nwith that prefix (replacing `/Bv` plus banner substring matching). Cache\nkeys therefore include version numbers and **ARCH** (x64 vs ARM64, etc.)\ninstead of banner text.\n> \n> Tests cover macro parsing (CRLF, missing macros, clang-cl, arch\nseparation) and a Windows integration test that runs the real `cl` probe\nwhen available.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n7b85c54e4e39ba4476bcb5d6a560279ac2adf4a6. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Improved Microsoft Visual C++ compiler detection by using\ncompiler-provided version information instead of banner text, making\ndetection less dependent on output wording.\n* Clang-cl is no longer classified as MSVC. Missing or unavailable\nversion information is reported as an unavailable compiler identity.\n* Compiler identities include detected target architecture,\ndistinguishing x64 and ARM64 builds.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-17T19:03:32Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/4492ad0da47b0557044e8143a7b9f6e4f9e57b38"}]},{"sha":"00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDAwZDZkY2RkODA1ZTQxNDNkM2E3OGFkMzNlYzkyYzFiMWE2MDNjZDI","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-18T00:15:38Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T00:15:38Z"},"message":"fix(setup): skip mise's mbx shim when the Windows cargo.exe shim forwards (#736)\n\nOn Windows, plain `cargo` commands through the standalone shim that `mbx\nsetup` installs at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\cargo.exe` fail in a shell\nwith mise activated, in any directory whose mise config does not enable\nmr-boxington:\n\n```\nmise ERROR No version is set for shim: mbx\n```\n\n`cargo.exe` forwards to the first `mbx.exe` on `PATH` before it reads\nthe mbx recorded in `mbx-target`. With mise activated,\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mise\\shims` is on `PATH`, and mise's `mbx.exe` shim\nthere fails wherever mise does not select an mbx version. This happens\neven though the recorded mbx exists.\n\nThe shim still resolves the active mbx from `PATH` first, as\n`docs/setup.md` documents, but now skips an `mbx.exe` in mise's shims\ndirectory and falls through to the next `mbx.exe` on `PATH` or the\nrecorded one. mise's shims directory is `shims` under a directory named\n`mise` or under `MISE_DATA_DIR`, or the directory `MISE_SHIMS_DIR`\nnames; on Windows the names compare without regard to case. A\n`shims_dir` set only in mise's global config is not visible to mbx. mbx\nalready used the same check, minus `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` and the case\nhandling, to exclude mise's `cargo.exe` shim while resolving the real\nCargo, and that exclusion now gains both. A real `mbx.exe` elsewhere on\n`PATH`, such as a mise install directory added by `mise activate`, still\nwins over the recorded mbx. Unix is not affected: its launcher runs the\nrecorded mbx first and falls back to `command -v mbx` only when that\nfile is gone.\n\nOne tradeoff: with shims-only mise activation, a directory that does\nenable mbx through mise now runs the recorded mbx instead of mise's\nselected version. Rerunning `mbx setup` records the current one.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nWindows 11, PowerShell 7, mise 2026.10.11, Rust 1.99.1:\n\n1. Copy `mbx.exe` to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\cargo.exe`.\n2. Write that `mbx.exe`'s absolute path to\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\mbx-target` as UTF-16LE without a BOM, the\nformat `mbx setup` writes.\n3. In an empty directory where mr-boxington is not enabled, with\n`RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1` and `PATH =\n<mbx\\bin>;<mise\\shims>;<mise>;<~\\.cargo\\bin>;System32`, run `cargo.exe\n--version`.\n\nBuilt from `main` (7d97796) and from this branch, with the file\n`mbx-target` names present in both runs:\n\n| Build | With `mise\\shims` on `PATH` | Without it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | `mise ERROR No version is set for shim: mbx`, exit 1 | `cargo\n1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 |\n| this branch | `cargo 1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 | `cargo\n1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 |\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cli::setup_tests::cargo_shim_skips_the_mise_mbx_shim_on_path`\n(Windows): with a recorded mbx and an `mbx.exe` under `mise\\shims` on\n`PATH`, the shim targets the recorded mbx; a real `mbx.exe` later on\n`PATH` still wins. With only the new check removed, it fails because the\nshim returns the `mise/shims\\mbx.exe` path.\n-\n`cli::setup_tests::cargo_shim_skips_the_mise_mbx_shim_however_path_spells_it`\n(Windows): the same with `PATH` naming `Mise\\Shims`. It failed before\nthe names compared without regard to case.\n-\n`cli::shim::tests::mise_shims_are_recognized_in_configured_and_default_directories`:\na shim in the `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` directory, under `MISE_DATA_DIR`, or\nunder a `mise` directory counts as a mise shim; `other/shims` does not.\nWith the `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` check removed, it fails.\n- `e2e-win/CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1`, `skips the mise mbx shim when the\ncargo.exe shim forwards`: installs the shim with `mbx setup`, puts a\nstand-in `mbx.exe` that fails under `<test root>\\mise\\shims` ahead of\nthe real mbx, and expects `cargo.exe --version` to print Cargo's\nversion. The full Windows Pester suite reports 9 passed and 1 failed\n(this case) on `main`, and 10 passed with this change.\n- Windows 11, Rust 1.99.1: `mbx fmt --all --check` and `mbx clippy\n--workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` exit 0, and\n`mbx test -p mbx --lib` passes (855 passed). Linux in a Debian\ncontainer, Rust 1.94.1: `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy` with the same flags,\nand `cargo test -p mbx --lib` (941 passed) all succeed. The reproduction\nand the full Windows Pester suite (10 passed) were rerun with the final\nchange.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* On Windows, Cargo skips mise’s `mbx.exe` shim when resolving the\nactive executable, including when the shim path uses different letter\ncasing. A real executable later on PATH is selected when available, with\nthe recorded executable retained as a fallback.\n* **Documentation**\n* Clarified how Windows Cargo shim resolution handles PATH, recorded\nexecutables, and mise shims.\n* **Tests**\n* Added Windows coverage for resolving executables when mise’s shim is\npresent and verifying Cargo commands continue to work.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"37ed90523f9fa664068453f9a4e80e18c3a3e84d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/37ed90523f9fa664068453f9a4e80e18c3a3e84d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1A+qCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADbIQADNfYAX1m5/+aegAOCt0Beua\nAMZaO+oHxXMF8OfmLDOSqLbCajJf8xVgMx297T6vGBX/ZKvblSTUGJ1nSQUJSex7\nTDz3Z0F42EaH3JA1DWMUFwMilf+8i8Av66FF/9CnK5Ea+SyGrWNPpGqC18KejZQp\nOt+py1JzY9D6i/2kSyLpmYaCAIR7fXenfQ9ZFIEqM4q0iEmumX0NaEJGkfr2Yb4y\nLYH88TLp8VC84FXgJBgG1glKpHfRKvEzyVRnTEOxAslBYojZFwSbxvCNzF9olJqf\nrkfzDw3g6JB3kwzhjnxo3uIjXhs3R10UvORs9zn1Jy8byoShFWdGWkQQmSxuYpJC\na7IO5GgTaJWXBXH9z3QQQw+mvRzhcZgomtJph6xgOfaRGeRz6gDGiKzKRW7j8NuF\ncSV3zjYf0+EARbTwkeayKzXw67tAWYb7/p+qryYpwMk+QNfuRBwmxUHUabh31IIy\nNOXbYNYPGDkqJG658NfH8BL6Lmks03p09X7u7ajSMoTqat7nyPCMb+1G5Ovq1YPa\nsfhJ9l5hGbB0Cb2ww2p/4dYrj7BOwACmQwY1xGd1Zv9iP1truM3pXxjltzvQrNEx\nePJWivDzlMVhlHy1uhErzg0hAwOAMb+Swhv7vkltYHDA+9MtomePmKoxGiK0Cmn5\nllnUNUMlxzW4tpJTNP9w\n=vDsE\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 37ed90523f9fa664068453f9a4e80e18c3a3e84d\nparent 7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792282538 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792282538 -0500\n\nfix(setup): skip mise's mbx shim when the Windows cargo.exe shim forwards (#736)\n\nOn Windows, plain `cargo` commands through the standalone shim that `mbx\nsetup` installs at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\cargo.exe` fail in a shell\nwith mise activated, in any directory whose mise config does not enable\nmr-boxington:\n\n```\nmise ERROR No version is set for shim: mbx\n```\n\n`cargo.exe` forwards to the first `mbx.exe` on `PATH` before it reads\nthe mbx recorded in `mbx-target`. With mise activated,\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mise\\shims` is on `PATH`, and mise's `mbx.exe` shim\nthere fails wherever mise does not select an mbx version. This happens\neven though the recorded mbx exists.\n\nThe shim still resolves the active mbx from `PATH` first, as\n`docs/setup.md` documents, but now skips an `mbx.exe` in mise's shims\ndirectory and falls through to the next `mbx.exe` on `PATH` or the\nrecorded one. mise's shims directory is `shims` under a directory named\n`mise` or under `MISE_DATA_DIR`, or the directory `MISE_SHIMS_DIR`\nnames; on Windows the names compare without regard to case. A\n`shims_dir` set only in mise's global config is not visible to mbx. mbx\nalready used the same check, minus `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` and the case\nhandling, to exclude mise's `cargo.exe` shim while resolving the real\nCargo, and that exclusion now gains both. A real `mbx.exe` elsewhere on\n`PATH`, such as a mise install directory added by `mise activate`, still\nwins over the recorded mbx. Unix is not affected: its launcher runs the\nrecorded mbx first and falls back to `command -v mbx` only when that\nfile is gone.\n\nOne tradeoff: with shims-only mise activation, a directory that does\nenable mbx through mise now runs the recorded mbx instead of mise's\nselected version. Rerunning `mbx setup` records the current one.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nWindows 11, PowerShell 7, mise 2026.10.11, Rust 1.99.1:\n\n1. Copy `mbx.exe` to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\cargo.exe`.\n2. Write that `mbx.exe`'s absolute path to\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin\\mbx-target` as UTF-16LE without a BOM, the\nformat `mbx setup` writes.\n3. In an empty directory where mr-boxington is not enabled, with\n`RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1` and `PATH =\n<mbx\\bin>;<mise\\shims>;<mise>;<~\\.cargo\\bin>;System32`, run `cargo.exe\n--version`.\n\nBuilt from `main` (7d97796) and from this branch, with the file\n`mbx-target` names present in both runs:\n\n| Build | With `mise\\shims` on `PATH` | Without it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | `mise ERROR No version is set for shim: mbx`, exit 1 | `cargo\n1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 |\n| this branch | `cargo 1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 | `cargo\n1.99.1 (5f94df478 2026-08-27)`, exit 0 |\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cli::setup_tests::cargo_shim_skips_the_mise_mbx_shim_on_path`\n(Windows): with a recorded mbx and an `mbx.exe` under `mise\\shims` on\n`PATH`, the shim targets the recorded mbx; a real `mbx.exe` later on\n`PATH` still wins. With only the new check removed, it fails because the\nshim returns the `mise/shims\\mbx.exe` path.\n-\n`cli::setup_tests::cargo_shim_skips_the_mise_mbx_shim_however_path_spells_it`\n(Windows): the same with `PATH` naming `Mise\\Shims`. It failed before\nthe names compared without regard to case.\n-\n`cli::shim::tests::mise_shims_are_recognized_in_configured_and_default_directories`:\na shim in the `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` directory, under `MISE_DATA_DIR`, or\nunder a `mise` directory counts as a mise shim; `other/shims` does not.\nWith the `MISE_SHIMS_DIR` check removed, it fails.\n- `e2e-win/CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1`, `skips the mise mbx shim when the\ncargo.exe shim forwards`: installs the shim with `mbx setup`, puts a\nstand-in `mbx.exe` that fails under `<test root>\\mise\\shims` ahead of\nthe real mbx, and expects `cargo.exe --version` to print Cargo's\nversion. The full Windows Pester suite reports 9 passed and 1 failed\n(this case) on `main`, and 10 passed with this change.\n- Windows 11, Rust 1.99.1: `mbx fmt --all --check` and `mbx clippy\n--workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` exit 0, and\n`mbx test -p mbx --lib` passes (855 passed). Linux in a Debian\ncontainer, Rust 1.94.1: `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy` with the same flags,\nand `cargo test -p mbx --lib` (941 passed) all succeed. The reproduction\nand the full Windows Pester suite (10 passed) were rerun with the final\nchange.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* On Windows, Cargo skips mise’s `mbx.exe` shim when resolving the\nactive executable, including when the shim path uses different letter\ncasing. A real executable later on PATH is selected when available, with\nthe recorded executable retained as a fallback.\n* **Documentation**\n* Clarified how Windows Cargo shim resolution handles PATH, recorded\nexecutables, and mise shims.\n* **Tests**\n* Added Windows coverage for resolving executables when mise’s shim is\npresent and verifying Cargo commands continue to work.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T00:15:39Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/7d97796a5e1e97019eb9182222fa6c1549f312ac"}]},{"sha":"1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDFiNjA0Y2I5NTU4NjIwNDI3YmI0ZjNlOTczYTQ2N2NkMTAxY2NlYjE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-18T12:13:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T12:13:59Z"},"message":"test(setup): keep the Windows end-to-end tests out of the developer's profile (#737)\n\nRunning the Windows end-to-end tests (`pwsh e2e-win/run.ps1`) on a\ndeveloper machine changed that machine's real profile. The `mbx setup`\ncases installed `cargo.exe` into `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin` and left it\nthere, and rewrote the user's\n`%APPDATA%\\rust-analyzer\\rust-analyzer.toml`. The Bats suite already\navoids this on Linux and macOS by pointing `HOME` and the `XDG_*`\nvariables at a temporary directory. That does not work on Windows:\n`dirs` finds `LocalAppData` and `AppData` through\n`SHGetKnownFolderPath`, which ignores the environment.\n\nDebug builds already take the shim directory from `MBX_TEST_SHIM_DIR`.\nThis adds `MBX_TEST_RUST_ANALYZER_CONFIG`, also honored only in debug\nbuilds, for the rust-analyzer user config path.\n`CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1` now sets both to paths under each test's root,\nso the tests no longer back up and restore the real `mbx-target`. One\ncase also checks that the rust-analyzer config lands in the temporary\nlocation. Release builds and user-facing behavior are unchanged.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11 by running the full Windows suite with `main`'s\nbuild and test file, then with this change, comparing the real profile\nbefore and after each run:\n\n| | Pester | `rust-analyzer.toml` afterwards | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin`\nafterwards |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` (00d6dcd) | 10 passed | rewritten: `\"--target-dir\",\n\"target/rust-analyzer\"` added to the existing `check.overrideCommand` |\ncontains `cargo.exe` |\n| this change | 10 passed | unchanged (same SHA-256) | not created |\n\nWith `main`'s build and the new test file, the new rust-analyzer config\ncheck fails, since that build ignores the variable.\n\nOther validation: `mbx fmt --all --check`, `mbx clippy --workspace\n--all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`, and `mbx test -p mbx\n--lib` pass on Windows with Rust 1.99.1 (855 passed); `cargo fmt --all\n--check`, `cargo clippy` with the same flags, and `cargo test -p mbx\n--lib` pass on Linux with Rust 1.94.1 (941 passed).\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Setup tests now direct generated files to a temporary test location,\navoiding changes to local user files.\n  * Added a check that setup creates the Rust analyzer configuration.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5f900ac6ffff118041319b715d83b03093de1bdd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/5f900ac6ffff118041319b715d83b03093de1bdd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1LgHCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/6oQAF5OGw8/9bMbdeYyOBBc6NFl\n5bJEId4L0UZ8m+nMuyVEYPl6cdJ2j9ebYgH5Oy5v+btfb1/y7yfc/sWOSTEHeUUE\nVHcmF5aQk2zAGNwAgZZE7Bm0b1ciIkQS6nRsZBeQIbGHkY5f13rHS0v+Q02+oKj1\nkDJsvy4pgusPySvdkBXeekoc3FK0KGIPbyHPU7L9lbV2O6eGGxCOM5s6ZOXef9cP\n3kIOsfITdRaFHaM1LhO6ZwSmlxGTRJ0nPCqwbKuwFspmJQ5NgaJuWKA0/5XxPvn9\n91CIhkChFwPuTWMQXjDgY4rgm1F060zZ+zdwdecZhCvRL6PUhNenLsjo7y76jZJ8\nas3urX2vH6NA+wa75DZ7V7V4xtse2S70P2he9HUAtU/G090phIGKjbHX0ptXlDHr\nqzyhy/PrIiP11HVkn5MJA21DCYi9cPb19POJLYUunVX0bstvyKVwZjZiMaD7w0HV\nyyYHZ2B4EJQDqMc80xXJQTrVhDeFOMRgsl7ns+b1F4c81LHybcARpQ+cPghClNwO\n1SeCjxipIcJ3L0BsffE/NiLVUlVAfqOBrH1MMvI71B5Lzuvy9tz4lEkecoi27TV+\nwDS8dHr4ueGnLwsNTSz8nTExVi21V6ic/bQpClg1ZMcUnNfMxUGSAjJkb6Ei4j45\nEuyCL1hQvoVbdEfJaF6l\n=OOKu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 5f900ac6ffff118041319b715d83b03093de1bdd\nparent 00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792325639 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792325639 -0500\n\ntest(setup): keep the Windows end-to-end tests out of the developer's profile (#737)\n\nRunning the Windows end-to-end tests (`pwsh e2e-win/run.ps1`) on a\ndeveloper machine changed that machine's real profile. The `mbx setup`\ncases installed `cargo.exe` into `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin` and left it\nthere, and rewrote the user's\n`%APPDATA%\\rust-analyzer\\rust-analyzer.toml`. The Bats suite already\navoids this on Linux and macOS by pointing `HOME` and the `XDG_*`\nvariables at a temporary directory. That does not work on Windows:\n`dirs` finds `LocalAppData` and `AppData` through\n`SHGetKnownFolderPath`, which ignores the environment.\n\nDebug builds already take the shim directory from `MBX_TEST_SHIM_DIR`.\nThis adds `MBX_TEST_RUST_ANALYZER_CONFIG`, also honored only in debug\nbuilds, for the rust-analyzer user config path.\n`CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1` now sets both to paths under each test's root,\nso the tests no longer back up and restore the real `mbx-target`. One\ncase also checks that the rust-analyzer config lands in the temporary\nlocation. Release builds and user-facing behavior are unchanged.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11 by running the full Windows suite with `main`'s\nbuild and test file, then with this change, comparing the real profile\nbefore and after each run:\n\n| | Pester | `rust-analyzer.toml` afterwards | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\mbx\\bin`\nafterwards |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` (00d6dcd) | 10 passed | rewritten: `\"--target-dir\",\n\"target/rust-analyzer\"` added to the existing `check.overrideCommand` |\ncontains `cargo.exe` |\n| this change | 10 passed | unchanged (same SHA-256) | not created |\n\nWith `main`'s build and the new test file, the new rust-analyzer config\ncheck fails, since that build ignores the variable.\n\nOther validation: `mbx fmt --all --check`, `mbx clippy --workspace\n--all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`, and `mbx test -p mbx\n--lib` pass on Windows with Rust 1.99.1 (855 passed); `cargo fmt --all\n--check`, `cargo clippy` with the same flags, and `cargo test -p mbx\n--lib` pass on Linux with Rust 1.94.1 (941 passed).\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Setup tests now direct generated files to a temporary test location,\navoiding changes to local user files.\n  * Added a check that setup creates the Rust analyzer configuration.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T12:14:00Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/00d6dcdd805e4143d3a78ad33ec92c1b1a603cd2"}]},{"sha":"3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDNlZTkyZDMyZjA1ZjZlNTU0YmMzOGI0MDBhODA2MzU0YjcyYjk5ODI","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-18T12:26:25Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T12:26:25Z"},"message":"fix(cc): enumerate include directories on every manifest request (#738)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/183\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nC/C++ builds could restore an object compiled against a different header\nsearch result than the current source tree.\n\nmbx remembered the list of includable header names under each `-I`\ndirectory for the life of the process and treated it as valid while the\ndirectory's `(dev, ino, mode, mtime, ctime)` was unchanged. On\nfilesystems whose timestamps advance in coarse ticks (observed on\nWSL2/ext4 with `CONFIG_HZ=250`), a header could appear or vanish during\na compile without changing that metadata. The snapshot taken before the\ncompiler and the one taken before publishing then read the same stale\nanswer, so the \"search path modified during compilation\" guard saw no\nchange. The object was published under a key describing a different\nheader resolution, and a later build with the restored tree computed the\nsame key and restored it without running the compiler.\n\nEvery manifest request now walks the directory afresh, so the two\nsnapshots are independent observations and the existing guard can detect\nthe race. The Unix-only metadata memo is removed. Repeated manifest\nrequests in one process re-enumerate; stable reuse of correct cache hits\nis unaffected.\n\nTests: the memo-reuse test now asserts that an unchanged directory is\nstill enumerated on each request (while still charging the entry\nbudget), and a new test checks that a header added or removed between\nrequests changes the manifest. `cargo test -p mbx-cache-cc`, clippy, and\nfmt pass. I did not reproduce the original race, which needs a\ncoarse-timestamp filesystem.\n\nReported by @lhzapata.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **High Risk**\n> Changes how include-directory manifests feed the compilation cache\nkey; incorrect behavior could restore objects built against a different\nheader resolution, though the change is a correctness fix.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes incorrect C/C++ cache hits when include search paths change\nwithout updating directory metadata.\n> \n> **Removes** the Unix-only `manifest_memo` cache that reused\ninclude-directory name digests while `(dev, ino, mode, mtime, ctime)`\nlooked unchanged. **`include_manifest` now re-enumerates on every\ncall**, with docs explaining that coarse directory timestamps cannot\nprove headers did not appear or vanish between the pre-compile and\npre-publish snapshots.\n> \n> Tests no longer expect memo hits: repeated manifest requests still\nwalk the tree and charge the entry budget, and adding/removing headers\nbetween requests changes the digest. Some memo-specific tests were\nrenamed or replaced with simpler mutation coverage.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n609a97c99567578ed3ed160784143f785c080fad. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Directory manifests now reflect the current set of includable files on\neach request, including when directory timestamps have limited\nprecision.\n* Entry limits are applied consistently each time a manifest is\ngenerated.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"05e1f1697a47654ab09bd76f41ee01d7461638e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/05e1f1697a47654ab09bd76f41ee01d7461638e2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1LrxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARl4QADN0vvk0+4VXNCrMcZy9gsTa\nOpdA4ymQabphQwLfB+I/ehBNGESN5f5ky36e7J/DF7B97tNu54wpe93XJNoirsiN\nrzFQxab2qzpxdQrfBrzaugPQVmPrxMPlbXYh5YhEHMbidjHEyiVd2kefMqVTppmS\n0LkqxlQ2SSwWGo6cYnwPrZRvRp4l0KBUqsb0a1MZi3kQHhAZbqFerv3GGAY5CSxB\nh4xscr3cF6Un2JVZB+WQnbV5FJkW3jLnBOjAjbD8ntb6PZxWYOMWVNmgazF9yOXi\nEUEzT0LH5uk8ffhsLOrN16Eg0J4HUcdkY86hjOOC0rQxhHMrQWc0k3EO6lZKivnJ\nCctzMDt7dWGO7fn6ZyLiNNAwqZH2txObbAlo0qhS9zpUGhA3NPzHVGi09tg2ps2+\nWVI/0KsW6AmMtzRXA8hb0k5p06k4cqZhBrquecPSUqcO+I5OuKjBMhQ4oOQ8g3Od\no6OfCOrPDQi2hpxLLYOBQPKMhTal1lPJj8ZL8H3ivgILDZIeOOnCT3qxpy/4RQ7Q\nZhF41JPY0jb4joeOtWG7Y+PM+cqUOkuAZWCyVdVDe4xKNm+GYXPs2OxGz5SM4+6l\n05bp1/q0+DQeHVbfu18ws5kmbQetrA1Gf8ta2yE43oHd59cVVcRvLUJZiVzEH+ny\nsT74VmNBjwgtkznzH02g\n=9+T+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 05e1f1697a47654ab09bd76f41ee01d7461638e2\nparent 1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792326385 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792326385 -0500\n\nfix(cc): enumerate include directories on every manifest request (#738)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/183\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nC/C++ builds could restore an object compiled against a different header\nsearch result than the current source tree.\n\nmbx remembered the list of includable header names under each `-I`\ndirectory for the life of the process and treated it as valid while the\ndirectory's `(dev, ino, mode, mtime, ctime)` was unchanged. On\nfilesystems whose timestamps advance in coarse ticks (observed on\nWSL2/ext4 with `CONFIG_HZ=250`), a header could appear or vanish during\na compile without changing that metadata. The snapshot taken before the\ncompiler and the one taken before publishing then read the same stale\nanswer, so the \"search path modified during compilation\" guard saw no\nchange. The object was published under a key describing a different\nheader resolution, and a later build with the restored tree computed the\nsame key and restored it without running the compiler.\n\nEvery manifest request now walks the directory afresh, so the two\nsnapshots are independent observations and the existing guard can detect\nthe race. The Unix-only metadata memo is removed. Repeated manifest\nrequests in one process re-enumerate; stable reuse of correct cache hits\nis unaffected.\n\nTests: the memo-reuse test now asserts that an unchanged directory is\nstill enumerated on each request (while still charging the entry\nbudget), and a new test checks that a header added or removed between\nrequests changes the manifest. `cargo test -p mbx-cache-cc`, clippy, and\nfmt pass. I did not reproduce the original race, which needs a\ncoarse-timestamp filesystem.\n\nReported by @lhzapata.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **High Risk**\n> Changes how include-directory manifests feed the compilation cache\nkey; incorrect behavior could restore objects built against a different\nheader resolution, though the change is a correctness fix.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes incorrect C/C++ cache hits when include search paths change\nwithout updating directory metadata.\n> \n> **Removes** the Unix-only `manifest_memo` cache that reused\ninclude-directory name digests while `(dev, ino, mode, mtime, ctime)`\nlooked unchanged. **`include_manifest` now re-enumerates on every\ncall**, with docs explaining that coarse directory timestamps cannot\nprove headers did not appear or vanish between the pre-compile and\npre-publish snapshots.\n> \n> Tests no longer expect memo hits: repeated manifest requests still\nwalk the tree and charge the entry budget, and adding/removing headers\nbetween requests changes the digest. Some memo-specific tests were\nrenamed or replaced with simpler mutation coverage.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\n609a97c99567578ed3ed160784143f785c080fad. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Directory manifests now reflect the current set of includable files on\neach request, including when directory timestamps have limited\nprecision.\n* Entry limits are applied consistently each time a manifest is\ngenerated.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T12:26:25Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/1b604cb9558620427bb4f3e973a467cd101cceb1"}]},{"sha":"0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDA2MDVmMzZjODg4MjZkOTdhOTA2M2YyYWVkM2JhNDE2Y2ExZWIxZDY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-18T13:28:04Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T13:28:04Z"},"message":"fix(mise): run the Windows tasks under mise's default cmd shell (#739)\n\nOn Windows, `mise run lint`, `mise run test`, and every other task that\nbuilds the bootstrap binary failed with mise's default shell. mise runs\na task's `run_windows` through `cmd /c` unless the user sets the\nglobal-only `windows_default_inline_shell_args`. These tasks, however,\nwere written for PowerShell (`New-Item`, `Copy-Item`, `Write-Output`,\n`[IO.File]::WriteAllText`) and started the binary as\n`./target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx.exe`, which cmd cannot run. On a\nJapanese-locale machine:\n\n```\n[bootstrap] $ New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force target/mbx-bootstrap | Out-Null\n'New-Item' は、内部コマンドまたは外部コマンド、\n操作可能なプログラムまたはバッチ ファイルとして認識されていません。\n[bootstrap] ERROR task failed\n```\n\n(cmd's \"is not recognized as an internal or external command\".) CI did\nnot notice because the Windows job calls cargo, `mbx.exe`, and `pwsh\ne2e-win/run.ps1` directly instead of through `mise run`.\n\nThe `run_windows` commands now work under either shell:\n\n- Commands that only start the bootstrap binary name it as\n`target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe`, which both cmd and PowerShell run.\n- Commands that use PowerShell are wrapped in `pwsh -NoProfile -Command\n\"...\"`, using the `pwsh` that `test:e2e` already required.\n\nContributors who already set `windows_default_inline_shell_args` to\nPowerShell keep working.\n\nTwo more fixes were needed for `mise run test:e2e` to run the suite:\n\n- With mise activated, `MISE_SHELL` reached the `mbx setup --status`\ncase, which then checked the developer's mise config: `Expected 0,\nbecause mbx setup is installed but is not active in the selected mise\nconfig, but got 1.` The value can come from a PowerShell profile that\n`pwsh` loads or from the shell that started `mise run`. `test:e2e` now\nstarts `pwsh -NoProfile`, and `CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1` clears\n`MISE_SHELL` and `MISE_CONFIG_FILE` for each test and restores them\nafterwards, as the Bats suite already does. CI runners have neither.\n- `e2e-win/run.ps1` now imports Pester 5 or later (`Import-Module Pester\n-MinimumVersion 5.0 -ErrorAction Stop`) before anything else. The script\nused to load Pester only after `Enter-VsDevShell`. On a profile path\nwith non-ASCII characters (here `OneDrive\\ドキュメント`), `Enter-VsDevShell`\ncan return `PSModulePath` with that entry mis-decoded, depending on the\nconsole code page. Only the Pester 3.4 that ships with Windows is then\nfound, `New-PesterConfiguration` is missing, and the script exited 0\nwithout running a test. Importing the module up front avoids that and\nfails loudly when Pester 5 is not installed. A `#Requires -Modules` line\nwould also load it, but then the script exits 0 even when a test fails,\nso it is not used.\n\nValidation on Windows 11 with mise 2026.10.11, Rust 1.99.1, and Pester\n6.1.0, running each task with mise's default `cmd /c` and again with\n`MISE_WINDOWS_DEFAULT_INLINE_SHELL_ARGS=\"pwsh -NoProfile -Command\"`:\n\n| Task | `cmd /c` before | `cmd /c` after | `pwsh` after |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `bootstrap` | fails (above) | exit 0 | exit 0 |\n| `build`, `format`, `lint`, `test:pretty`, `render:docs`, `check:docs`\n| fail in `bootstrap` | exit 0 | exit 0 |\n| `test:e2e` | fails in `bootstrap` | 10 passed | 10 passed |\n\nAfter rebasing onto #737, `mise run test:e2e` with the default `cmd /c`\nreports 10 passed both with `MISE_SHELL=pwsh` in the calling environment\nand without it; before clearing it, `MISE_SHELL=pwsh` failed the setup\ncase. With one test failing, `pwsh e2e-win/run.ps1` and `pwsh -NoProfile\ne2e-win/run.ps1` exit 1, as `main`'s script does. `mise run test` with\nthe default `cmd /c`, run before that rebase, exits 0: every `cargo\ntest` binary passes, and Pester reports 10 passed (`testResults.xml`:\ntotal 10, failures 0). From a shell whose console mis-decodes the\nprofile path, `pwsh -NoProfile -File e2e-win/run.ps1` used to stop at\n`Invoke-Pester -Configuration $config` because Pester 3.4 has no\n`-Configuration` parameter, and still exited 0. It now runs under Pester\n6.1.0, the only Pester 5 or later installed there.\n\nTwo things these runs exposed are left for separate changes:\n\n- `mbx` starts `gc --automatic` from `target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe`\nafter a build. While it runs, Windows refuses to overwrite that file, so\nthe next task's `bootstrap` fails to copy the binary. The runs above set\n`MBX_GC_AUTO=0` to avoid this.\n- `render:docs` writes `docs/cli` with CRLF line endings on Windows.\n`check:docs` still passes because Git normalizes them with\n`core.autocrlf`.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Chores**\n* Updated Windows task commands for more consistent PowerShell\nexecution, including documentation and bootstrap tasks.\n* **Tests**\n* Windows end-to-end tests now require Pester 5 or later and run through\nPowerShell without loading user profiles.\n* Test setup checks run without relying on your existing mise\nconfiguration, which is restored after testing.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6477d987a02cce21e0d6d8e9382e2d6bc137b67b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/6477d987a02cce21e0d6d8e9382e2d6bc137b67b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1MlkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmNYQADvDTGDvS6++IGVRSzCWKdYT\nTGpKN4lkjhDQhkLVGT9g0YaOWkHskfRO7eUOLiWdHoxV5p6aXp9PkOTOBCsvRHCD\nea1sqfuiAIVgl85tRXk+vc3bB3YBUfBeDZ9jn8gZFOSMprGEVTyUq6PILhdpdhJS\nA2KsF5D8MK6GadIwsnCtpAgOojarWT9pSB1MSRa+BYBQ/OrIdoH76vncIFnx9F2K\nBTN3Wa1pA+aIvUI9I1Y9LuC8r3UUIW7K7hTc9BKidU31Y3ZrnlNeyhA/7yV3FyGI\n3NfgwdGSGnSuLlUwq2qfsYoB0lthkX4iUFf0Dn5NRo2Bh2NLTwQ2oJaoiRVLAJOF\ncANJqKUumcUdDsrnPKJmb+qe8IW5UBuC7Mv05NGPiJf+SGUn5Kx4XZsbGoaee5aq\ncg/HkB6J6C179y+hTKj9PdgS3YsLDpOmc+EoFvrEbPiVRE9TOk+gDYk9GsHvo/+L\naCkr1Uom2vBnAbUHBxCM6pKVxsexYh8g70tJkOQixnNhmEYHKoEhaVf+bHXzmPrM\nUYW15SOwhv6UwLReM/MeyldpHcBd+/zZupumPtvzqUXl6qadkP0t5+8wg90iZxQy\nFz/ZwGE19ppFkxcKYgOz1P/Hdz/rb/9dSig+5cSwa4/QyxcApkvk+RgDWpvXOiC9\ne0tUl8ibV21s5WJb+PTm\n=EUup\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 6477d987a02cce21e0d6d8e9382e2d6bc137b67b\nparent 3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792330084 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792330084 -0500\n\nfix(mise): run the Windows tasks under mise's default cmd shell (#739)\n\nOn Windows, `mise run lint`, `mise run test`, and every other task that\nbuilds the bootstrap binary failed with mise's default shell. mise runs\na task's `run_windows` through `cmd /c` unless the user sets the\nglobal-only `windows_default_inline_shell_args`. These tasks, however,\nwere written for PowerShell (`New-Item`, `Copy-Item`, `Write-Output`,\n`[IO.File]::WriteAllText`) and started the binary as\n`./target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx.exe`, which cmd cannot run. On a\nJapanese-locale machine:\n\n```\n[bootstrap] $ New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force target/mbx-bootstrap | Out-Null\n'New-Item' は、内部コマンドまたは外部コマンド、\n操作可能なプログラムまたはバッチ ファイルとして認識されていません。\n[bootstrap] ERROR task failed\n```\n\n(cmd's \"is not recognized as an internal or external command\".) CI did\nnot notice because the Windows job calls cargo, `mbx.exe`, and `pwsh\ne2e-win/run.ps1` directly instead of through `mise run`.\n\nThe `run_windows` commands now work under either shell:\n\n- Commands that only start the bootstrap binary name it as\n`target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe`, which both cmd and PowerShell run.\n- Commands that use PowerShell are wrapped in `pwsh -NoProfile -Command\n\"...\"`, using the `pwsh` that `test:e2e` already required.\n\nContributors who already set `windows_default_inline_shell_args` to\nPowerShell keep working.\n\nTwo more fixes were needed for `mise run test:e2e` to run the suite:\n\n- With mise activated, `MISE_SHELL` reached the `mbx setup --status`\ncase, which then checked the developer's mise config: `Expected 0,\nbecause mbx setup is installed but is not active in the selected mise\nconfig, but got 1.` The value can come from a PowerShell profile that\n`pwsh` loads or from the shell that started `mise run`. `test:e2e` now\nstarts `pwsh -NoProfile`, and `CargoDispatch.Tests.ps1` clears\n`MISE_SHELL` and `MISE_CONFIG_FILE` for each test and restores them\nafterwards, as the Bats suite already does. CI runners have neither.\n- `e2e-win/run.ps1` now imports Pester 5 or later (`Import-Module Pester\n-MinimumVersion 5.0 -ErrorAction Stop`) before anything else. The script\nused to load Pester only after `Enter-VsDevShell`. On a profile path\nwith non-ASCII characters (here `OneDrive\\ドキュメント`), `Enter-VsDevShell`\ncan return `PSModulePath` with that entry mis-decoded, depending on the\nconsole code page. Only the Pester 3.4 that ships with Windows is then\nfound, `New-PesterConfiguration` is missing, and the script exited 0\nwithout running a test. Importing the module up front avoids that and\nfails loudly when Pester 5 is not installed. A `#Requires -Modules` line\nwould also load it, but then the script exits 0 even when a test fails,\nso it is not used.\n\nValidation on Windows 11 with mise 2026.10.11, Rust 1.99.1, and Pester\n6.1.0, running each task with mise's default `cmd /c` and again with\n`MISE_WINDOWS_DEFAULT_INLINE_SHELL_ARGS=\"pwsh -NoProfile -Command\"`:\n\n| Task | `cmd /c` before | `cmd /c` after | `pwsh` after |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `bootstrap` | fails (above) | exit 0 | exit 0 |\n| `build`, `format`, `lint`, `test:pretty`, `render:docs`, `check:docs`\n| fail in `bootstrap` | exit 0 | exit 0 |\n| `test:e2e` | fails in `bootstrap` | 10 passed | 10 passed |\n\nAfter rebasing onto #737, `mise run test:e2e` with the default `cmd /c`\nreports 10 passed both with `MISE_SHELL=pwsh` in the calling environment\nand without it; before clearing it, `MISE_SHELL=pwsh` failed the setup\ncase. With one test failing, `pwsh e2e-win/run.ps1` and `pwsh -NoProfile\ne2e-win/run.ps1` exit 1, as `main`'s script does. `mise run test` with\nthe default `cmd /c`, run before that rebase, exits 0: every `cargo\ntest` binary passes, and Pester reports 10 passed (`testResults.xml`:\ntotal 10, failures 0). From a shell whose console mis-decodes the\nprofile path, `pwsh -NoProfile -File e2e-win/run.ps1` used to stop at\n`Invoke-Pester -Configuration $config` because Pester 3.4 has no\n`-Configuration` parameter, and still exited 0. It now runs under Pester\n6.1.0, the only Pester 5 or later installed there.\n\nTwo things these runs exposed are left for separate changes:\n\n- `mbx` starts `gc --automatic` from `target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe`\nafter a build. While it runs, Windows refuses to overwrite that file, so\nthe next task's `bootstrap` fails to copy the binary. The runs above set\n`MBX_GC_AUTO=0` to avoid this.\n- `render:docs` writes `docs/cli` with CRLF line endings on Windows.\n`check:docs` still passes because Git normalizes them with\n`core.autocrlf`.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Chores**\n* Updated Windows task commands for more consistent PowerShell\nexecution, including documentation and bootstrap tasks.\n* **Tests**\n* Windows end-to-end tests now require Pester 5 or later and run through\nPowerShell without loading user profiles.\n* Test setup checks run without relying on your existing mise\nconfiguration, which is restored after testing.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T13:28:05Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/3ee92d32f05f6e554bc38b400a806354b72b9982"}]},{"sha":"2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDJjZGU1NThmMjI4MDI5NzljYjBlYzY0ZjQyYzhlNWViZTUyNDNkYmE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jam Balaya","email":"jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp","date":"2026-10-18T16:32:34Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:32:34Z"},"message":"fix(mise): replace the bootstrap binary while a previous one still runs (#740)\n\nAfter a build, mbx starts `gc --automatic` from the binary that ran it.\nWhen that binary is the task bootstrap's `target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx`, the\ncollection can keep running for minutes on a large local cache.\nMeanwhile, the next `mise run` task, such as `lint` after `build`, fails\nin `bootstrap` because it cannot write over the running binary:\n\n```\nCopy-Item: The process cannot access the file 'C:\\...\\target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe' because it is being used by another process.\n```\n\nOn Linux, `cp` fails the same way with `cp: cannot create regular file\n'target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx': Text file busy`, and the old binary stays in\nplace.\n\n`bootstrap` now never writes into the installed binary. It copies the\nnew one to a staging file that belongs to the current run, then renames\nit into place:\n\n- Unix stages into `mktemp target/mbx-bootstrap/.mbx.XXXXXX` with `cp\n-p`, which keeps the executable bit, and `mv -f`s it over `mbx`. The\nrename replaces the directory entry, and a running process keeps its own\ncopy. If the copy or rename fails, the staging file is removed and `mbx`\nis untouched.\n- Windows does the same in the new `scripts/install-bootstrap.ps1`,\nwhich mise runs with `pwsh -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File` so\nthat a `Restricted` execution policy does not block it. A script avoids\nquoting PowerShell variables through both cmd and PowerShell. It stages\n`mbx-<guid>.new` and replaces `mbx.exe` with `[IO.File]::Move(...,\noverwrite: true)`, an atomic replace, rather than `Move-Item -Force`,\nwhich deletes the destination first. While `mbx.exe` runs, Windows\nrefuses that with `UnauthorizedAccessException`. The script then calls\n`[IO.File]::Replace` with a `mbx-<guid>.old` backup, which moves the\nrunning file aside and puts the new one in its place. It retries up to\nfive times when another run interferes. If `ReplaceFile` fails after\nmoving the old file aside, the script moves it back. Other errors fail\nat once. After any failure, including a copy that stopped partway, the\nrun removes its own staging file. Each run first removes `*.old` files\nthat nothing runs any more.\n\nOverlapping `mise run` invocations in one checkout no longer collide\neither, since each uses its own staging file.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11 with mise 2026.10.11. A running bootstrap binary\nwas simulated with `target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe exec ping -n 60\n127.0.0.1`, and `MBX_GC_AUTO=0` kept a real collection out of the runs:\n\n| `mise run bootstrap` | `main` | this change |\n|---|---|---|\n| nothing running | exit 0 | exit 0, no leftover file |\n| old binary running, `cmd /c` | exit 1 (error above) | exit 0, one\n`.old` file |\n| old binary running, `pwsh` default shell | exit 1 | exit 0 |\n| after the old binary exits | exit 0 | exit 0, `.old` file removed |\n\nThe install script on its own:\n\n- 60 rounds of two overlapping runs: 0 of 120 failed with nothing\nrunning, and 0 of 120 failed while the old binary ran. Earlier revisions\nof the script passed 100 rounds the same way.\n- With the build output removed: it exits 1 and leaves `mbx.exe`\nbyte-identical, both with and without the old binary running.\n- With a failure injected right after the staging copy: it exits 1,\nkeeps `mbx.exe`, and leaves no `.new` file.\n- With `PSExecutionPolicyPreference=Restricted`, plain `pwsh -File`\nrefuses the script, while `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` runs it, and `mise\nrun bootstrap` exits 0 under both default shells.\n- Separately, on a running copy of an executable: `File.Move` with\noverwrite failed with `UnauthorizedAccessException` (`0x80070005`) and\nleft both files in place, while `File.Replace` succeeded and kept the\nrunning file as the backup.\n- Not exercised: a `ReplaceFile` failure after it moves the old file\naside, and five failed attempts in a row. Neither could be induced here.\n\nOn Linux (Debian container), the `run` commands were executed with `sh\n-o errexit -c`, mise's Unix default:\n\n- While `target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx` ran, `main`'s command failed with\n`Text file busy` (exit 1). This change exits 0 and installs the new\nbinary with mode 755.\n- Over 300 rounds of two overlapping runs, a shared `mbx.new` failed 294\nof 600 runs, while this change failed 0 of 600 and left no staging file.\n- With the build output missing, it exits 1, leaves `mbx` in place, and\nremoves its staging file.\n\nmise itself was not run in the container. On Windows, mise ran the same\nUnix command string through Git's `sh -o errexit -c` and passed it\nthrough unchanged.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Bootstrap installation on Unix and Windows stages the executable in a\nuniquely named temporary file before installing it.\n* Windows installation retries replacement up to five times when needed\nand attempts to restore the previous executable if replacement fails.\n* Temporary installation files are cleaned up when copying or\nreplacement fails. On Windows, leftover backup files are also removed on\na best-effort basis.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7a89c0da13d9719d93a7c9ce6b28fc9575b16738","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/7a89c0da13d9719d93a7c9ce6b28fc9575b16738"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1PSiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAJMYQAI3gAOYHY6DF6l4K0Yr/q7Xe\njNTLFjQ/GRg1f+blJEyNtfoHkJ9/r1YosYfIfNM0xXPBtr07IncjDYJcHKLurBCF\nBElTVxcc1EqWmvFj3pLZOudrcz8qUd2a0rqrY8UItATADmJRG2QWXUkPbU/dZVEK\nZsvNwUqp0rJz7sX3JPuIQUXssgqleEN5grj6ztMi3xncP375FXFI7vx+ELH5du8/\n7cqQBIKwF025FIBevW1zPCNmjDm5kx3M+ykxaj4uTmu1MZddAqu2wpfmpnkY/dP6\naE+c+c+ydo6qI/rbULcmNdGYtrXENuw8FIYWXWKOsY+uk5vLfnAnzLCVEbpvCaXK\niuRlRnLmg+WQbfUvtz7UcdLlFR4LWDw/IAnMZBIPcXnSVLSxrQF/iiO9fnZT7Ybj\ndOsCKwBIRIM6zXRFXewYCSEpnIB1MMvmAfjjo/MUk6VSIKlcX/uK0/yUmi2vOsC0\nlRjQszTRdLYmjf6GIcFFCtOUiFtV+6bHRduwl5I67ifGHGOB+Q+WKdUM0QbQ9N1w\nRrwH5UrU/h+tYEichk6WQc0XzcDDEjCpv4ph9NMCupajxeNm66xbPEvvuN/TGxV4\nLRreL47IgNV4fSPxhKBTiBsKpitQpBmJqQZPHMtVkiKnItxSuRUA8a+naydeIcKr\ntQrDgwLdDB16bVdL6lES\n=mInX\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7a89c0da13d9719d93a7c9ce6b28fc9575b16738\nparent 0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6\nauthor Jam Balaya <jambalaya.pyoncafe@outlook.jp> 1792341154 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792341154 -0500\n\nfix(mise): replace the bootstrap binary while a previous one still runs (#740)\n\nAfter a build, mbx starts `gc --automatic` from the binary that ran it.\nWhen that binary is the task bootstrap's `target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx`, the\ncollection can keep running for minutes on a large local cache.\nMeanwhile, the next `mise run` task, such as `lint` after `build`, fails\nin `bootstrap` because it cannot write over the running binary:\n\n```\nCopy-Item: The process cannot access the file 'C:\\...\\target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe' because it is being used by another process.\n```\n\nOn Linux, `cp` fails the same way with `cp: cannot create regular file\n'target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx': Text file busy`, and the old binary stays in\nplace.\n\n`bootstrap` now never writes into the installed binary. It copies the\nnew one to a staging file that belongs to the current run, then renames\nit into place:\n\n- Unix stages into `mktemp target/mbx-bootstrap/.mbx.XXXXXX` with `cp\n-p`, which keeps the executable bit, and `mv -f`s it over `mbx`. The\nrename replaces the directory entry, and a running process keeps its own\ncopy. If the copy or rename fails, the staging file is removed and `mbx`\nis untouched.\n- Windows does the same in the new `scripts/install-bootstrap.ps1`,\nwhich mise runs with `pwsh -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File` so\nthat a `Restricted` execution policy does not block it. A script avoids\nquoting PowerShell variables through both cmd and PowerShell. It stages\n`mbx-<guid>.new` and replaces `mbx.exe` with `[IO.File]::Move(...,\noverwrite: true)`, an atomic replace, rather than `Move-Item -Force`,\nwhich deletes the destination first. While `mbx.exe` runs, Windows\nrefuses that with `UnauthorizedAccessException`. The script then calls\n`[IO.File]::Replace` with a `mbx-<guid>.old` backup, which moves the\nrunning file aside and puts the new one in its place. It retries up to\nfive times when another run interferes. If `ReplaceFile` fails after\nmoving the old file aside, the script moves it back. Other errors fail\nat once. After any failure, including a copy that stopped partway, the\nrun removes its own staging file. Each run first removes `*.old` files\nthat nothing runs any more.\n\nOverlapping `mise run` invocations in one checkout no longer collide\neither, since each uses its own staging file.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11 with mise 2026.10.11. A running bootstrap binary\nwas simulated with `target\\mbx-bootstrap\\mbx.exe exec ping -n 60\n127.0.0.1`, and `MBX_GC_AUTO=0` kept a real collection out of the runs:\n\n| `mise run bootstrap` | `main` | this change |\n|---|---|---|\n| nothing running | exit 0 | exit 0, no leftover file |\n| old binary running, `cmd /c` | exit 1 (error above) | exit 0, one\n`.old` file |\n| old binary running, `pwsh` default shell | exit 1 | exit 0 |\n| after the old binary exits | exit 0 | exit 0, `.old` file removed |\n\nThe install script on its own:\n\n- 60 rounds of two overlapping runs: 0 of 120 failed with nothing\nrunning, and 0 of 120 failed while the old binary ran. Earlier revisions\nof the script passed 100 rounds the same way.\n- With the build output removed: it exits 1 and leaves `mbx.exe`\nbyte-identical, both with and without the old binary running.\n- With a failure injected right after the staging copy: it exits 1,\nkeeps `mbx.exe`, and leaves no `.new` file.\n- With `PSExecutionPolicyPreference=Restricted`, plain `pwsh -File`\nrefuses the script, while `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` runs it, and `mise\nrun bootstrap` exits 0 under both default shells.\n- Separately, on a running copy of an executable: `File.Move` with\noverwrite failed with `UnauthorizedAccessException` (`0x80070005`) and\nleft both files in place, while `File.Replace` succeeded and kept the\nrunning file as the backup.\n- Not exercised: a `ReplaceFile` failure after it moves the old file\naside, and five failed attempts in a row. Neither could be induced here.\n\nOn Linux (Debian container), the `run` commands were executed with `sh\n-o errexit -c`, mise's Unix default:\n\n- While `target/mbx-bootstrap/mbx` ran, `main`'s command failed with\n`Text file busy` (exit 1). This change exits 0 and installs the new\nbinary with mode 755.\n- Over 300 rounds of two overlapping runs, a shared `mbx.new` failed 294\nof 600 runs, while this change failed 0 of 600 and left no staging file.\n- With the build output missing, it exits 1, leaves `mbx` in place, and\nremoves its staging file.\n\nmise itself was not run in the container. On Windows, mise ran the same\nUnix command string through Git's `sh -o errexit -c` and passed it\nthrough unchanged.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Bootstrap installation on Unix and Windows stages the executable in a\nuniquely named temporary file before installing it.\n* Windows installation retries replacement up to five times when needed\nand attempts to restore the previous executable if replacement fails.\n* Temporary installation files are cleaned up when copying or\nreplacement fails. On Windows, leftover backup files are also removed on\na best-effort basis.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T16:32:35Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba/comments","author":{"login":"JamBalaya56562","id":88115388,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg4MTE1Mzg4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88115388?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562","html_url":"https://github.com/JamBalaya56562","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JamBalaya56562/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/0605f36c88826d97a9063f2aed3ba416ca1eb1d6"}]},{"sha":"2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDJlNDY3OTQ5NGQwOWQ0NzU4YjZjMzY2NjI2YzJkNDMxNzAxZTdkODU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:33:13Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:33:13Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): estimate compile memory for each build mode (#742)\n\nA crate's memory estimate for compiles that do not link a program was\none number per crate. That number was the highest peak ever recorded for\nany kind of compile of that crate, and it was never lowered. One heavy\nrelease build therefore set the charge for every later `cargo check`,\nClippy run, and debug library build of the same crate. On a real host,\nthe library crate `brokk_bifrost_analysis` had a recorded peak of 41.3\nGiB. It was charged that for every compile, while the measured peaks\nthere were:\n\n| Compile | Highest measured peak |\n|---|---:|\n| `cargo check` (metadata only) | 6.4 GiB |\n| debug library build | 10.8 GiB |\n| release library build | 15.3 GiB |\n| check or Clippy of tests | 8.4 GiB |\n\nWith an 83 GiB budget, only 2 of these compiles could run at once, and\nqueued checks waited up to 97 minutes.\n\nmbx now also records a peak for each compile mode. The mode is the set\nof emitted outputs, crate types, target, `--test`, optimization level,\nLTO, codegen units, debug info, incremental compilation, and `-Z\nthreads`. After a mode has 3 successful samples, it is charged its\nhighest peak plus 50%, but never more than the crate-wide peak. In the\nexample above, a `cargo check` is charged 9.6 GiB instead of 41.3 GiB,\nso 8 can run at once instead of 2.\n\nSafety rules:\n\n- Until a mode has 3 successful samples, it is charged the crate-wide\npeak, exactly as before. No compile is ever charged more than before.\n- Peaks are never lowered. An OOM kill raises both the mode peak and the\ncrate-wide peak.\n- Some compiles never count as samples, because their measurement may be\ntoo low: compiles that fail or are OOM-killed, and compiles run through\nanother wrapper such as sccache, which may return its own cache hit\nwithout running rustc.\n- The estimate is chosen from the command that actually runs, including\nincremental flags that mbx adds itself.\n- Clippy compiles learn their own estimates, apart from `cargo check`,\nbecause Clippy runs extra lint passes. A compile with more than one\n`--target` matches no single-target mode, so it falls back to the\ncrate-wide peak.\n- Links and test runs keep their existing estimates.\n\nTo see why a compile waits, use the new `mbx stats --scheduler`. It\nlists recorded peaks, sample counts, and charges, and a read-only\nsnapshot of current leases and waiters. Example output from a test\nfixture:\n\n```\nheavy: peak=41.3 GiB, samples=0, charge=41.3 GiB / 42 permits (crate-wide fallback)\nheavy [compile emit=metadata crate-type=rlib opt0 lto-default cgu- debug0 incremental-off]: peak=6.4 GiB, samples=3, charge=9.6 GiB / 10 permits (mode)\n```\n\n`--json` gives the same data in bytes. Its charges use the global\nscheduler settings, since a workspace `.mbx.toml` applies only to that\nworkspace's builds. The lease and waiter rows show the charge each\ncompile was really admitted or queued with. Without `--scheduler`, `mbx\nstats` output is unchanged.\n\nUpgrading: an older mbx sharing the same cache keeps charging the\ncrate-wide peak and may discard the new sample counts when it rewrites\nthe ledger. That only makes affected modes fall back to the crate-wide\npeak until they collect 3 new samples. Lease and waiter records gain\noptional fields, and older records still read correctly in both\ndirections.\n\nLimits: no estimate based on past compiles can prevent every\nout-of-memory kill. A source edit that makes rustc use much more memory\ncan still exceed a mode's charge once. After that compile, the higher\npeak is recorded. Pressure control and the available-memory check still\napply.\n\nValidation: all 964 mbx library tests pass, including the new scheduler,\nrustc, session, and stats tests. `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets\n--all-features -- -D warnings` passes. An independent review traced\nevery compiler launch path for estimates that are too low, and all its\nfindings were fixed. `mise` was not available locally, so the generated\n`docs/cli` pages were produced with the same `usage` generator and\noptions that `render:docs` uses. The full `mise run ci` gate was not run\nlocally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Added per-compile-mode memory estimates for scheduling. After three\nsuccessful samples, estimates use the mode’s measured peak with\nadditional headroom, capped at the crate-wide peak; until then,\nscheduling uses the crate-wide estimate.\n* Added `mbx stats --scheduler` to display memory estimates and a\nread-only snapshot of scheduler activity in text or JSON. Settings are\nread from the nearest `.mbx.toml`.\n* Scheduler estimates distinguish Clippy and compiler settings; wrapped\nor failed compiles do not count toward successful samples.\n* **Documentation**\n* Documented scheduler estimates, sampling behavior, settings\nresolution, and scheduler statistics.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f0c336f8a6ef5f5a13cecced119556751f27756f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/f0c336f8a6ef5f5a13cecced119556751f27756f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1PTJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsQMQABNDwgfsBZi5hxgSCTu2b7ZH\nQNyMMFkSBue70KjEvvogHhmvRDks6gkaZQ0gFsT/JFJb9Ric4Ak5EDXr47CyAcnP\nQ098KNbu/br7+9YZuVlCP/4EQV3UuZo6MxbOcf6DPEydE06TJuIfFO2gq8sZa14S\na0Y1WLkqRGq/drZZJ6qMgJ2Ik4fRYzAPoJSD7Jqwkl2UqR+/WWbMEKZGVYAP1igO\neYYzqbw9t/u9jNjyRqG3OaAcBaU7mbH+hCuO9c6vMyC9tt35RcgSVl2EE0L+ucrB\nPfGkoWkb69YD4zFS5nLBjIPBwvI44I5TLJtNnh7wGWdXNFHjk6nAR/2T3+coQlY+\nifJ42/oA9WKSclmi5gEzPwzBv7oqFwGqlh3qOlUF4ZceJ79ZWKxXkZzCw4MGD9Ui\nmNjDtufCXIMzU4Uyxm0nwO2GnCwk4MDNMzOPFAwj33TIVim5UA066QApvQGrJ+NI\n/K2qIW1mQ5lr245absnFG5owJE+5KGiDfaxW1/mx/zRQnmbRP+loxTLzWeA+UfxJ\nP/nF3d3IqKJ85vXheQltSFIFZajdOTmwmFgnXlyEl3pExb4M9JFb4fBMscH/plyl\nvLtYJf6cXLFOhgsDG6PlsxDlxFy/5ktIRQ2yu5nZHINGA6CjUSrb+8wjywPjergR\nZIRDJgmjVHEsqNTFAh8o\n=Ik5q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree f0c336f8a6ef5f5a13cecced119556751f27756f\nparent 2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792341193 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792341193 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): estimate compile memory for each build mode (#742)\n\nA crate's memory estimate for compiles that do not link a program was\none number per crate. That number was the highest peak ever recorded for\nany kind of compile of that crate, and it was never lowered. One heavy\nrelease build therefore set the charge for every later `cargo check`,\nClippy run, and debug library build of the same crate. On a real host,\nthe library crate `brokk_bifrost_analysis` had a recorded peak of 41.3\nGiB. It was charged that for every compile, while the measured peaks\nthere were:\n\n| Compile | Highest measured peak |\n|---|---:|\n| `cargo check` (metadata only) | 6.4 GiB |\n| debug library build | 10.8 GiB |\n| release library build | 15.3 GiB |\n| check or Clippy of tests | 8.4 GiB |\n\nWith an 83 GiB budget, only 2 of these compiles could run at once, and\nqueued checks waited up to 97 minutes.\n\nmbx now also records a peak for each compile mode. The mode is the set\nof emitted outputs, crate types, target, `--test`, optimization level,\nLTO, codegen units, debug info, incremental compilation, and `-Z\nthreads`. After a mode has 3 successful samples, it is charged its\nhighest peak plus 50%, but never more than the crate-wide peak. In the\nexample above, a `cargo check` is charged 9.6 GiB instead of 41.3 GiB,\nso 8 can run at once instead of 2.\n\nSafety rules:\n\n- Until a mode has 3 successful samples, it is charged the crate-wide\npeak, exactly as before. No compile is ever charged more than before.\n- Peaks are never lowered. An OOM kill raises both the mode peak and the\ncrate-wide peak.\n- Some compiles never count as samples, because their measurement may be\ntoo low: compiles that fail or are OOM-killed, and compiles run through\nanother wrapper such as sccache, which may return its own cache hit\nwithout running rustc.\n- The estimate is chosen from the command that actually runs, including\nincremental flags that mbx adds itself.\n- Clippy compiles learn their own estimates, apart from `cargo check`,\nbecause Clippy runs extra lint passes. A compile with more than one\n`--target` matches no single-target mode, so it falls back to the\ncrate-wide peak.\n- Links and test runs keep their existing estimates.\n\nTo see why a compile waits, use the new `mbx stats --scheduler`. It\nlists recorded peaks, sample counts, and charges, and a read-only\nsnapshot of current leases and waiters. Example output from a test\nfixture:\n\n```\nheavy: peak=41.3 GiB, samples=0, charge=41.3 GiB / 42 permits (crate-wide fallback)\nheavy [compile emit=metadata crate-type=rlib opt0 lto-default cgu- debug0 incremental-off]: peak=6.4 GiB, samples=3, charge=9.6 GiB / 10 permits (mode)\n```\n\n`--json` gives the same data in bytes. Its charges use the global\nscheduler settings, since a workspace `.mbx.toml` applies only to that\nworkspace's builds. The lease and waiter rows show the charge each\ncompile was really admitted or queued with. Without `--scheduler`, `mbx\nstats` output is unchanged.\n\nUpgrading: an older mbx sharing the same cache keeps charging the\ncrate-wide peak and may discard the new sample counts when it rewrites\nthe ledger. That only makes affected modes fall back to the crate-wide\npeak until they collect 3 new samples. Lease and waiter records gain\noptional fields, and older records still read correctly in both\ndirections.\n\nLimits: no estimate based on past compiles can prevent every\nout-of-memory kill. A source edit that makes rustc use much more memory\ncan still exceed a mode's charge once. After that compile, the higher\npeak is recorded. Pressure control and the available-memory check still\napply.\n\nValidation: all 964 mbx library tests pass, including the new scheduler,\nrustc, session, and stats tests. `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets\n--all-features -- -D warnings` passes. An independent review traced\nevery compiler launch path for estimates that are too low, and all its\nfindings were fixed. `mise` was not available locally, so the generated\n`docs/cli` pages were produced with the same `usage` generator and\noptions that `render:docs` uses. The full `mise run ci` gate was not run\nlocally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Added per-compile-mode memory estimates for scheduling. After three\nsuccessful samples, estimates use the mode’s measured peak with\nadditional headroom, capped at the crate-wide peak; until then,\nscheduling uses the crate-wide estimate.\n* Added `mbx stats --scheduler` to display memory estimates and a\nread-only snapshot of scheduler activity in text or JSON. Settings are\nread from the nearest `.mbx.toml`.\n* Scheduler estimates distinguish Clippy and compiler settings; wrapped\nor failed compiles do not count toward successful samples.\n* **Documentation**\n* Documented scheduler estimates, sampling behavior, settings\nresolution, and scheduler statistics.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T16:33:13Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2cde558f22802979cb0ec64f42c8e5ebe5243dba"}]},{"sha":"b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGIyNDRjNTBmYWZhYWJkNGJmYjBiNDhjZGIyZjVhYTcxYWNjNDY2NmQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:33:32Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:33:32Z"},"message":"fix(scheduler): honor an explicit memory budget that fits the local machine (#741)\n\nSince #721, mbx holds each container (pressure domain) to 85% of its own\ndetected memory. This keeps containers that share one host-sized budget\nwithin their cgroup limits. The same ceiling also applied on an ordinary\nhost, where it overrode an explicit `scheduler.memory` above 85% of RAM.\nBecause the ceiling is counted in permits, it also lowered CPU\nconcurrency for compiles that need no extra memory.\n\nExample: a host with 120 CPUs and 98.2 GiB of memory, configured with\n\n```toml\n[scheduler]\nmemory = \"98590438452\" # 91.8 GiB, 93.5% of RAM\n```\n\n- Before: the host was capped at 109 of its 120 permits. Admission\nstayed within the 85% default memory budget, and at most 109\nsingle-permit compiles ran at once.\n- After: all 120 permits and the full 91.8 GiB budget are available.\n\nThe 85% local ceiling now applies only when the configured budget is\nlarger than the local machine's memory. That is the shared-host case\n#721 targets, for example `memory = \"1260GiB\"` seen from a container\nwith a 64 GiB limit. These are unchanged: the CPU ceiling, CPU-only mode\n(`scheduler.memory = \"none\"`), and the behavior when memory cannot be\ndetected. #721 is not in a release yet, so no released version has this\nproblem.\n\nValidation: the 72 scheduler unit tests pass. New tests cover a budget\nat 93.5% of local memory, a budget equal to local memory, and one just\nabove it. `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D\nwarnings` passes. The full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Corrected scheduler capacity calculations so memory limits reduce\navailable permits only when the configured budget exceeds local memory.\nCapacity remains bounded by available CPUs.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to describe when local memory limits\naffect per-container capacity.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5e0b194600f65a23ebbdc289a3b745adc8a58e19","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/5e0b194600f65a23ebbdc289a3b745adc8a58e19"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1PTcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+gAQAIk7cfOFG2tpkppLHAtqir9T\nOpboE85orj8SWkz1zdmW70/kVk/i/Wwf4oLy9OPBQTEaSYb3hy026L+QrXoHWkBM\n3S/ENT2qugLa18RD4Tdt3YeHQw6DMWrG86ZXE/3/fzz+/HeL4wdSGBMXeZQQFa05\nxYLMLjmM0sXeluzAgjqrDZrQ5GzFQIFCLH5R3RwjlUb0FpqGGiL9kk3vmxlFtqOz\nZD7RPuERyz2hioLoFtATmnntqAcMLrawga23Oc8yWonYezY8z9mQHnnrSuMvjbKy\nQ+e5emioi8Z79h+AhZrxMs8zIoc+E4Cggp7WakkkCpU006YTgow1PVTFr7eTFfa4\nLmSTthpOvTW99pJ6kF/HEc1SNHtbERCq8RHKa2zqNNHI9ul3kGRDB+bjklWgiMEe\nhcB91nlzM9WIAdM6pT+ZMOWv1nG/ahuqR2eS9l/wVnuxpL0K5keC2gfLxutFW9PW\nAP1uPi29/vHoTAgD45Juf1tJgUZgulhhGRxc0jitx6GPb1h6k5lbl9WSXg2MUREq\nOxExLJGsPRL863XUZWXzfQYUFtmU54YmRO4N3uN3R9Gq0QioITdBMPqrV8/q2K88\n9wwT4B+Ftqh6Cy8wZSxkOnF1oXYpKJODBXw0MA2bmkpLq0w0de93a41vKDO+RnUZ\nsLfRXMiTmz2D67aai93H\n=q2au\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 5e0b194600f65a23ebbdc289a3b745adc8a58e19\nparent 2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792341212 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792341212 -0500\n\nfix(scheduler): honor an explicit memory budget that fits the local machine (#741)\n\nSince #721, mbx holds each container (pressure domain) to 85% of its own\ndetected memory. This keeps containers that share one host-sized budget\nwithin their cgroup limits. The same ceiling also applied on an ordinary\nhost, where it overrode an explicit `scheduler.memory` above 85% of RAM.\nBecause the ceiling is counted in permits, it also lowered CPU\nconcurrency for compiles that need no extra memory.\n\nExample: a host with 120 CPUs and 98.2 GiB of memory, configured with\n\n```toml\n[scheduler]\nmemory = \"98590438452\" # 91.8 GiB, 93.5% of RAM\n```\n\n- Before: the host was capped at 109 of its 120 permits. Admission\nstayed within the 85% default memory budget, and at most 109\nsingle-permit compiles ran at once.\n- After: all 120 permits and the full 91.8 GiB budget are available.\n\nThe 85% local ceiling now applies only when the configured budget is\nlarger than the local machine's memory. That is the shared-host case\n#721 targets, for example `memory = \"1260GiB\"` seen from a container\nwith a 64 GiB limit. These are unchanged: the CPU ceiling, CPU-only mode\n(`scheduler.memory = \"none\"`), and the behavior when memory cannot be\ndetected. #721 is not in a release yet, so no released version has this\nproblem.\n\nValidation: the 72 scheduler unit tests pass. New tests cover a budget\nat 93.5% of local memory, a budget equal to local memory, and one just\nabove it. `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D\nwarnings` passes. The full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Corrected scheduler capacity calculations so memory limits reduce\navailable permits only when the configured budget exceeds local memory.\nCapacity remains bounded by available CPUs.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to describe when local memory limits\naffect per-container capacity.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T16:33:32Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2e4679494d09d4758b6c366626c2d431701e7d85"}]},{"sha":"1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDFlMDAwNGQ1NzE3ZWU5NWU4ZDlkYWY2ZmI0MTE0NWE3ZjQwMjgwOTk","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:58:57Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T16:58:57Z"},"message":"chore(mise): move the benchmark and docs-render tasks to file tasks (#743)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/184\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThe largest shell tasks in `mise.toml` now live as file tasks under\n`mise-tasks/`, and `mise.toml` is about 55 lines shorter.\n\n- `bench` and `bench:refresh` are `mise-tasks/bench/_default` and\n`mise-tasks/bench/refresh`. They keep their names, descriptions, and\n`perf:prepare` dependency, so `mise run bench` and `mise run\nbench:refresh` work as before. The scripts use `set -euo pipefail` in\nplace of the explicit `|| exit \"$?\"`.\n- `render:docs` is `mise-tasks/render/docs`, a single Python script for\nevery platform. It replaces the Unix `sed` steps and the separate\n`run_windows` PowerShell one-liners, so the two platforms can no longer\ndrift apart. It preserves line endings, so a Windows run writes the same\nbytes as a Unix one.\n\n`bootstrap` stays in `mise.toml`: it has a `run_windows` variant and is\nthree lines.\n\nValidation: `mise tasks` lists all three tasks. On Linux, `mise run\nrender:docs` leaves `docs/cli` byte-identical to main. On the Windows\nrunner I ran the script's rewrite step and confirmed it keeps LF\nendings; that box has no `mise` or `usage`, so a full `render:docs` run\non Windows, including mise launching a Python file task there, is\nuntested. The benchmarks themselves were not run, since each is a full\nbuild of a third-party workspace.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Dev/CI task wiring and doc generation only; behavior is intended to\nstay equivalent aside from unified cross-platform docs rendering.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Moves the largest **mise** inline tasks out of `mise.toml` into **file\ntasks** under `mise-tasks/`, shortening the config by ~55 lines while\nkeeping the same task names (`bench`, `bench:refresh`, `render:docs`)\nand `perf:prepare` / `bootstrap` dependencies.\n> \n> **Benchmarks** (`mise-tasks/bench/_default` and `bench/refresh`) are\nthe same `real_world.py` invocations as before, with `set -euo pipefail`\ninstead of explicit `|| exit \"$?\"` in the refresh loop.\n> \n> **Docs rendering** (`mise-tasks/render/docs`) replaces separate Unix\n`sed` and Windows PowerShell `run_windows` blocks with one Python script\nthat generates `docs/cli`, demotes configuration headings, and fixes\nindex links while preserving line endings across platforms.\n> \n> **Tooling**: adds `python = \"3\"` to `[tools]` and pins **CPython\n3.15.0** in `mise.lock` so mise can run the Python file task\nconsistently.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\ncba4093d192cf60cec866b9d5ab834c9dc5b08e9. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Added benchmark tasks for running real-world performance scenarios,\nincluding warm, worktree, commit, edit, and contention tests. Results\ncan be saved for `hk` and `aube`.\n* Added a task to generate CLI reference documentation, including\nupdated configuration headings and links.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4dcd5fd86a08e5759f0b8163621d13e9e8897698","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/4dcd5fd86a08e5759f0b8163621d13e9e8897698"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1PrRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATgIQADYDnHzqPQ2EESkjHvpFsPn0\nz14gIgxSM0vNjYdqWQX3hTS59jZmQJj9k3bLkdjEHZMmb9ekq4/Xn3JvBDnFKGiT\nCch3ZDRAGrfM50ZmzcmoNWHMylYL1imj6lgG0TzK9AyKlwNU1jyTM1yBHdkfHn4B\n4aP3N7/rxkH86FdWe0BewpUAYEIWmVI+83Hy0Jr2K17N/r3EaEDt4KMLguKPjEb1\ny6I5QfrlOC2jd5zmwDFfIRjPMnJzdl0auGdi/Yz4QElsdL3XGSAvkjcxkwppeATo\nV6Is0zSDfufvcnbou+i/jsorRfiBa5cy1smqH3+HMkuIbdxZuCNcbdCBNUrCL2kW\ntM93GRtYxpDEBRWtadivDMgCcERUbzX4q2DLy6F1UTgMmAOW7ZWNemlksmHLW6JM\nhNUqEhR3SVi7FaUJAMCxKr+803HreXwyhozikzdG/P9/SVXomeHOJQhaRL2EiyEO\nBbVz9ZnXFV6bCh7Hc1udQBKLeoN97cQ8yykXl7ag7DctUzL7cRurLXNmr8NeqCeX\npAxZHmPd7NMfBE5xpXGUlihGIGoy8k6ZE/OanUyWary1BZ2A7a2QU1CoswCcK6Ar\nDtxa/L4wDrhtVb60byUVQPdZ1kVYmI+sEKC7+u4fNWXU5BrYwU7+IgBiN95S4Z+M\nA4u+9DXCCzKF4O0aityl\n=2Zuq\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 4dcd5fd86a08e5759f0b8163621d13e9e8897698\nparent b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792342737 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792342737 -0500\n\nchore(mise): move the benchmark and docs-render tasks to file tasks (#743)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/184\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThe largest shell tasks in `mise.toml` now live as file tasks under\n`mise-tasks/`, and `mise.toml` is about 55 lines shorter.\n\n- `bench` and `bench:refresh` are `mise-tasks/bench/_default` and\n`mise-tasks/bench/refresh`. They keep their names, descriptions, and\n`perf:prepare` dependency, so `mise run bench` and `mise run\nbench:refresh` work as before. The scripts use `set -euo pipefail` in\nplace of the explicit `|| exit \"$?\"`.\n- `render:docs` is `mise-tasks/render/docs`, a single Python script for\nevery platform. It replaces the Unix `sed` steps and the separate\n`run_windows` PowerShell one-liners, so the two platforms can no longer\ndrift apart. It preserves line endings, so a Windows run writes the same\nbytes as a Unix one.\n\n`bootstrap` stays in `mise.toml`: it has a `run_windows` variant and is\nthree lines.\n\nValidation: `mise tasks` lists all three tasks. On Linux, `mise run\nrender:docs` leaves `docs/cli` byte-identical to main. On the Windows\nrunner I ran the script's rewrite step and confirmed it keeps LF\nendings; that box has no `mise` or `usage`, so a full `render:docs` run\non Windows, including mise launching a Python file task there, is\nuntested. The benchmarks themselves were not run, since each is a full\nbuild of a third-party workspace.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Dev/CI task wiring and doc generation only; behavior is intended to\nstay equivalent aside from unified cross-platform docs rendering.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Moves the largest **mise** inline tasks out of `mise.toml` into **file\ntasks** under `mise-tasks/`, shortening the config by ~55 lines while\nkeeping the same task names (`bench`, `bench:refresh`, `render:docs`)\nand `perf:prepare` / `bootstrap` dependencies.\n> \n> **Benchmarks** (`mise-tasks/bench/_default` and `bench/refresh`) are\nthe same `real_world.py` invocations as before, with `set -euo pipefail`\ninstead of explicit `|| exit \"$?\"` in the refresh loop.\n> \n> **Docs rendering** (`mise-tasks/render/docs`) replaces separate Unix\n`sed` and Windows PowerShell `run_windows` blocks with one Python script\nthat generates `docs/cli`, demotes configuration headings, and fixes\nindex links while preserving line endings across platforms.\n> \n> **Tooling**: adds `python = \"3\"` to `[tools]` and pins **CPython\n3.15.0** in `mise.lock` so mise can run the Python file task\nconsistently.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\ncba4093d192cf60cec866b9d5ab834c9dc5b08e9. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Added benchmark tasks for running real-world performance scenarios,\nincluding warm, worktree, commit, edit, and contention tests. Results\ncan be saved for `hk` and `aube`.\n* Added a task to generate CLI reference documentation, including\nupdated configuration headings and links.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T16:58:58Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b244c50fafaabd4bfb0b48cdb2f5aa71acc4666d"}]},{"sha":"37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDM3Zjc4YWQwNTdhYTFiNjdiZTJlMzI3OTQyZDY4NjJlOGEyMWQ2N2U","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-18T18:23:37Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T18:23:37Z"},"message":"fix(materialize): stop restored build scripts from rerunning on the next unchanged build (#745)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/185\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nFixes #744.\n\nOn a cache hit, mbx stamped the restored build-script binary with an\nexplicit \\`SystemTime::now()\\`. Linux stamps ordinary writes from its\ncoarse clock, which can be up to one tick behind that reading. When\nCargo wrote \\`invoked.timestamp\\` within the same tick, the run's\n\\`output\\` came out older than the binary, so the next unchanged \\`cargo\nbuild\\` printed \\`Dirty ...: the dependency was rebuilt\\` and reran the\nbuild script and relinked. The issue measured this on 11-30% of\nfresh-target-dir builds.\n\nOn Unix, \\`set_modified_now\\` now calls \\`futimens\\` with \\`UTIME_NOW\\`,\nso the kernel picks the time from the same clock as every later write. A\nlater write can then tie with the stamp but never precede it, and\nCargo's strict \\`>\\` check treats a tie as fresh. Windows keeps the\nexplicit stamp.\n\nAdded a regression test that stamps a file and immediately writes\nanother 500 times, asserting the write is never older. I did not run the\nissue's end-to-end repro script; the test covers the ordering property\ndirectly.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Localized Unix mtime stamping for cache restores plus a regression\ntest; Windows behavior unchanged and failure mode is limited to\ntimestamp ordering for build artifacts.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes spurious Cargo “dependency was rebuilt” / build-script reruns\nafter a cache restore by aligning restored artifact mtimes with the\nkernel’s write clock on Unix.\n> \n> **`set_modified_now`** no longer stamps via `SystemTime::now()` on\nUnix. It opens the file read-only and calls **`futimens`** with\n**`UTIME_NOW`** for mtime (access time omitted), so the stamp uses the\nsame coarse clock as normal writes. That avoids a restored build-script\nbinary looking *newer* than **`invoked.timestamp`** when Cargo writes it\nin the same tick—Cargo’s strict `>` comparison then wrongly treats the\ndependency as dirty. **Windows** still uses the explicit\n`SystemTime::now()` path.\n> \n> Adds a **Unix regression test** that runs 500 stamp-then-write cycles\nand asserts the later write’s mtime is never older than the restore\nstamp.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\na4e8b0ca3238070b801af3fcb3bac7d1c5196e7c. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* On Unix, updating a file’s modification time now preserves its access\ntime and reports system-call errors. Other platforms retain their\nexisting behavior.\n* Added a check that subsequent file writes do not receive a timestamp\nearlier than the restored modification time.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ce575223a10cc19446c34772a159ae5b82174c47","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/ce575223a10cc19446c34772a159ae5b82174c47"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1Q6pCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAS3YQAB2ng9AsQgoP3MQ2GaCfK5YI\nLWL3jG+GAZfkxBBz1HpfctzQrKas5Z2cXlABN1i4ABTQafU+vwn0J77NGr0Kta/8\n8nz8xb2BKIV1FE+FoZW3ylOyR9Y2e2dYZ2AqJ86i9c+QAlZ7oaKbP665Egpjpvyl\nOujP97nOA+SlGzcMOasjsynbePgvxwVVVYwZPi2OX/nK+fzNeyFtF3mJXSwwxo14\nt5JRRFFYHGEDE9vCzx+GwZPfZW2+nXSOSTDAOa4szmKnJ1GP8ZsiQ1ijoomYWlAj\nlEc35aQ88eHAesASgGLyOa5xFjqHm6x5Ok5BWqnZmTsxJ8ITCQIusUOxySI1Gjkm\nMclcQBIXbyv6PZf3QgVFV8ykD6bdhrF8GfBWf+uUjpHJxdStMk4TOslYRryDO2cQ\nZ3a7m1fhdhVc4K2ysE36I6tutMFpVjCp1ce8hxqWGNBiHzDf3ufNHtcqueWqzvBC\ne34Ha5cgdmHNKVxbWfpJnhgg9zxV3KLFh5cAG61tZMeduBQn21e6w0q+mgDd6bP1\nwcnODVWJKEVlqJqmz4UFeZ3vHW2mW5D1c7Rr/SMzfLCAo559Xe6VN4aWaTuZ9MUl\nE4FChTDwMHT8wD2JX/ROAlrbe9zxnWgWj1Rh+y+hTCfsqgzu0qPYuL+lDLJeIKpo\nwxHfP/Xqu15W+p8OLDWc\n=hKys\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree ce575223a10cc19446c34772a159ae5b82174c47\nparent 1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792347817 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792347817 -0500\n\nfix(materialize): stop restored build scripts from rerunning on the next unchanged build (#745)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/185\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nFixes #744.\n\nOn a cache hit, mbx stamped the restored build-script binary with an\nexplicit \\`SystemTime::now()\\`. Linux stamps ordinary writes from its\ncoarse clock, which can be up to one tick behind that reading. When\nCargo wrote \\`invoked.timestamp\\` within the same tick, the run's\n\\`output\\` came out older than the binary, so the next unchanged \\`cargo\nbuild\\` printed \\`Dirty ...: the dependency was rebuilt\\` and reran the\nbuild script and relinked. The issue measured this on 11-30% of\nfresh-target-dir builds.\n\nOn Unix, \\`set_modified_now\\` now calls \\`futimens\\` with \\`UTIME_NOW\\`,\nso the kernel picks the time from the same clock as every later write. A\nlater write can then tie with the stamp but never precede it, and\nCargo's strict \\`>\\` check treats a tie as fresh. Windows keeps the\nexplicit stamp.\n\nAdded a regression test that stamps a file and immediately writes\nanother 500 times, asserting the write is never older. I did not run the\nissue's end-to-end repro script; the test covers the ordering property\ndirectly.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: unavailable.*\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Localized Unix mtime stamping for cache restores plus a regression\ntest; Windows behavior unchanged and failure mode is limited to\ntimestamp ordering for build artifacts.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Fixes spurious Cargo “dependency was rebuilt” / build-script reruns\nafter a cache restore by aligning restored artifact mtimes with the\nkernel’s write clock on Unix.\n> \n> **`set_modified_now`** no longer stamps via `SystemTime::now()` on\nUnix. It opens the file read-only and calls **`futimens`** with\n**`UTIME_NOW`** for mtime (access time omitted), so the stamp uses the\nsame coarse clock as normal writes. That avoids a restored build-script\nbinary looking *newer* than **`invoked.timestamp`** when Cargo writes it\nin the same tick—Cargo’s strict `>` comparison then wrongly treats the\ndependency as dirty. **Windows** still uses the explicit\n`SystemTime::now()` path.\n> \n> Adds a **Unix regression test** that runs 500 stamp-then-write cycles\nand asserts the later write’s mtime is never older than the restore\nstamp.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\na4e8b0ca3238070b801af3fcb3bac7d1c5196e7c. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* On Unix, updating a file’s modification time now preserves its access\ntime and reports system-call errors. Other platforms retain their\nexisting behavior.\n* Added a check that subsequent file writes do not receive a timestamp\nearlier than the restored modification time.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T18:23:38Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/1e0004d5717ee95e8d9daf6fb41145a7f4028099"}]},{"sha":"f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGY5MWRhNTg0NjFjNWRiMTZiNWY1NDFmNjE0ZTY4YWFlYWE2OTQyOGQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T19:53:11Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T19:53:11Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): record estimate accuracy and summarize it in mbx stats (#746)\n\nmbx reserves memory for each compile, link, and test run from an\nestimate. Until now there was no way to check those estimates against\nwhat the processes actually used. The ledger keeps only the highest peak\nfor each key, and lease files are deleted when a compile ends. This\nchange records both numbers for every scheduled process and summarizes\nthem in `mbx stats --scheduler`.\n\nEach finished compile, link, test binary, or nextest test that was\nadmitted with memory weighting on appends one JSON line to\n`<cache>/scheduler/observations.jsonl`. The line records:\n\n- the key, and where its estimate came from: `mode`, `crate-wide`,\n`link-history`, `link-window`, `link-floor`, `test-history`, or\n`unmeasured`;\n- the predicted bytes and the permits charged;\n- the peak memory mbx measured when the process exited;\n- the outcome: success, failure, OOM kill, or another signal;\n- whether another wrapper ran the compiler;\n- how long the request waited for admission and how long it ran.\n\nThe file is limited to 8 MiB, plus one older file. It is written only\nwhen the existing `events` setting is on, which is the default, so there\nis no new setting. Writing a line is a single append that never takes a\nlock or waits.\n\n`mbx stats --scheduler` now ends with an accuracy section. For each key\nit shows:\n\n- how many runs exceeded their reservation, and the worst ratio of peak\nto reservation;\n- the median and p90 ratio of peak to estimate;\n- the highest peak;\n- the average reserved memory that went unused.\n\nFailed and OOM-killed runs are counted separately, because they matter\nmost for safety but are not clean samples. Example from a host running\nthis build (abridged):\n\n```\nEstimate accuracy, 2026-10-18 18:06:43 UTC to 2026-10-18 18:13:17 UTC: 238 observations, 0 exceeded their reservation.\n  Worst: 0.95x of its reservation, bifrost_reference_differential [opt0 lto-default cgu- debugline-tables-only] [link] (success), at 2026-10-18 18:11:21 UTC.\n  Outcomes: 238 success, 0 failed, 0 OOM-killed, 0 other. Exceeded: 0 success, 0 failed, 0 OOM-killed, 0 other.\n  Median, p90 and unused memory count only successful compiles that ran without another wrapper.\n  bifrost_reference_differential [opt0 lto-default cgu- debugline-tables-only] [link]:\n    n=1 (failed=0, OOM=0), exceeded=0 (success=0, failed=0, OOM=0), worst=0.95x at 2026-10-18 18:11:21 UTC\n    peak/predicted median=0.99x p90=0.99x, highest peak=5.1 GiB, unused on average=295.1 MiB\n```\n\n`--json` includes every key, not just the 20 the text shows. Without\n`--scheduler`, `mbx stats` output is unchanged.\n\nLimits: the log is best-effort. When the file rotates while other\nprocesses are writing, a few lines can be lost, and a reader ignores any\npartial line. The estimate's source is recorded at admission from the\nsame function that chose the charge, so it is never reconstructed later.\n\nValidation: the 101 scheduler tests pass, including 11 new tests that\ncover:\n\n- the log schema and its line size limit;\n- rotation, including when another process holds the rotation lock;\n- the `events` and memory-weighting gates;\n- the recorded source for each prediction path;\n- the summary statistics on a fixture;\n- unchanged default `mbx stats` output.\n\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass. Before the last formatting commit, the full\nlibrary suite had 977 tests passing and one failure. That failure was\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\na renderer test this change does not touch. It failed in some earlier\nlocal runs too and passed in others, and it passes in CI. The build has\nbeen running on one shared build host and logging there. The full `mise\nrun ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Scheduler statistics now include admission-accuracy summaries,\nincluding observation counts, reservation exceedances,\npeak-to-reservation ratios, and per-key memory metrics.\n* When events are enabled and memory weighting is active, scheduler\nobservations record predictions and execution outcomes for reporting.\nFailed and out-of-memory runs count toward exceedances and worst-case\nratios; detailed clean-sample metrics use successful, unwrapped runs\nwith measured peaks.\n\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to explain observation collection and\nscheduler statistics reporting.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"02e28cd593ddf9dbfdc6d2d67db8ecd27935dd66","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/02e28cd593ddf9dbfdc6d2d67db8ecd27935dd66"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1SOnCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvRUQAA2bSIOx7vcSv+flko14lbOR\nR2lw+9Y7IYd4u7Ubx8n50D7T8SARim5JcKk71GO8KJ3+Ybnfx67h6vNoYP41rlDQ\njGBHWy7CZpA93W7ByFQLAf52yyzxVqrwAmTS7NWDABVbnmNJmAKMQVDHKE1Q73fc\ngzua+tYDlAWxB53cjiU7O+wxE6BxShqEs/SM/v3ka3SPjHgBSbEQQLgn6TuSY8uz\nwXiSllPMVw90eXcFokuECj3AHZIpGxxi+Oy1tTImeJsfLU9F996Dk+Z7Ed4mgApV\nCvLyJYZ15Ql9Xq1qNTtsqLZIZNkl/WVCVZuboqKlwM1oweXDB4oGPFys1CvrU/5p\nzFCoOQTVM4hZVbV90tK03k+vz9tDPxi2RZ0d1AD6JgjD2zV4cNZ57tSG9vvsmwLY\nvDvznPO2xxIY+GssbocI6t5X1YwKx0c/i3HXAmdZeQuAkH6/8v5r4QQTT1AwY3I6\nFS2i11nQyiXYJr1u1Exg0ASTrv8Hpr/eqcA5y0fG5a6Cf6Z478oz1OQEHoVmMrKE\n57wK+GYZs1HpfgfaaYoQ7Od8KVBfnmTJq33qVLqpn/TaLIapgCLABd4a2r6URISp\nIKh0X632C1td4Kk+974yYtWRpvdphAt+Wcto7M6kaMFYoWwZMZsLySJpSWpc4FRj\nJSSUKUsKpvLeuUtb69tG\n=IBlz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 02e28cd593ddf9dbfdc6d2d67db8ecd27935dd66\nparent 37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792353191 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792353191 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): record estimate accuracy and summarize it in mbx stats (#746)\n\nmbx reserves memory for each compile, link, and test run from an\nestimate. Until now there was no way to check those estimates against\nwhat the processes actually used. The ledger keeps only the highest peak\nfor each key, and lease files are deleted when a compile ends. This\nchange records both numbers for every scheduled process and summarizes\nthem in `mbx stats --scheduler`.\n\nEach finished compile, link, test binary, or nextest test that was\nadmitted with memory weighting on appends one JSON line to\n`<cache>/scheduler/observations.jsonl`. The line records:\n\n- the key, and where its estimate came from: `mode`, `crate-wide`,\n`link-history`, `link-window`, `link-floor`, `test-history`, or\n`unmeasured`;\n- the predicted bytes and the permits charged;\n- the peak memory mbx measured when the process exited;\n- the outcome: success, failure, OOM kill, or another signal;\n- whether another wrapper ran the compiler;\n- how long the request waited for admission and how long it ran.\n\nThe file is limited to 8 MiB, plus one older file. It is written only\nwhen the existing `events` setting is on, which is the default, so there\nis no new setting. Writing a line is a single append that never takes a\nlock or waits.\n\n`mbx stats --scheduler` now ends with an accuracy section. For each key\nit shows:\n\n- how many runs exceeded their reservation, and the worst ratio of peak\nto reservation;\n- the median and p90 ratio of peak to estimate;\n- the highest peak;\n- the average reserved memory that went unused.\n\nFailed and OOM-killed runs are counted separately, because they matter\nmost for safety but are not clean samples. Example from a host running\nthis build (abridged):\n\n```\nEstimate accuracy, 2026-10-18 18:06:43 UTC to 2026-10-18 18:13:17 UTC: 238 observations, 0 exceeded their reservation.\n  Worst: 0.95x of its reservation, bifrost_reference_differential [opt0 lto-default cgu- debugline-tables-only] [link] (success), at 2026-10-18 18:11:21 UTC.\n  Outcomes: 238 success, 0 failed, 0 OOM-killed, 0 other. Exceeded: 0 success, 0 failed, 0 OOM-killed, 0 other.\n  Median, p90 and unused memory count only successful compiles that ran without another wrapper.\n  bifrost_reference_differential [opt0 lto-default cgu- debugline-tables-only] [link]:\n    n=1 (failed=0, OOM=0), exceeded=0 (success=0, failed=0, OOM=0), worst=0.95x at 2026-10-18 18:11:21 UTC\n    peak/predicted median=0.99x p90=0.99x, highest peak=5.1 GiB, unused on average=295.1 MiB\n```\n\n`--json` includes every key, not just the 20 the text shows. Without\n`--scheduler`, `mbx stats` output is unchanged.\n\nLimits: the log is best-effort. When the file rotates while other\nprocesses are writing, a few lines can be lost, and a reader ignores any\npartial line. The estimate's source is recorded at admission from the\nsame function that chose the charge, so it is never reconstructed later.\n\nValidation: the 101 scheduler tests pass, including 11 new tests that\ncover:\n\n- the log schema and its line size limit;\n- rotation, including when another process holds the rotation lock;\n- the `events` and memory-weighting gates;\n- the recorded source for each prediction path;\n- the summary statistics on a fixture;\n- unchanged default `mbx stats` output.\n\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass. Before the last formatting commit, the full\nlibrary suite had 977 tests passing and one failure. That failure was\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\na renderer test this change does not touch. It failed in some earlier\nlocal runs too and passed in others, and it passes in CI. The build has\nbeen running on one shared build host and logging there. The full `mise\nrun ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Scheduler statistics now include admission-accuracy summaries,\nincluding observation counts, reservation exceedances,\npeak-to-reservation ratios, and per-key memory metrics.\n* When events are enabled and memory weighting is active, scheduler\nobservations record predictions and execution outcomes for reporting.\nFailed and out-of-memory runs count toward exceedances and worst-case\nratios; detailed clean-sample metrics use successful, unwrapped runs\nwith measured peaks.\n\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to explain observation collection and\nscheduler statistics reporting.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T19:53:12Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/37f78ad057aa1b67be2e327942d6862e8a21d67e"}]},{"sha":"b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGI0MDE3NTk2YjIzYTAyNzU0N2MyNDc3ZDUxMTQyOTk3NmZjZDUzMGY","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-18T21:08:24Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T21:08:24Z"},"message":"fix(docs): make sponsor logos visible in light mode (#747)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/186\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThe footer sponsor logos are light-coloured SVGs from the jdx.dev feed,\ndesigned for dark backgrounds. On the live site's light theme they have\nno background, so white logos sit on a white page and are nearly\ninvisible.\n\nThis gives each logo a dark chip in light mode (hover: slightly lighter)\nand keeps the theme's soft background in dark mode, matching the mise\ndocs. The logos themselves are unchanged.\n\nVerified against the live site's computed styles in both themes; the fix\nitself was checked by building the docs and inspecting the compiled CSS,\nnot visually in a browser.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.289.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Scoped CSS-only change to the docs footer sponsor component with no\nauth, data, or runtime behavior impact.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Footer sponsor logos from the jdx.dev feed are light SVGs that were\nhard to see on the docs site’s **light theme** because the logo chips\nhad no fill.\n> \n> This PR adds a **dark chip** (`#171717`, hover `#242424`) behind each\n`.EndevSponsorsLogo` in light mode, while **dark mode** uses VitePress\nsoft/elevated backgrounds on default and hover. Hover still uses the\nbrand border color; logo assets and fetch logic are unchanged.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\na5f4bf5b63cfe9ae72aebb2f8b254dad9b3b612f. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Style**\n* Updated sponsor logo backgrounds in light and dark themes, including\nsofter backgrounds on hover and elevated backgrounds in dark mode.\nHovering also retains the brand-colored border change.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3e8cbb08cd3bfc882c0d7cc234ed17fb2802e24e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/3e8cbb08cd3bfc882c0d7cc234ed17fb2802e24e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1TVICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/QoQAB8MCd8HSAXd2xtDO6U5Kb6S\ndsuaswkvIAIVq2AkZGodCPBxLC/mchb1fWwGAp6GmXQt55XX2SJdsX1fLV1VxIi2\nGS+yN1lkiwhVVhxb4RqyMS+7Bdub+8+efTJEX2y2RV0uL/cOCmVXbTTIyTRD3msS\nG4qQ0YfY/mIeb5fOxbLIeFFpkbuM2b0CJKX3e0mfrQhUC1Zhw4RXh922nR/JgtZz\nlyV0dwzBqcwuX55/COrhX+C5K31ZvYgLGvYzPEsQlejGWJ18Xhg3KzLrr6N1cU+C\nn8Ls9EyBBBj1i6wUwoNw2R840SxDABnULHMJ0lLUopR7wprIVnO6uQqyMRnT1qms\neaK95zzebgHfv+VpYkoaS92TRm2NxO7uCr/ni/WQSYJIkoYm2ncwUbeAPITz0M40\nDWSYt3QhPISA9NRcOMXaY4WRqPCrt3VeO25PQemVZ2NHx+doUe8OSNJRdBOqyc9x\nwWdc7UhKkzmI3/zfqHaO2oZG6CELBjZpq6Mclezxi59990y7UyHz/FrZffAbYENP\nxn6B0a0LsG52j/ReCR2Xj4MTjMidfji5S7uQ41j2SSdcHGxB7rwt+K/U4DXZUXGG\nzFPoktSWLBR5MKb3qAHFyHsICGualUxjq6kB8lXVFLisUtxkAxRkofSYZ3qPc1rZ\nEpiVw2TeskUdKcPE2ZKr\n=yT0D\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 3e8cbb08cd3bfc882c0d7cc234ed17fb2802e24e\nparent f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792357704 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792357704 -0500\n\nfix(docs): make sponsor logos visible in light mode (#747)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/186\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThe footer sponsor logos are light-coloured SVGs from the jdx.dev feed,\ndesigned for dark backgrounds. On the live site's light theme they have\nno background, so white logos sit on a white page and are nearly\ninvisible.\n\nThis gives each logo a dark chip in light mode (hover: slightly lighter)\nand keeps the theme's soft background in dark mode, matching the mise\ndocs. The logos themselves are unchanged.\n\nVerified against the live site's computed styles in both themes; the fix\nitself was checked by building the docs and inspecting the compiled CSS,\nnot visually in a browser.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.289.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> Scoped CSS-only change to the docs footer sponsor component with no\nauth, data, or runtime behavior impact.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> Footer sponsor logos from the jdx.dev feed are light SVGs that were\nhard to see on the docs site’s **light theme** because the logo chips\nhad no fill.\n> \n> This PR adds a **dark chip** (`#171717`, hover `#242424`) behind each\n`.EndevSponsorsLogo` in light mode, while **dark mode** uses VitePress\nsoft/elevated backgrounds on default and hover. Hover still uses the\nbrand border color; logo assets and fetch logic are unchanged.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\na5f4bf5b63cfe9ae72aebb2f8b254dad9b3b612f. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Style**\n* Updated sponsor logo backgrounds in light and dark themes, including\nsofter backgrounds on hover and elevated backgrounds in dark mode.\nHovering also retains the brand-colored border change.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T21:08:25Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/f91da58461c5db16b5f541f614e68aaeaa69428d"}]},{"sha":"ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGNhNjRjNmVjMzYwMDQzZjM1ZmFkOTZiMjI5ZjBjZTg0MGY3YTczOGE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T21:09:24Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T21:09:24Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): record build identity and reservations in the observation log (#749)\n\nmbx's scheduler observation log (#746) records one line per finished\ncompile, link, and test run. Replaying it to compare admission policies\nshowed two gaps. The log has no build identity, so per-build limits\ncannot be modelled. And `mbx reserve` writes no observation, so capacity\nheld by reserved external commands, such as test runs that wrap a whole\nbuild, is invisible. In a replay of 3,333 real admissions, the modelled\nbaseline accounted for only 57% of the waiting actually recorded. These\nwere the two main missing inputs.\n\nThis change fills both gaps:\n\n- Each observation can now include `build_id`, `build_capacity` (that\nbuild's permit ceiling), and `domain_capacity`, when the scheduler knows\nthem. All three are optional and omitted when unset. The line version\nstays 1, so older readers and older lines keep working.\n- When an `mbx reserve` reservation ends, mbx appends an observation\nwith kind `reservation`. It records:\n  - the reserved permits;\n  - the bytes per permit;\n  - the admission wait and run times;\n  - the external command's outcome, or `unknown` if it could not start.\n\nA reservation has no measured peak, so `mbx stats --scheduler` counts\nreservations under their own `reservation` key and never treats them as\nexceeding a reservation.\n\nBoth follow the existing gates. Nothing is written when `events = false`\nor when memory weighting is off.\n\nValidation: the scheduler and reserve tests pass. They cover:\n\n- the schema with and without the new fields;\n- old lines still parsing;\n- a reservation being recorded when it ends, and respecting the `events`\ngate;\n- the summary handling reservation lines.\n\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass. The full library suite passed 979 tests and\nfailed 3, all in tests this change does not touch. One is the renderer\ntest `mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`. The\nother two are `materialize` filesystem-timestamp tests:\n`a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it` and\n`a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThey ran on a heavily loaded host, and they fail intermittently there.\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Reservation activity is now included in scheduling observations, with\nreservation weight, wait and run times, and command outcome.\n* Scheduling observations can include build identity and applicable\nbuild and domain capacity details. Older observation records remain\ncompatible.\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Reservation requests that lack required resources, or request memory\nwhen memory scheduling is unavailable, now return an error.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to describe the additional reservation\nobservation details.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d45c380a470e88642e7a7f1558eb28962bf0df42","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/d45c380a470e88642e7a7f1558eb28962bf0df42"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1TWECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQM0QAD+8U19//6gSaSCh3n+AZP/o\nhoqZj93rSlgkHZcmLB5MGKq0NYZfVMadmEJW4bfpTYIyfzS4CkAqLPPmgY1u9Jrk\niqxBc+Ew2hZA/C56pgHRfy7UmXMuBF9jN8NYjB0KWABg3/Y1cDtV52WfiRfkHE+6\n21GlkIuOoQT6QCSp6fE80zUYoH8jIno22IgE+06V+hukxiUz3IE6Za7NOw09a3Y9\np9vKjfbGKYn50K2iFu3upCwowqkr95G89nQqaeTBC4mVkBzeOpGidoRAUfUK+J+v\nR+d5v5DruAhSkzCCLtO40uV7TZO25Mv4dYygYJY/gL5SW0eiaTUUuc8pFoYh6RUQ\nqiGsfpr+7CRPQedLmMWk9XbJ0gceZ5lKQBg0JmehGI3A7qzqZduuFVWmzRdkg5li\nZxu60K7UmC2f8K2Egg7wFSfd3mEZXaax82ydTt18bX8FKX9/I3ZxCaTiSIsvVmIH\ngMrYmiQwEPQb5ldbGvd+MNKmU7uDAAbPlfq7w6vcv7Ig1O35cPd9nPDnc+HnyfvI\nOe1XVrzdSch09R/WmYyc/xcvAmilaTPSpyETc1FF4P0qbfy7QnaMtG9W1jsrRuC5\nBPK/sVEbc+X37YGGFNDeIAAVO56+e1n1+lg8WTAZ0Bp0kvd6DMCS7rlKhMVqHrGE\nyMSh4g0oX73Xct3mRSSV\n=WmOC\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree d45c380a470e88642e7a7f1558eb28962bf0df42\nparent b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792357764 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792357764 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): record build identity and reservations in the observation log (#749)\n\nmbx's scheduler observation log (#746) records one line per finished\ncompile, link, and test run. Replaying it to compare admission policies\nshowed two gaps. The log has no build identity, so per-build limits\ncannot be modelled. And `mbx reserve` writes no observation, so capacity\nheld by reserved external commands, such as test runs that wrap a whole\nbuild, is invisible. In a replay of 3,333 real admissions, the modelled\nbaseline accounted for only 57% of the waiting actually recorded. These\nwere the two main missing inputs.\n\nThis change fills both gaps:\n\n- Each observation can now include `build_id`, `build_capacity` (that\nbuild's permit ceiling), and `domain_capacity`, when the scheduler knows\nthem. All three are optional and omitted when unset. The line version\nstays 1, so older readers and older lines keep working.\n- When an `mbx reserve` reservation ends, mbx appends an observation\nwith kind `reservation`. It records:\n  - the reserved permits;\n  - the bytes per permit;\n  - the admission wait and run times;\n  - the external command's outcome, or `unknown` if it could not start.\n\nA reservation has no measured peak, so `mbx stats --scheduler` counts\nreservations under their own `reservation` key and never treats them as\nexceeding a reservation.\n\nBoth follow the existing gates. Nothing is written when `events = false`\nor when memory weighting is off.\n\nValidation: the scheduler and reserve tests pass. They cover:\n\n- the schema with and without the new fields;\n- old lines still parsing;\n- a reservation being recorded when it ends, and respecting the `events`\ngate;\n- the summary handling reservation lines.\n\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass. The full library suite passed 979 tests and\nfailed 3, all in tests this change does not touch. One is the renderer\ntest `mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`. The\nother two are `materialize` filesystem-timestamp tests:\n`a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it` and\n`a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThey ran on a heavily loaded host, and they fail intermittently there.\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Reservation activity is now included in scheduling observations, with\nreservation weight, wait and run times, and command outcome.\n* Scheduling observations can include build identity and applicable\nbuild and domain capacity details. Older observation records remain\ncompatible.\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Reservation requests that lack required resources, or request memory\nwhen memory scheduling is unavailable, now return an error.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to describe the additional reservation\nobservation details.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T21:09:25Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b4017596b23a027547c2477d511429976fcd530f"}]},{"sha":"06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDA2NjA1YmI2NWJhZmNlZTM1YTNhZGZlNTFmYmU0Y2EyMzA3NmYyZGQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:54:06Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:54:06Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): let queued builds share capacity they cannot use yet (#748)\n\nOrdered admission (#721) keeps a large compilation from being starved by\na stream of small ones: while an older request waits, newer requests are\nrefused. That refusal is broader than it needs to be. On a shared build\nhost, a typical state was 120 permits, two 54-permit compilations\nrunning, and a 54-permit request waiting. Only 12 permits were idle, but\nevery new 1-permit C compile from a build script was refused until a\nlarge compilation finished. mbx's observation log (#746) recorded the\ncost over one hour. Of 708 requests that waited more than 5 seconds, 642\nhad enough free permits for their own size during most of their wait.\nTogether they waited 832 minutes, and one 2-second compile waited 400\nseconds.\n\nNow a newer request may start while older requests wait, provided it\nonly uses capacity the waiting requests could not use when they next\nbecome able to start. The check needs no run-time estimates:\n\n- The older waiting requests are taken in queue order. Each one becomes\nable to start only once enough running leases and earlier queued\nrequests have finished.\n- For each of them, mbx computes the spare room it would have at that\nmoment: the smallest amount any combination of finished leases and\nrequests could overshoot its need by.\n- A newer request passes only if it fits in that spare room for every\nolder request.\n- This check is applied in every scope the two requests share: the whole\npool, the container's limit, and the build's limit. Processes sharing a\ncache can have different total budgets, so the whole-pool check is also\nrun against each waiting process's own budget.\n- Anything today's rule already admits is still admitted.\n\nThe computation is one bitset subset-sum pass over permit counts per\nscope, done inside the registrar lock that admission already holds.\n\nIn the example above, the spare room is 12 permits. Small compiles can\nuse the idle permits, and the waiting 54-permit request still starts as\nsoon as one 54-permit compilation finishes.\n\nWhat this guarantees:\n\n- **Within one scope, a newer request never delays an older one.** A\nrandomized simulation checked this against today's rule. It ran 20,000\nseeds, 1.39 million requests and 188,491 passes, with capacities of 4 to\n128 permits. In every case the run was split at the moment of each pass\nand continued under today's rule. No waiting request started later,\nwhether it arrived before or after the passer. Over whole runs, no\nrequest started later, 675,001 started earlier, and total waiting fell\n10.8%.\n- **With build and container limits, a pass can rarely lead to a small\ndelay.**\n- The cause is today's existing exception. When a request waits on its\nown build's or container's limit, requests from other builds and\ncontainers may already start.\n- Once a passer leaves the queue, it no longer holds those requests\nback, and one of them can take capacity the waiting request later needs.\n- In 20,000 multi-scope simulations, an older request was delayed in 229\nof 180,805 passes (0.13%), by 10 simulation ticks on average and 45 at\nmost.\n- Rerunning those cases with today's exceptions turned off in both\ncopies removes every delay. The pass check itself is therefore not the\ncause.\n- Over whole runs with these limits, 0.9% of requests started later and\n37% started earlier. Total waiting fell 9.3%, and the p95 wait for\nrequests over 20 permits was unchanged.\n- **No request can starve.** Once an older request's own limit allows it\nto start, it again holds back every newer request that does not fit in\nits spare room.\n\nA replay of 3,333 real admissions from mbx's observation log points the\nsame way. It modelled one scope and kept the recorded arrival and run\ntimes. This rule cut the modelled total wait from 25.6 to 9.1\nrequest-hours and delayed no request. The modelled baseline accounts for\nonly part of the waiting actually recorded, because the log does not\ncapture build limits or `mbx reserve` reservations. So that figure is a\ncomparison between policies, not a forecast.\n\nTests:\n\n- property tests over 512 seeds each, running in-process against the\nsame decision function the pool uses;\n- the worked example above;\n- every counterexample found while designing the rule, including one\nthat documents the known one-tick delay caused by today's build\nexception;\n- integration tests through the pool on a temporary directory.\n\nOne existing test,\n`cancelled_and_crashed_waiters_release_their_queue_positions`,\npreviously asserted that a 1-permit request is refused beside a 2-permit\nwaiter in a 4-permit pool where it provably cannot delay that waiter. It\nnow asserts admission. Its crash subcase uses a full-pool waiter, so the\nrefusal-and-recovery path is still covered.\n\nValidation:\n\n- the 102 scheduler tests pass;\n- `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass;\n- the full library suite passed 978 tests with 2 failures, in code this\nchange does not touch:\n-\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\nwhich is intermittent locally;\n-\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it`,\na filesystem timing test in a file identical to `main`, run on a heavily\nloaded host.\n\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Scheduling**\n* Newer compilation requests can use capacity an older request cannot\nuse, while respecting queue priority when admission could delay older\nrequests.\n* Cancelled or terminated waiting requests release their place in the\nqueue.\n* Requests waiting on their own build or container limits may be delayed\nafter a scheduling pass.\n* **Documentation**\n* Clarified how request ordering, capacity limits, and cancellation\naffect scheduling.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"22e2a4e662925cc262f0436510e9cd399f3569f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/22e2a4e662925cc262f0436510e9cd399f3569f5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1VweCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHVAQAHy1LQEmwR7dMmVfa9686nlN\nLo3xffSVaxEivR9Kj3Kc0FSMf33NWxP+Azp5FTJpKlIWQSAysXt2Z45tm9e3ZUaj\n5AIfyGg0Ih66Shb7IqFuz0Vlzrns7pxCFKJKEQ7dj5b/49whNPu8Ql/VgvVIDknQ\nK2hYu8ADy6CG3t9me+1/JEul5fwOqf39iWpMWltvRvjcW9LpYZeg0Ei8YsMVd3M3\nMommGKgCPBTBG3PsA5LayTmMqZG0T9qef5sF+E++nZHfXoCdi+8NR8qn62yySKvS\ntRcgpOnIKxWV1ZjDm4gDTXMMvz+IvWJ5v/lHkCPlIpB0OZKX/JIdbxRsQdeFvy+6\n4t1si78NWi7WYk+fs9NH48FS0lXtYoGGtRGG+2mGFSdh0fP4e4BbZFArlQbOJo6P\ntlZFkfKgleZn1ChiEm7a5NhrBCRWCiEM1PlS5gQmZWJLcFSPesno7I85jUiUJNKw\ngJ7wob6KSWW6BruJfe4Qk0RPV6WIk+lcwLKvkBoxAxNHuNaUQ1WF4PBqUro0VSCA\ntGLdhcrMY+0woTxSMWAWkNcmOZLWP36wt9qGS0DJkw1+6TmRNXl2F56Z6sE08VX/\nm+I4LoayTTWKkBPdw5WL6YMEtGT6dZ+eeF+2P2A+xvyuPtlpU5wd9t7vR2Qy/kCt\n5wJW7WL5um9p5uMpV+71\n=Jsx1\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 22e2a4e662925cc262f0436510e9cd399f3569f5\nparent ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792367646 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792367646 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): let queued builds share capacity they cannot use yet (#748)\n\nOrdered admission (#721) keeps a large compilation from being starved by\na stream of small ones: while an older request waits, newer requests are\nrefused. That refusal is broader than it needs to be. On a shared build\nhost, a typical state was 120 permits, two 54-permit compilations\nrunning, and a 54-permit request waiting. Only 12 permits were idle, but\nevery new 1-permit C compile from a build script was refused until a\nlarge compilation finished. mbx's observation log (#746) recorded the\ncost over one hour. Of 708 requests that waited more than 5 seconds, 642\nhad enough free permits for their own size during most of their wait.\nTogether they waited 832 minutes, and one 2-second compile waited 400\nseconds.\n\nNow a newer request may start while older requests wait, provided it\nonly uses capacity the waiting requests could not use when they next\nbecome able to start. The check needs no run-time estimates:\n\n- The older waiting requests are taken in queue order. Each one becomes\nable to start only once enough running leases and earlier queued\nrequests have finished.\n- For each of them, mbx computes the spare room it would have at that\nmoment: the smallest amount any combination of finished leases and\nrequests could overshoot its need by.\n- A newer request passes only if it fits in that spare room for every\nolder request.\n- This check is applied in every scope the two requests share: the whole\npool, the container's limit, and the build's limit. Processes sharing a\ncache can have different total budgets, so the whole-pool check is also\nrun against each waiting process's own budget.\n- Anything today's rule already admits is still admitted.\n\nThe computation is one bitset subset-sum pass over permit counts per\nscope, done inside the registrar lock that admission already holds.\n\nIn the example above, the spare room is 12 permits. Small compiles can\nuse the idle permits, and the waiting 54-permit request still starts as\nsoon as one 54-permit compilation finishes.\n\nWhat this guarantees:\n\n- **Within one scope, a newer request never delays an older one.** A\nrandomized simulation checked this against today's rule. It ran 20,000\nseeds, 1.39 million requests and 188,491 passes, with capacities of 4 to\n128 permits. In every case the run was split at the moment of each pass\nand continued under today's rule. No waiting request started later,\nwhether it arrived before or after the passer. Over whole runs, no\nrequest started later, 675,001 started earlier, and total waiting fell\n10.8%.\n- **With build and container limits, a pass can rarely lead to a small\ndelay.**\n- The cause is today's existing exception. When a request waits on its\nown build's or container's limit, requests from other builds and\ncontainers may already start.\n- Once a passer leaves the queue, it no longer holds those requests\nback, and one of them can take capacity the waiting request later needs.\n- In 20,000 multi-scope simulations, an older request was delayed in 229\nof 180,805 passes (0.13%), by 10 simulation ticks on average and 45 at\nmost.\n- Rerunning those cases with today's exceptions turned off in both\ncopies removes every delay. The pass check itself is therefore not the\ncause.\n- Over whole runs with these limits, 0.9% of requests started later and\n37% started earlier. Total waiting fell 9.3%, and the p95 wait for\nrequests over 20 permits was unchanged.\n- **No request can starve.** Once an older request's own limit allows it\nto start, it again holds back every newer request that does not fit in\nits spare room.\n\nA replay of 3,333 real admissions from mbx's observation log points the\nsame way. It modelled one scope and kept the recorded arrival and run\ntimes. This rule cut the modelled total wait from 25.6 to 9.1\nrequest-hours and delayed no request. The modelled baseline accounts for\nonly part of the waiting actually recorded, because the log does not\ncapture build limits or `mbx reserve` reservations. So that figure is a\ncomparison between policies, not a forecast.\n\nTests:\n\n- property tests over 512 seeds each, running in-process against the\nsame decision function the pool uses;\n- the worked example above;\n- every counterexample found while designing the rule, including one\nthat documents the known one-tick delay caused by today's build\nexception;\n- integration tests through the pool on a temporary directory.\n\nOne existing test,\n`cancelled_and_crashed_waiters_release_their_queue_positions`,\npreviously asserted that a 1-permit request is refused beside a 2-permit\nwaiter in a 4-permit pool where it provably cannot delay that waiter. It\nnow asserts admission. Its crash subcase uses a full-pool waiter, so the\nrefusal-and-recovery path is still covered.\n\nValidation:\n\n- the 102 scheduler tests pass;\n- `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check` pass;\n- the full library suite passed 978 tests with 2 failures, in code this\nchange does not touch:\n-\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\nwhich is intermittent locally;\n-\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it`,\na filesystem timing test in a file identical to `main`, run on a heavily\nloaded host.\n\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Scheduling**\n* Newer compilation requests can use capacity an older request cannot\nuse, while respecting queue priority when admission could delay older\nrequests.\n* Cancelled or terminated waiting requests release their place in the\nqueue.\n* Requests waiting on their own build or container limits may be delayed\nafter a scheduling pass.\n* **Documentation**\n* Clarified how request ordering, capacity limits, and cancellation\naffect scheduling.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T23:54:07Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/ca64c6ec360043f35fad96b229f0ce840f7a738a"}]},{"sha":"742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDc0MjkyN2VhODJmNjhlMjViMzc5YWU5N2I2OTc5YmFiOTBlNGQ4NGU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:54:55Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:54:55Z"},"message":"fix(gc): prune incremental state after worktree removal (#752)\n\n## Problem\n\nIncremental GC checked whether a checkout still exists through\n`checkout_is_live`, which uses the checkout's parent directory as its\nreference point. When a worktree manager or a test harness removes the\ncheckout together with its parent, the parent is gone too, so\n`checkout_is_live` answered \"live\" and the state stayed until\n`gc.incremental_max_age` (30 days). On the reporting machine 26 such\ncheckouts were stuck this way.\n\n## Change\n\nIncremental state now uses `checkout_is_live_on` with the incremental\nroot, as managed targets already do with their root. A deleted checkout\nis collected on the next sweep. A checkout whose nearest existing\nancestor is on another filesystem (an unavailable mount, or a container\npath seen from the host) is still treated as live.\n\n## Validation\n\nA regression test removes the checkout and its parent and asserts the\nstate is collected; a Linux test confirms a missing checkout on another\nfilesystem is kept. `cargo test -p mbx incremental`, `cargo test -p\nmbx-cache-store`, clippy and fmt pass.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code via Mjolnir; model:\nanthropic/claude-fable-5-1 (design, review), openai/gpt-6-luna\n(implementation subagent); version: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Improved incremental checkout cleanup so active checkouts are retained\nwhen their parent workspace is removed or when they reside on a\ndifferent filesystem. This prevents valid checkouts from being\nincorrectly treated as abandoned during collection.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7470bbe4fb083ff0cfa0e7e40658fb611303c846","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/7470bbe4fb083ff0cfa0e7e40658fb611303c846"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1VxPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHxYQABbULy/UVtWTvxyqHmpsVnku\n92MRY1wNYwDt4+9g7Dl7yzT/a4kAhvm1o/uAqxgEhEvEmcR3pI+XXaw/3CPNhkCg\n6P8QaEG5ksrjT8TWTmiRx/5UZY5Ls8HAsaIw4BDIyZTekFTQS0JWmcayzaNYgQTw\nl31AMk1msajswyJl/8+vBXJW/kudRWSMGAq0ESUk/pXuGL9GxS17XZqR1w2zRbOM\ngZEek5f1LTQA24IFz7UuJ8OAKXwdYyKU9NLVHsnEEr79/m8J8xHfmHhGmPylcrsG\ndQjGxKy7eo2ziKvyIhD1Q/yWLH5yxEncLWwXdF/1YB3At7ckWWGxvGof9RwAx5GO\nw+UT82KML977HA5ziYZ+uemj8UIpF547HQ/qVKDwng573gAoqJ2pksgG4lh3qaXk\nI4UeaKccB7AQ01mhkmQisATpJbSSDpNBdY/2jj12oRcrG1AqnLkT6kLflCqwVRDO\nGrHcWdZAxL6rK/tavd7h1y5lJ9lA1H3oDdnnCf9u81Nhc099tOAhKf0EnTAZ9ysX\nPGJEosOHjamOBi0uEGtulFxjSfVwuk9XSAgnw9wkBlWOX62N1GwgFZBxg0SVcGk1\n8VFLU/BboIFqCsW/qOTsLWnLCxxeiqGoILRckF85pm52kh6cN295boXB4p4SvLSU\nLs89oSgfs70FPu3f1ahQ\n=BTj5\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7470bbe4fb083ff0cfa0e7e40658fb611303c846\nparent 06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792367695 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792367695 -0500\n\nfix(gc): prune incremental state after worktree removal (#752)\n\n## Problem\n\nIncremental GC checked whether a checkout still exists through\n`checkout_is_live`, which uses the checkout's parent directory as its\nreference point. When a worktree manager or a test harness removes the\ncheckout together with its parent, the parent is gone too, so\n`checkout_is_live` answered \"live\" and the state stayed until\n`gc.incremental_max_age` (30 days). On the reporting machine 26 such\ncheckouts were stuck this way.\n\n## Change\n\nIncremental state now uses `checkout_is_live_on` with the incremental\nroot, as managed targets already do with their root. A deleted checkout\nis collected on the next sweep. A checkout whose nearest existing\nancestor is on another filesystem (an unavailable mount, or a container\npath seen from the host) is still treated as live.\n\n## Validation\n\nA regression test removes the checkout and its parent and asserts the\nstate is collected; a Linux test confirms a missing checkout on another\nfilesystem is kept. `cargo test -p mbx incremental`, `cargo test -p\nmbx-cache-store`, clippy and fmt pass.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code via Mjolnir; model:\nanthropic/claude-fable-5-1 (design, review), openai/gpt-6-luna\n(implementation subagent); version: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Improved incremental checkout cleanup so active checkouts are retained\nwhen their parent workspace is removed or when they reside on a\ndifferent filesystem. This prevents valid checkouts from being\nincorrectly treated as abandoned during collection.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T23:54:55Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/06605bb65bafcee35a3adfe51fbe4ca23076f2dd"}]},{"sha":"614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDYxNGZkN2FhNjRkODYwYWNmOWFjMjZjZTdmMTY0ZGRkYjNiY2EwNmM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:55:48Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-18T23:55:48Z"},"message":"fix(gc): cap incremental state at 10% of gc.max_total_size (#751)\n\n## Problem\n\nWith `gc.max_total_size` set, mbx gave learned incremental state\neverything the action store did not currently occupy, and managed\ntargets only what incremental left over. On a machine that builds about\n90 agent worktrees a day this reached 9.6 TB of incremental state across\n303 checkouts against 89 GB of managed targets. 596 managed targets had\nbeen evicted while 284 of those checkouts still held 8 TiB of\nincremental state. Targets are the only class that lets Cargo-fresh\nunits skip the shim entirely, so this was the wrong trade. A second\nmachine with the same setting (8000 GB) and 309 GB of incremental state\nwas unaffected.\n\n## Change\n\nWhen `gc.max_total_size` is set and `gc.incremental_max_size` is not,\nincremental state (shared with generated source trees) is capped at 10%\nof the total, at least 10 GiB and never above the total. An explicit\n`gc.incremental_max_size`, including `\"none\"`, still wins.\n`learned_incremental_max_size` defaults to 8 GiB again instead of being\nlifted to the total. Store and target allocation are unchanged.\n\n```toml\n[gc]\nmax_total_size = \"10TB\"   # incremental state now <= 1 TB; previously <= 10 TB minus the store\n```\n\nThe gc help and the configuration, incremental and managed-targets docs\ndescribe the new rule; `docs/cli` is regenerated.\n\n## Validation\n\nConfig tests cover 10 TiB -> 1 TiB, 100 GiB -> 10 GiB, 50 GiB -> 10 GiB\n(floor), 8 GiB -> 8 GiB (never above the total), explicit and `\"none\"`\noverrides, and the unchanged default without a total. Clippy and fmt are\nclean. `cargo test -p mbx` passes except three tests that fail\nidentically on `main` in this environment: one needs `NO_COLOR` unset\nand two depend on mtime granularity on a ZFS `TMPDIR`.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code via Mjolnir; model:\nanthropic/claude-fable-5-1 (design, review), openai/gpt-6.1-sol and\nopenai/gpt-6-luna (implementation subagents); version: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Behavior Changes**\n* When a total garbage-collection budget is configured,\nincremental-state and generated-source-tree limits now default to 10% of\nthat budget, with a 10 GiB minimum and a cap at the total budget.\n* The per-crate incremental-state limit remains 8 GiB by default, even\nwhen a total budget is set.\n* Managed targets use the remaining budget, while `--max-size` caps the\naction store’s share.\n\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated configuration and garbage-collection guidance to explain\nbudget allocation and component limits.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dad24a784edbc6a3d1da50781a8bac12cec7bcfb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/dad24a784edbc6a3d1da50781a8bac12cec7bcfb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1VyECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAWsYQAAVrck7TlkzhwmYS3FoJCCn9\nkKtXCYApEz2knaDJ05GYUgwbf7WDvk7eqBZ3JoWXFmwNVoSsyvGIj69gEIZ00Xgl\niyifRXJEIBMDTafgI/K1zgZe7afDAZpstJ73Oos7me7Q8RlldSoOOTVXrPNvfP/R\n7dQrMm8LIS2sWKbYSAaelDlVHX+jlU3WIwM8MuHRGumCzyzb113GH/GI5GSbkpqE\n3NXggWYB0IBX1UYPIoguTH0MIff3a/lPDUa8l49H4QPQme/I5p233KD1MWPZ4YPk\nzjzi+iWfnlMZkwHkvghC43TWbi+y1jBig7vrtAq/Pd3SBVNs1PBEg7yC7XA426UF\nhzk5kexAofQshhdqWAoVcjuGGzdNcAHTaFN+K5LXzKK6G2pPDgj6oxh3+CMJ+hTG\npMFDk2skvR3JlzWi1rLIflCYdSAzKR+qY2f4FBnjncrTZY/Z3hqMhN0aBIRV5Fwa\nSn4/jfZvNmFz0fQhW71JG04MHiSm3uDcloKwl/1oE/o4R7+7N6FfKcDR9N7mI9GP\nA7WK/mQoKO4T+uFOryKNGKCletiHsIdOMIyouQ58ZMrR3PYE0QqNE3zuZ8uFCKOC\nLuSgVFA7Mym3ugK8MN4/owv1fyI0nTuTmomfWL1go9pMYQs33Ja50URQ3mFhz6Wk\n7EPKtSoroqxVTmPcJaX4\n=1Nnq\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree dad24a784edbc6a3d1da50781a8bac12cec7bcfb\nparent 742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792367748 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792367748 -0500\n\nfix(gc): cap incremental state at 10% of gc.max_total_size (#751)\n\n## Problem\n\nWith `gc.max_total_size` set, mbx gave learned incremental state\neverything the action store did not currently occupy, and managed\ntargets only what incremental left over. On a machine that builds about\n90 agent worktrees a day this reached 9.6 TB of incremental state across\n303 checkouts against 89 GB of managed targets. 596 managed targets had\nbeen evicted while 284 of those checkouts still held 8 TiB of\nincremental state. Targets are the only class that lets Cargo-fresh\nunits skip the shim entirely, so this was the wrong trade. A second\nmachine with the same setting (8000 GB) and 309 GB of incremental state\nwas unaffected.\n\n## Change\n\nWhen `gc.max_total_size` is set and `gc.incremental_max_size` is not,\nincremental state (shared with generated source trees) is capped at 10%\nof the total, at least 10 GiB and never above the total. An explicit\n`gc.incremental_max_size`, including `\"none\"`, still wins.\n`learned_incremental_max_size` defaults to 8 GiB again instead of being\nlifted to the total. Store and target allocation are unchanged.\n\n```toml\n[gc]\nmax_total_size = \"10TB\"   # incremental state now <= 1 TB; previously <= 10 TB minus the store\n```\n\nThe gc help and the configuration, incremental and managed-targets docs\ndescribe the new rule; `docs/cli` is regenerated.\n\n## Validation\n\nConfig tests cover 10 TiB -> 1 TiB, 100 GiB -> 10 GiB, 50 GiB -> 10 GiB\n(floor), 8 GiB -> 8 GiB (never above the total), explicit and `\"none\"`\noverrides, and the unchanged default without a total. Clippy and fmt are\nclean. `cargo test -p mbx` passes except three tests that fail\nidentically on `main` in this environment: one needs `NO_COLOR` unset\nand two depend on mtime granularity on a ZFS `TMPDIR`.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code via Mjolnir; model:\nanthropic/claude-fable-5-1 (design, review), openai/gpt-6.1-sol and\nopenai/gpt-6-luna (implementation subagents); version: unavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Behavior Changes**\n* When a total garbage-collection budget is configured,\nincremental-state and generated-source-tree limits now default to 10% of\nthat budget, with a 10 GiB minimum and a cap at the total budget.\n* The per-crate incremental-state limit remains 8 GiB by default, even\nwhen a total budget is set.\n* Managed targets use the remaining budget, while `--max-size` caps the\naction store’s share.\n\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated configuration and garbage-collection guidance to explain\nbudget allocation and component limits.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-18T23:55:49Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/742927ea82f68e25b379ae97b6979bab90e4d84e"}]},{"sha":"65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDY1ZGE3ZjQ1MTNiMjdjMTFlMDIxMWNkYjY3NDEzMGU4MzI4YjEwZTM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-19T00:32:55Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-19T00:32:55Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): budget CPU and memory separately (#750)\n\nBuilds on #748 (merged), which lets queued builds share capacity they\ncannot use yet.\n\nmbx's scheduler charged every compile, link, and test run a single\nnumber of permits: the larger of its thread count and its predicted\nmemory divided by `scheduler.memory / scheduler.cpus`. So one number\nstood for both CPU and memory. A compile that needs 13 GiB but keeps\nonly one or two cores busy took 17 permits. Each permit also stands for\none core, so the other 15 or so cores it was charged for sat idle while\nCPU-light requests waited.\n\nThis change gives the scheduler separate budgets for CPU cores and for\nmemory bytes:\n\n- A request starts only if both its cores and its memory fit, in the\nwhole pool and in its container's limit. The per-build limit, which\ncomes from Cargo's `-j`, constrains cores only.\n- Each request asks for the number of threads mbx already charges for it\n(usually 1 for a compile), plus its predicted memory in bytes. Measured\npredictions are charged as their raw bytes. A request with no memory\nhistory is still charged today's default memory, so nothing is charged\nless memory than a guess gets today.\n- `mbx reserve --cpus N --memory M` now reserves exactly N cores and M\nbytes, instead of the larger of the two as permits.\n- A request larger than a budget may still run on its own in that\ndimension, as before. It must still fit the other dimension.\n- The priority headroom held back from low-priority work applies to both\nbudgets.\n- #748's pass check runs separately for cores and for memory. A newer\nrequest passes only if it fits the spare room in both. Checking each\ndimension on its own is stricter than a joint check, so #748's guarantee\nstill holds. For that check, memory is rounded up to 16 MiB units with a\nmargin for the rounding error. An exhaustive check of 49,152 boundary\ncases found no admission that the exact computation would refuse.\n- `mbx stats --scheduler` and the observation log show cores and memory\ncharged.\n\nThe settings are unchanged. Their descriptions now say that\n`scheduler.cpus` and `scheduler.reserve_cpus` set a core budget, and\n`scheduler.memory` sets an independent memory budget.\n\n**Measured effect.** These figures come from a replay of 6,873\nadmissions recorded by mbx's observation log on a shared build host over\nabout 3.3 hours. The replay keeps the recorded arrival and run times and\nmodels a single scope.\n\n| Policy | Total wait (request-hours) | p95 wait | p99 wait |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Single permit budget, today's ordering | 55.3 | 207 s | 286 s |\n| Separate budgets, today's ordering | 46.6 | 200 s | 253 s |\n| Single permit budget, #748 | 22.6 | 79 s | 121 s |\n| Separate budgets, #748 | 14.8 | 57 s | 81 s |\n\n- Under today's ordering, separate budgets delayed no request.\n- With #748, 21 requests started later than under a single budget, by at\nmost 48 s, because the schedule shifts.\n- While requests waited under the single budget, about 95% of the CPU\nbudget was unassigned. For 23% of that waiting time, the first request\nin the queue would have fit separate budgets. The rest of the waiting\nwas for memory that really was reserved.\n\nThe modelled baseline accounts for only part of the waiting actually\nrecorded (correlation 0.85), and the model charges exact bytes, where\ntoday's permits round up by about 2%. So read these figures as a\ncomparison between policies, not as a forecast.\n\n**Compatibility.** Lease and waiting records gain explicit core and\nmemory fields. They keep the old fields, computed exactly as before, and\na test checks 168 demand and configuration cases against the previous\ncode.\n\n- Older mbx versions sharing the cache read the same single-number\ncharge as today. That includes today's cap at the writing process's own\ncapacity, so an old reader still under-counts a request larger than the\nwriter's budget, as it does now.\n- Records from older versions are charged in full on both axes.\n- An older version decides whether its own waiting request fits by\nadding up single-number weights. So when a newer request would pass an\nolder-version waiter, it must also pass #748's single-number check,\ncomputed with that waiter's own capacities. A mixed-version property\ntest, where old waiters use the previous scheduler code to judge their\nown admission, found no older waiter delayed in 512 seeds.\n- Two new versions see each other's exact cores and bytes.\n\nKnown behavior: a compile is charged its thread count, but rustc can use\nmore threads during code generation through Cargo's jobserver. With\nmemory no longer taking up CPU permits, more small compiles can run\nalongside large ones, so the host's CPUs can be oversubscribed. That\nslows compiles but does not cause out-of-memory kills.\n\nValidation:\n\n- the scheduler tests pass (143), as do the reserve (32) and stats (11)\ntests, `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D\nwarnings`, and `cargo fmt --check`;\n- the generated CLI docs were regenerated with the pinned `usage`\ngenerator and the options from `mise.toml`, because mise was not\navailable locally;\n- the full library suite passed 1,011 tests before the final commit. The\nfinal commit changed only scheduler code and tests. Three tests failed,\nall of them outside this change:\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it`,\nand\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThese are renderer and filesystem-timing tests, run on a heavily loaded\nhost.\n\nAn independent review compared the code with the previous commit using\nthe exact functions from both. It found one mixed-version problem: the\ncontainer-limit field in waiting records had changed meaning. A second\nmixed-version problem came from review on this PR: older waiters were\nnot protected by their own single-number rule. Both are fixed here, with\nregression tests.\n\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* The scheduler now budgets CPU cores and memory independently,\nadmitting work based on both available resources.\n* Workload history and memory measurements help estimate resource needs;\nunmeasured work uses defaults.\n* Scheduler reports show separate CPU and memory charges. Reservations\ncan specify CPU and memory independently.\n* Low-priority work leaves headroom in both budgets for normal-priority\nwork.\n  * Setting `scheduler.memory` to `\"none\"` enables CPU-only scheduling.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated configuration and scheduling guides to explain resource\nbudgets, estimates, reservations, and admission behavior. **`mbx reserve\n--cpus` now describes the reserved amount in CPU cores.**\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a2107407d62f8c158682cd33ddd2d0b9c307be06","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/a2107407d62f8c158682cd33ddd2d0b9c307be06"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1WU3CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAr0IQAGrYzslpne3owU4AdVCdE9V9\n9wg1LwEJ66wNhDtvZ3tCO+wd510Ikr++XkgSU46ZqD1BTTGqBnkbjHVUjky8hUQp\nMfHBO7terTsZHbC6eifjV1sYHMQeWd3hV7qf08qC+bUJAVRkr589JFhpKJTKB0HT\n/tHjv9JEEIiTibIPJV6Jq9C7S8SuNqanV92osysbFcJGbLH+8xbcl1/6WNanQMJH\nC0gufHygc/uhMnILE/SHwsIkMUdKj9JT4aNPDg0XYK8FDIhqPrRwYEbmD+WZJocc\nBNilgLeuCGuyPjVSFkYnU0jTS+SY49jDz3oVEuddfUEh4oGz70N0lRbpgdiaOPhT\n29veCAwoQWDH56c8xfZGozyCH6E0a90P0NIhVSfLicAeo9ppVO10q0vFHT7lSt6m\nnDwNBFSgyoBlUBBxH+VMXYTw1LcPcG6rZTdHzIo6c1hsW4Z93BEeI2W3MqzJjEX2\nnwAJ5PnHYhHgaYERi5kP/qXcW6ncDuuRY04vl56i+pMDXNQOCbh8Fm9+bENL/onm\n32xThnGYWAZlOId45uePnB4tZuZyAhBaWXRsDZK6Ud+1jAJXu/evl8R59uiaHpSQ\n2g9gR2Hmk2UGFzLRwOqR26NCVdfs+rr8Ts1hWCXfc7UetlyKpi8c/EgEqPurKo3V\nDtJL8TVrmNucaf12OgWf\n=Nf5/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a2107407d62f8c158682cd33ddd2d0b9c307be06\nparent 614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792369975 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792369975 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): budget CPU and memory separately (#750)\n\nBuilds on #748 (merged), which lets queued builds share capacity they\ncannot use yet.\n\nmbx's scheduler charged every compile, link, and test run a single\nnumber of permits: the larger of its thread count and its predicted\nmemory divided by `scheduler.memory / scheduler.cpus`. So one number\nstood for both CPU and memory. A compile that needs 13 GiB but keeps\nonly one or two cores busy took 17 permits. Each permit also stands for\none core, so the other 15 or so cores it was charged for sat idle while\nCPU-light requests waited.\n\nThis change gives the scheduler separate budgets for CPU cores and for\nmemory bytes:\n\n- A request starts only if both its cores and its memory fit, in the\nwhole pool and in its container's limit. The per-build limit, which\ncomes from Cargo's `-j`, constrains cores only.\n- Each request asks for the number of threads mbx already charges for it\n(usually 1 for a compile), plus its predicted memory in bytes. Measured\npredictions are charged as their raw bytes. A request with no memory\nhistory is still charged today's default memory, so nothing is charged\nless memory than a guess gets today.\n- `mbx reserve --cpus N --memory M` now reserves exactly N cores and M\nbytes, instead of the larger of the two as permits.\n- A request larger than a budget may still run on its own in that\ndimension, as before. It must still fit the other dimension.\n- The priority headroom held back from low-priority work applies to both\nbudgets.\n- #748's pass check runs separately for cores and for memory. A newer\nrequest passes only if it fits the spare room in both. Checking each\ndimension on its own is stricter than a joint check, so #748's guarantee\nstill holds. For that check, memory is rounded up to 16 MiB units with a\nmargin for the rounding error. An exhaustive check of 49,152 boundary\ncases found no admission that the exact computation would refuse.\n- `mbx stats --scheduler` and the observation log show cores and memory\ncharged.\n\nThe settings are unchanged. Their descriptions now say that\n`scheduler.cpus` and `scheduler.reserve_cpus` set a core budget, and\n`scheduler.memory` sets an independent memory budget.\n\n**Measured effect.** These figures come from a replay of 6,873\nadmissions recorded by mbx's observation log on a shared build host over\nabout 3.3 hours. The replay keeps the recorded arrival and run times and\nmodels a single scope.\n\n| Policy | Total wait (request-hours) | p95 wait | p99 wait |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Single permit budget, today's ordering | 55.3 | 207 s | 286 s |\n| Separate budgets, today's ordering | 46.6 | 200 s | 253 s |\n| Single permit budget, #748 | 22.6 | 79 s | 121 s |\n| Separate budgets, #748 | 14.8 | 57 s | 81 s |\n\n- Under today's ordering, separate budgets delayed no request.\n- With #748, 21 requests started later than under a single budget, by at\nmost 48 s, because the schedule shifts.\n- While requests waited under the single budget, about 95% of the CPU\nbudget was unassigned. For 23% of that waiting time, the first request\nin the queue would have fit separate budgets. The rest of the waiting\nwas for memory that really was reserved.\n\nThe modelled baseline accounts for only part of the waiting actually\nrecorded (correlation 0.85), and the model charges exact bytes, where\ntoday's permits round up by about 2%. So read these figures as a\ncomparison between policies, not as a forecast.\n\n**Compatibility.** Lease and waiting records gain explicit core and\nmemory fields. They keep the old fields, computed exactly as before, and\na test checks 168 demand and configuration cases against the previous\ncode.\n\n- Older mbx versions sharing the cache read the same single-number\ncharge as today. That includes today's cap at the writing process's own\ncapacity, so an old reader still under-counts a request larger than the\nwriter's budget, as it does now.\n- Records from older versions are charged in full on both axes.\n- An older version decides whether its own waiting request fits by\nadding up single-number weights. So when a newer request would pass an\nolder-version waiter, it must also pass #748's single-number check,\ncomputed with that waiter's own capacities. A mixed-version property\ntest, where old waiters use the previous scheduler code to judge their\nown admission, found no older waiter delayed in 512 seeds.\n- Two new versions see each other's exact cores and bytes.\n\nKnown behavior: a compile is charged its thread count, but rustc can use\nmore threads during code generation through Cargo's jobserver. With\nmemory no longer taking up CPU permits, more small compiles can run\nalongside large ones, so the host's CPUs can be oversubscribed. That\nslows compiles but does not cause out-of-memory kills.\n\nValidation:\n\n- the scheduler tests pass (143), as do the reserve (32) and stats (11)\ntests, `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D\nwarnings`, and `cargo fmt --check`;\n- the generated CLI docs were regenerated with the pinned `usage`\ngenerator and the options from `mise.toml`, because mise was not\navailable locally;\n- the full library suite passed 1,011 tests before the final commit. The\nfinal commit changed only scheduler code and tests. Three tests failed,\nall of them outside this change:\n`cli::pretty::tests::mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it`,\nand\n`materialize::materialization_tests::a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThese are renderer and filesystem-timing tests, run on a heavily loaded\nhost.\n\nAn independent review compared the code with the previous commit using\nthe exact functions from both. It found one mixed-version problem: the\ncontainer-limit field in waiting records had changed meaning. A second\nmixed-version problem came from review on this PR: older waiters were\nnot protected by their own single-number rule. Both are fixed here, with\nregression tests.\n\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* The scheduler now budgets CPU cores and memory independently,\nadmitting work based on both available resources.\n* Workload history and memory measurements help estimate resource needs;\nunmeasured work uses defaults.\n* Scheduler reports show separate CPU and memory charges. Reservations\ncan specify CPU and memory independently.\n* Low-priority work leaves headroom in both budgets for normal-priority\nwork.\n  * Setting `scheduler.memory` to `\"none\"` enables CPU-only scheduling.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated configuration and scheduling guides to explain resource\nbudgets, estimates, reservations, and admission behavior. **`mbx reserve\n--cpus` now describes the reserved amount in CPU cores.**\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-19T00:32:55Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/614fd7aa64d860acf9ac26ce7f164dddb3bca06c"}]},{"sha":"b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGI1YzMyY2I1MDY0ZDQ2YTM1ZGNjYmFmMWUzNTZjM2ZiNWQ2NWY5YmM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:01:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:01:59Z"},"message":"feat(scheduler): estimate memory from a calibrated window of recent peaks (#755)\n\nmbx charged a Rust compile mode that had fewer than 3 successful samples\nthe crate's all-time maximum peak. Once a mode had 3 samples, it was\ncharged 1.5× the largest peak recorded for it, and that figure was never\nlowered. On a shared build host this produced long stalls. Two\n`brokk_bifrost_analysis` compiles in modes with only 1–2 samples were\neach charged the crate-wide 41.3 GiB while peaking at 7.3 and 23.5 GiB.\nFor about 18 minutes they held almost the whole 91.8 GiB memory budget,\nwhile about 150 small compiles waited up to 15 minutes behind them.\n\nThis replaces the thresholds with a calibrated estimate from a window of\nrecent peaks:\n\n- **Window.** Each Rust compile mode and link key keeps its last 16\nsuccessful peaks, excluding runs through another wrapper. Peaks are\ncompared on a log scale.\n- **Shared spread.** For each kind (compile, link), mbx takes the\ntypical variation of peaks within a key: the median standard deviation\nover keys with at least 4 samples. This needs at least 10 qualifying\nkeys; otherwise mbx assumes 0.5.\n- **Bound.** For a key with n samples, mbx charges an upper prediction\nbound for the next peak: `exp(mean + q · s · √(1 + 1/n))`.\n- `s` is the key's own spread, shrunk toward the shared spread when n is\nsmall.\n- `q` is calibrated from each kind's own recent prediction errors: the\n95% conformal quantile over the last 256 errors, at finite-sample rank\n`ceil(0.95 · (N+1))`. Until a kind has 50 errors, mbx uses the Student-t\nquantile instead.\n  - The charge is never below the largest peak in the window.\n- **Estimates can fall.** As the window rolls, the estimate tracks the\ncode as it is now. An out-of-memory kill still enters the window at\ntwice its measured peak.\n- **Cold starts.** A key with no window yet uses its own legacy maximum,\nif one exists, then the largest charge among sibling modes of the same\ncrate, then the default. Links fall back from their own legacy maximum\nto the profile-wide link window, then to the link floor. A mode charged\nby these fallbacks therefore never gets less than the previous rule\nwould give it.\n- **C and C++ compiles keep the previous never-lowered maximum.** Their\ncache keys are only the source file name, so same-named files in\ndifferent libraries share one key. A rolling window would let one\nlibrary's small compiles push out another's heavy peak.\n- **Test runs** keep their existing history mechanism.\n\n`mbx stats --scheduler` now shows, for each key, the sample count,\nwindow maximum, bound, charge and the estimate's source (`window`,\n`sibling`, `legacy`, `default`, `link-window`, `link-floor`). For each\nkind it shows the shared spread, the number of recorded errors and the\ncurrent `q`.\n\n**Calibration.** The 95% target was chosen by replaying 14,842 recorded\nruns from that host's observation log through the separate CPU and\nmemory budgets admission from #750. The rule was the smallest coverage\ntarget in {0.90, 0.95, 0.99} for which (a) the actual peaks of\nconcurrent runs never exceeded physical RAM and (b) at most 5% of runs\nexceeded their charge.\n\n| Charges | Runs exceeding their charge | Modelled total wait | Highest\nconcurrent actual peaks | Average unused reservation |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Previous rule (as recorded) | 0.03% | 116.5 h | 77.6 GiB | 28.0 GiB |\n| Coverage target 0.90 | 6.5% | 3.2 h | 86.4 GiB | 6.6 GiB |\n| **Coverage target 0.95 (chosen)** | **5.0%** | **4.4 h** | **85.9\nGiB** | **8.6 GiB** |\n| Coverage target 0.99 | 2.4% | 2,804 h | 90.2 GiB | 27.9 GiB |\n\nNone of the three targets ever exceeded the 91.8 GiB budget or the 98\nGiB of RAM. Peaks within a key vary by about 3.5% at the median, so a\nkey with a few samples is charged close to what it uses.\n\nAt 0.99, a few volatile keys get charges larger than the whole budget. A\ncharge that large forces the run to execute alone, so everything queues\nbehind it. That is the reason for the 2,804-hour figure.\n\nLinks exceed their charge more often than compiles: 7.9% of link runs at\n0.95, against 4.8% of compiles.\n\nThe replay keeps the recorded arrival and run times. It assumes each run\nholds its peak for its whole run time, and it counts runs with no\nmeasured peak as zero, so read it as a comparison between policies\nrather than a forecast.\n\n**Compatibility.** The windows and recorded errors are new optional\nledger fields. The legacy fields are written exactly as before, so older\nmbx versions sharing the ledger see today's values. When an older mbx\nrewrites the ledger, it drops the new fields. A new reader then falls\nback to each key's legacy maximum, so its charges never fall below what\nthe previous rule gives. A test strips the new fields from a ledger and\ncompares every compile-mode and link charge with the previous code. The\nledger grows from about 96 KB to about 254 KB with 1,024 windows. In a\nbenchmark, one locked read, update and write cycle took 7 ms at the\nmedian and 22 ms at p95.\n\nAn independent review found two ways the first version could charge too\nlittle: a link whose window was missing skipped its own legacy maximum,\nand C/C++ filename collisions under rolling windows. It also found the\nconformal quantile one rank low. All three are fixed here, with\nregression tests.\n\nValidation: the scheduler, reserve and stats tests pass (183), and so do\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check`. Before the review fixes, the full library suite\npassed 1,029 tests with three failures, all in tests this change does\nnot touch:\n`mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\n`a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it` and\n`a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThese are renderer and filesystem-timing tests on a heavily loaded host.\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Memory estimates for Rust compile and link tasks now adapt to recent\nsuccessful measurements, with separate estimates by compile mode and\nlink profile.\n* Scheduler statistics now include prediction diagnostics and\ncalibration details.\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Memory estimates respond to out-of-memory measurements by accounting\nfor higher observed peaks.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to explain rolling estimates, fallback\nbehavior, and scheduler statistics.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5b6bc3d2b7847425bd1ad55dc1cbf39ccf8e47b5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/5b6bc3d2b7847425bd1ad55dc1cbf39ccf8e47b5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1WwHCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAy7UQAABJfQCXb9SJ7fxnox6mJfDI\nFoErGeEDv5nRWr7HSwSnjU8zEr0fHehRc4fS2UlluZtIHaeJr2dYE3pid3/ANpj+\n5ld3y5IaVdeWuC8sbGLiSQbgbAb4DcJAQOjNN/Xa+XFbB0LugMbj6BmtXKJ8DQqO\nfPJoe8WPsVdY5am0/e4rvjAZ4tOD95Exs/+zOBg//VXCsfvz6HzoYaRysFl9JS5D\naco5tArwYvnEtErvc/9SYBDdetCfWlt/hO69HoeoTF3BCerayY9Xs89KFbDBvYkg\nOrkGnVlHXdr9iFsFXNFtjZ6wtFMCkjDh/XgYlcwSrWtt2DDAoik0KM7SNSpzXsMA\noEyF9oV3lipgcxC9RnHoSUEmqvY+Ta8K2+DFz/Y5FRGyl851doonTk6z3HojnCGu\nVKF4iWY7VHyTcoGPjR/SgOflmUxbDKh4rFfRH1YCuUFgHarEQRdQUqkLiQQ8GSDd\nkL0lK/UeneF6z8i6wNqlUYDv8N3sz9flOcDNWqdRZ/NAwdsFuVQpdYByfDXndxXB\n6J52h/pcnDw3AhF/PMRpA/5jYYabuQBepDgt0tFL/mB9O34ieggAbILV7RqEKOIt\n40z4ixKUjgtW8QI65dRZEwI+6lTad7emoJcH27gJPJiU4SYQ+aP6RalUXWXdQ68S\n+SglWnoeD94YErOYAA68\n=0RbC\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 5b6bc3d2b7847425bd1ad55dc1cbf39ccf8e47b5\nparent 65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792371719 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792371719 -0500\n\nfeat(scheduler): estimate memory from a calibrated window of recent peaks (#755)\n\nmbx charged a Rust compile mode that had fewer than 3 successful samples\nthe crate's all-time maximum peak. Once a mode had 3 samples, it was\ncharged 1.5× the largest peak recorded for it, and that figure was never\nlowered. On a shared build host this produced long stalls. Two\n`brokk_bifrost_analysis` compiles in modes with only 1–2 samples were\neach charged the crate-wide 41.3 GiB while peaking at 7.3 and 23.5 GiB.\nFor about 18 minutes they held almost the whole 91.8 GiB memory budget,\nwhile about 150 small compiles waited up to 15 minutes behind them.\n\nThis replaces the thresholds with a calibrated estimate from a window of\nrecent peaks:\n\n- **Window.** Each Rust compile mode and link key keeps its last 16\nsuccessful peaks, excluding runs through another wrapper. Peaks are\ncompared on a log scale.\n- **Shared spread.** For each kind (compile, link), mbx takes the\ntypical variation of peaks within a key: the median standard deviation\nover keys with at least 4 samples. This needs at least 10 qualifying\nkeys; otherwise mbx assumes 0.5.\n- **Bound.** For a key with n samples, mbx charges an upper prediction\nbound for the next peak: `exp(mean + q · s · √(1 + 1/n))`.\n- `s` is the key's own spread, shrunk toward the shared spread when n is\nsmall.\n- `q` is calibrated from each kind's own recent prediction errors: the\n95% conformal quantile over the last 256 errors, at finite-sample rank\n`ceil(0.95 · (N+1))`. Until a kind has 50 errors, mbx uses the Student-t\nquantile instead.\n  - The charge is never below the largest peak in the window.\n- **Estimates can fall.** As the window rolls, the estimate tracks the\ncode as it is now. An out-of-memory kill still enters the window at\ntwice its measured peak.\n- **Cold starts.** A key with no window yet uses its own legacy maximum,\nif one exists, then the largest charge among sibling modes of the same\ncrate, then the default. Links fall back from their own legacy maximum\nto the profile-wide link window, then to the link floor. A mode charged\nby these fallbacks therefore never gets less than the previous rule\nwould give it.\n- **C and C++ compiles keep the previous never-lowered maximum.** Their\ncache keys are only the source file name, so same-named files in\ndifferent libraries share one key. A rolling window would let one\nlibrary's small compiles push out another's heavy peak.\n- **Test runs** keep their existing history mechanism.\n\n`mbx stats --scheduler` now shows, for each key, the sample count,\nwindow maximum, bound, charge and the estimate's source (`window`,\n`sibling`, `legacy`, `default`, `link-window`, `link-floor`). For each\nkind it shows the shared spread, the number of recorded errors and the\ncurrent `q`.\n\n**Calibration.** The 95% target was chosen by replaying 14,842 recorded\nruns from that host's observation log through the separate CPU and\nmemory budgets admission from #750. The rule was the smallest coverage\ntarget in {0.90, 0.95, 0.99} for which (a) the actual peaks of\nconcurrent runs never exceeded physical RAM and (b) at most 5% of runs\nexceeded their charge.\n\n| Charges | Runs exceeding their charge | Modelled total wait | Highest\nconcurrent actual peaks | Average unused reservation |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Previous rule (as recorded) | 0.03% | 116.5 h | 77.6 GiB | 28.0 GiB |\n| Coverage target 0.90 | 6.5% | 3.2 h | 86.4 GiB | 6.6 GiB |\n| **Coverage target 0.95 (chosen)** | **5.0%** | **4.4 h** | **85.9\nGiB** | **8.6 GiB** |\n| Coverage target 0.99 | 2.4% | 2,804 h | 90.2 GiB | 27.9 GiB |\n\nNone of the three targets ever exceeded the 91.8 GiB budget or the 98\nGiB of RAM. Peaks within a key vary by about 3.5% at the median, so a\nkey with a few samples is charged close to what it uses.\n\nAt 0.99, a few volatile keys get charges larger than the whole budget. A\ncharge that large forces the run to execute alone, so everything queues\nbehind it. That is the reason for the 2,804-hour figure.\n\nLinks exceed their charge more often than compiles: 7.9% of link runs at\n0.95, against 4.8% of compiles.\n\nThe replay keeps the recorded arrival and run times. It assumes each run\nholds its peak for its whole run time, and it counts runs with no\nmeasured peak as zero, so read it as a comparison between policies\nrather than a forecast.\n\n**Compatibility.** The windows and recorded errors are new optional\nledger fields. The legacy fields are written exactly as before, so older\nmbx versions sharing the ledger see today's values. When an older mbx\nrewrites the ledger, it drops the new fields. A new reader then falls\nback to each key's legacy maximum, so its charges never fall below what\nthe previous rule gives. A test strips the new fields from a ledger and\ncompares every compile-mode and link charge with the previous code. The\nledger grows from about 96 KB to about 254 KB with 1,024 windows. In a\nbenchmark, one locked read, update and write cycle took 7 ms at the\nmedian and 22 ms at p95.\n\nAn independent review found two ways the first version could charge too\nlittle: a link whose window was missing skipped its own legacy maximum,\nand C/C++ filename collisions under rolling windows. It also found the\nconformal quantile one rank low. All three are fixed here, with\nregression tests.\n\nValidation: the scheduler, reserve and stats tests pass (183), and so do\n`cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` and\n`cargo fmt --check`. Before the review fixes, the full library suite\npassed 1,029 tests with three failures, all in tests this change does\nnot touch:\n`mascot_keeps_progress_readable_and_leaves_successful_builds`,\n`a_restore_stamp_is_never_ahead_of_a_write_that_follows_it` and\n`a_filesystem_without_clones_links_the_object_instead_of_copying_it`.\nThese are renderer and filesystem-timing tests on a heavily loaded host.\nThe full `mise run ci` gate was not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6.1-sol; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* Memory estimates for Rust compile and link tasks now adapt to recent\nsuccessful measurements, with separate estimates by compile mode and\nlink profile.\n* Scheduler statistics now include prediction diagnostics and\ncalibration details.\n* **Bug Fixes**\n* Memory estimates respond to out-of-memory measurements by accounting\nfor higher observed peaks.\n* **Documentation**\n* Updated scheduling guidance to explain rolling estimates, fallback\nbehavior, and scheduler statistics.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-19T01:02:00Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/65da7f4513b27c11e0211cdb674130e8328b10e3"}]},{"sha":"e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGU4M2Q3ZDE0M2M3ODBkOWRmYjdmNDdhNTk2ZWJlMjgxZjZhMDJiYTE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jonathan Ellis","email":"jbellis@gmail.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:57:13Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:57:13Z"},"message":"fix(scheduler): cap memory estimates at the previous rule until the spread is calibrated (#756)\n\n#755's memory estimator charges each Rust compile mode and link from a\nwindow of its recent peaks. It widens the charge by a spread pooled\nacross keys of the same kind. Until 10 keys of a kind have at least 4\nsamples, that spread falls back to a default of 0.5. On a host where\npeaks actually vary by about 3.5%, that default made a key's first\nsamples cost about 4.5 times their peak.\n\nThe calibration replay for #755 did not show this. It pooled the spread\nfrom C/C++ compile keys, which filled it almost at once. Review then\nremoved C/C++ keys from the estimator, so on a live host only Rust keys\ncount, and they take far longer to reach 10 calibrated keys.\n\nOn a shared build host running #755, within minutes of the upgrade:\n\n- 5 GiB test-binary links were charged 22–25 GiB each;\n- four leases held the entire 91.8 GiB memory budget;\n- the next large compiles would have been charged more than the whole\nbudget, so each would have run alone.\n\nThe build was rolled back after 19 minutes.\n\nWith this change, each window charge is capped at what the previous rule\ncharges for that key until both the key's kind is calibrated (10 keys\nwith at least 4 samples) and the key itself has at least 4 samples. The\nper-key condition matters because a calibrated kind can still have a\nwide spread, and a key with a single sample would then jump far above\nits previous charge without receiving a new sample. For compile modes,\nthe previous rule is the per-mode maximum with 1.5× headroom after three\nsuccesses, otherwise the crate-wide maximum. For links, it is the\nper-link maximum, otherwise the profile window's maximum, otherwise the\nlink floor. A charge is never capped below the largest peak in its\nwindow. A key with no previous charge, such as a light crate that never\ngot a legacy entry, is capped at the default one-permit charge the\nprevious rule gave it. The cap only ever lowers a charge, so the\nestimator still cuts over-charging immediately.\n\nThe cap applies to admission and to the charges `mbx stats --scheduler`\nreports.\n\nTests:\n\n- new tests cover the cap for compile modes and links, the floor at the\nwindow maximum, the cap lifting once a kind is calibrated and a key has\n4 samples, each link fallback, keys with no previous charge, and a\ncalibrated kind with a wide spread (sigma about 0.5) that must not uncap\na key with one sample;\n- estimator-math tests now use calibrated fixtures, so they still\nexercise the uncapped path;\n- existing expectations that assumed uncapped warm-up charges now expect\nthe previous rule's charge, for example a first-sample sequence of 366,\n245, 203, 181 bytes becomes 366, 245, 122, 122.\n\nAn independent review on the affected host found the per-key and\nno-history gaps before this was installed; both are fixed here.\n\nValidation: the scheduler, reserve and stats tests pass (189 together),\nas do `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`\nand `cargo fmt --check`. The full library suite and the full `mise run\nci` gate were not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Improvements**\n* Compile and link memory predictions are limited during warm-up when\nprior charge information is available, helping keep estimates within\nestablished bounds until more samples accumulate.\n* Compile predictions can use the configured permit size as a default\nwhen prior charge information is unavailable. Link predictions use\nhistorical peak values or an applicable baseline as their warm-up\nreference.\n* As estimates become calibrated, predictions can reflect the full\nmeasured values.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"479a1d9d50ff90daad4dcd6f240a301e1e3a36e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/479a1d9d50ff90daad4dcd6f240a301e1e3a36e4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1Xj5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAunIQADjzClufmIAWPkxuwcXEIeCh\njdD7XFEC6FUjcAKPI9IOLP07w54NDjgf9siQ7uI0DSAux8zJvQZKXQqkQjL125Np\nGnILgovbtfVL+ctECQt57tOk9uwBhaY5eGLPTltTHjyjewSerdS6D7ItT+bZRibA\nD9KC3wzrih8IVaxOGFIeBJ/fizcS3PTp4NWHxiWpCtewxgdl3XWcSWEWzSmYAqiq\nGdKE9vHOX5xlzpPGcTWJclOKUJ1GkVlR2JdmL4PJevWzAQykMnAj34WHYAPYo8iZ\nBJNSUHt3Ykb4qWLfCKetvXDRhuuUzxK4zNBcsaCFU4N8IJHcpFVbP2tGY1rXe2xd\nRLGcKCd3iaSh5ie9vkiD2ObqAX6GkPn9iAufCt6SE8xeLbVQjk/6ARSO7Pwh/+Fw\nH2QA+isgr7we+K6D/omfPQzRkFKlpeh4mFS2sZ0asQ4qJ3d2e/ygUlMI8Z11ABNX\n0+WEYtH6IP1bPgOp+qO8Pj2plhG5odlHddjJjMaE8YSyTtQVsX50uTEaiE196asi\nKHjvdSmQCO4IpCGkD1iGYR0ToEVwCY8bB6OYpmYbGPlQJoR+GFrFS7DGH9y0CSK3\nkC45s9bGeYZ9PLwgL271vt4TvomBBoRuXyn0rzW22Da0KbmZfNEIbERo68uZ5Q2f\nCMTIH4MEiFMqLmAPlD/H\n=pVlp\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 479a1d9d50ff90daad4dcd6f240a301e1e3a36e4\nparent b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc\nauthor Jonathan Ellis <jbellis@gmail.com> 1792375033 +0900\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792375033 +0000\n\nfix(scheduler): cap memory estimates at the previous rule until the spread is calibrated (#756)\n\n#755's memory estimator charges each Rust compile mode and link from a\nwindow of its recent peaks. It widens the charge by a spread pooled\nacross keys of the same kind. Until 10 keys of a kind have at least 4\nsamples, that spread falls back to a default of 0.5. On a host where\npeaks actually vary by about 3.5%, that default made a key's first\nsamples cost about 4.5 times their peak.\n\nThe calibration replay for #755 did not show this. It pooled the spread\nfrom C/C++ compile keys, which filled it almost at once. Review then\nremoved C/C++ keys from the estimator, so on a live host only Rust keys\ncount, and they take far longer to reach 10 calibrated keys.\n\nOn a shared build host running #755, within minutes of the upgrade:\n\n- 5 GiB test-binary links were charged 22–25 GiB each;\n- four leases held the entire 91.8 GiB memory budget;\n- the next large compiles would have been charged more than the whole\nbudget, so each would have run alone.\n\nThe build was rolled back after 19 minutes.\n\nWith this change, each window charge is capped at what the previous rule\ncharges for that key until both the key's kind is calibrated (10 keys\nwith at least 4 samples) and the key itself has at least 4 samples. The\nper-key condition matters because a calibrated kind can still have a\nwide spread, and a key with a single sample would then jump far above\nits previous charge without receiving a new sample. For compile modes,\nthe previous rule is the per-mode maximum with 1.5× headroom after three\nsuccesses, otherwise the crate-wide maximum. For links, it is the\nper-link maximum, otherwise the profile window's maximum, otherwise the\nlink floor. A charge is never capped below the largest peak in its\nwindow. A key with no previous charge, such as a light crate that never\ngot a legacy entry, is capped at the default one-permit charge the\nprevious rule gave it. The cap only ever lowers a charge, so the\nestimator still cuts over-charging immediately.\n\nThe cap applies to admission and to the charges `mbx stats --scheduler`\nreports.\n\nTests:\n\n- new tests cover the cap for compile modes and links, the floor at the\nwindow maximum, the cap lifting once a kind is calibrated and a key has\n4 samples, each link fallback, keys with no previous charge, and a\ncalibrated kind with a wide spread (sigma about 0.5) that must not uncap\na key with one sample;\n- estimator-math tests now use calibrated fixtures, so they still\nexercise the uncapped path;\n- existing expectations that assumed uncapped warm-up charges now expect\nthe previous rule's charge, for example a first-sample sequence of 366,\n245, 203, 181 bytes becomes 366, 245, 122, 122.\n\nAn independent review on the affected host found the per-key and\nno-history gaps before this was installed; both are fixed here.\n\nValidation: the scheduler, reserve and stats tests pass (189 together),\nas do `cargo clippy -p mbx --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`\nand `cargo fmt --check`. The full library suite and the full `mise run\nci` gate were not run locally.\n\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Claude Code; model: Anthropic/claude-opus-5-5;\nversion: 2.1.302.*\n*AI-assisted — Tool: Codex; model: OpenAI/gpt-6-luna; version:\nunavailable.*\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Improvements**\n* Compile and link memory predictions are limited during warm-up when\nprior charge information is available, helping keep estimates within\nestablished bounds until more samples accumulate.\n* Compile predictions can use the configured permit size as a default\nwhen prior charge information is unavailable. Link predictions use\nhistorical peak values or an applicable baseline as their warm-up\nreference.\n* As estimates become calibrated, predictions can reflect the full\nmeasured values.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-19T01:57:14Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1/comments","author":{"login":"jbellis","id":42158,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQyMTU4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/42158?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis","html_url":"https://github.com/jbellis","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jbellis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/b5c32cb5064d46a35dccbaf1e356c3fb5d65f9bc"}]},{"sha":"edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKGVkZDAzZDA0N2MzMGY0Nzk5ODUyOTY4MmFmMWExYjVkN2Y5ZTU4ZjE","commit":{"author":{"name":"ViRb3","email":"ViRb3@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:58:41Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-19T01:58:41Z"},"message":"feat: seed incremental state (#754)\n\nI have been doing some serious agentic development in a Rust codebase\nlately, with 3-5 worktrees in parallel. As the codebase grew, I noticed\nthat most of the dev time was wasted compiling - that's when I found\nmbx. It helped a lot, but fresh worktrees were significantly slower than\na warm incremental build. I set out to investigate it, and with the help\nof Claude came up with the following implementation. It has been tested\non macOS, Windows and Linux, all 3 confirmed to produce valid hard links\nof incremental cache and a new worktree compiles just as fast as its\nsource branch/worktree.\n\n- **feat(incremental): start a new checkout's edited crates from another\ncheckout's state**\n- **fix(incremental): seeded state starts as warm as the checkout's\nown**\n- **fix(incremental): recognise either path separator under the working\ndirectory on Windows**\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* With Rust’s `-Zremap-cwd-prefix` option configured and rustc 1.98 or\nlater, incremental build state can be reused across checkouts of the\nsame crate with matching build settings, helping avoid recompiling\nunchanged code.\n* Paths under the working directory are recorded relative to it, so\ndebug information and panic messages use the remapped path.\n* **Changes**\n* State from inactive checkouts remains available for reuse until it\nexpires or is removed during collection.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->","tree":{"sha":"d66556e997fc9c763533040c86f3d2d17ed7b67a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/d66556e997fc9c763533040c86f3d2d17ed7b67a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1XlRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAb+sQACUaINsaUDt2nBWO6JdZvwVp\nJRZRMGIVMmL2fBJR80DEKO/QEA4QCPhUhl7zNf1VUTKxnfyvzlunGONZzO/oIOHR\ntzIPTdfzv0dHLLLz2bn53k7A0ssQRHnVbz5DZWJW5qpa7FVw2+D+QYNbEwDorTiX\nKPPy3OIhbeZi8JrZh78nX0mGOYQz9X1EApibkpz8HFEGcNc72onXNJTJllyVBf+B\n7OiOswzn+na97GCR+T3QcyBFMBqaeaOb5nmVZZJttudoI7b5T7il5/kgwHW5RrzF\nIkuwSwKhR3jce9sJc/BvdqazDFKAK4S9CNCLHU9lBf/wqeQXINI4CYjA7y2rBTPy\nyjdHhJNdj3P+dz4NZDaM77PMt7B++lICO8gMEV1tw5osQ2gveq2nkTXoSv+29ih7\nNVCFtwwDbzVAbaVbysC9Ue/sm35IIxG0186jNloVIxVISJVvwiddHh9VZAblG+3L\n7uJWWlYeZ5e5uJzefySWHspsvleaXBK7oQgyHKjsxD/t/AMIgc24OVu7FofxUNsa\noz+ZfEomECsxYKMoAQvLUsql1XBANg0R9NiXuPnVnMWVmq0k1GfUnqTy2hd+UnV9\nrhTtHsITM2vL4pLRKauL0/4znzmnpziDaXUt2Ftxoj+Jida3dbj3+wd5WqdUFpfN\n0IRzsv2iVVMJTCCU0SL+\n=hEje\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree d66556e997fc9c763533040c86f3d2d17ed7b67a\nparent e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1\nauthor ViRb3 <ViRb3@users.noreply.github.com> 1792375121 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792375121 +0000\n\nfeat: seed incremental state (#754)\n\nI have been doing some serious agentic development in a Rust codebase\nlately, with 3-5 worktrees in parallel. As the codebase grew, I noticed\nthat most of the dev time was wasted compiling - that's when I found\nmbx. It helped a lot, but fresh worktrees were significantly slower than\na warm incremental build. I set out to investigate it, and with the help\nof Claude came up with the following implementation. It has been tested\non macOS, Windows and Linux, all 3 confirmed to produce valid hard links\nof incremental cache and a new worktree compiles just as fast as its\nsource branch/worktree.\n\n- **feat(incremental): start a new checkout's edited crates from another\ncheckout's state**\n- **fix(incremental): seeded state starts as warm as the checkout's\nown**\n- **fix(incremental): recognise either path separator under the working\ndirectory on Windows**\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai\n-->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n* With Rust’s `-Zremap-cwd-prefix` option configured and rustc 1.98 or\nlater, incremental build state can be reused across checkouts of the\nsame crate with matching build settings, helping avoid recompiling\nunchanged code.\n* Paths under the working directory are recorded relative to it, so\ndebug information and panic messages use the remapped path.\n* **Changes**\n* State from inactive checkouts remains available for reuse until it\nexpires or is removed during collection.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->","verified_at":"2026-10-19T01:58:41Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1/comments","author":{"login":"ViRb3","id":2650170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI2NTAxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2650170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3","html_url":"https://github.com/ViRb3","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ViRb3/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/e83d7d143c780d9dfb7f47a596ebe281f6a02ba1"}]},{"sha":"2ade2bc6b661f1b40a55cfa7083fa9a4efc9348d","node_id":"C_kwDOT-nLJdoAKDJhZGUyYmM2YjY2MWYxYjQwYTU1Y2ZhNzA4M2ZhOWE0ZWZjOTM0OGQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"jdx","email":"216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-10-19T10:18:47Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-10-19T10:18:47Z"},"message":"chore: release (#723)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/176\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n\n\n\n## 🤖 New release\n\n* `mbx-cache-core`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx-cache-cc`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx-cache-rustc`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx`: 1.24.1 -> 1.25.0\n* `mbx-cache-cargo`: 0.3.14 -> 0.3.15\n* `mbx-cache-store`: 0.1.40 -> 0.1.41\n\n<details><summary><i><b>Changelog</b></i></summary><p>\n\n## `mbx-cache-core`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-core-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-core-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-cc`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-cc-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-cc-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Fixed\n\n- *(cc)* enumerate include directories on every manifest request\n([#738](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/738))\n- *(cc)* identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text\n([#735](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/735))\n\n### Other\n\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-rustc`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-rustc-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-rustc-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Added\n\n- seed incremental state\n([#754](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/754))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[1.25.0](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/v1.24.1...v1.25.0)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Added\n\n- seed incremental state\n([#754](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/754))\n- *(scheduler)* estimate memory from a calibrated window of recent peaks\n([#755](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/755))\n- *(scheduler)* budget CPU and memory separately\n([#750](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/750))\n- *(scheduler)* let queued builds share capacity they cannot use yet\n([#748](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/748))\n- *(scheduler)* record build identity and reservations in the\nobservation log ([#749](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/749))\n- *(scheduler)* record estimate accuracy and summarize it in mbx stats\n([#746](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/746))\n- *(scheduler)* estimate compile memory for each build mode\n([#742](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/742))\n- *(cache)* add remote.trust_writes for CI systems mbx does not detect\n([#724](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/724))\n\n### Fixed\n\n- *(scheduler)* cap memory estimates at the previous rule until the\nspread is calibrated\n([#756](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/756))\n- *(gc)* cap incremental state at 10% of gc.max_total_size\n([#751](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/751))\n- *(gc)* prune incremental state after worktree removal\n([#752](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/752))\n- *(materialize)* stop restored build scripts from rerunning on the next\nunchanged build ([#745](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/745))\n- *(scheduler)* honor an explicit memory budget that fits the local\nmachine ([#741](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/741))\n- *(setup)* skip mise's mbx shim when the Windows cargo.exe shim\nforwards ([#736](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/736))\n- *(cc)* identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text\n([#735](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/735))\n- *(cli)* let cargo clean -p run when a managed target link dangles\n([#732](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/732))\n- *(cli)* report build.rustc-wrapper = \"mbx\" instead of failing with\n-Zscript ([#727](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/727))\n- *(scheduler)* prevent starvation across shared container builds\n([#721](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/721))\n\n### Other\n\n- *(setup)* keep the Windows end-to-end tests out of the developer's\nprofile ([#737](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/737))\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n- *(seed)* run the access-time seeding test on Windows volumes that\ntrack access times\n([#730](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/730))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-cargo`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.3.15](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-cargo-v0.3.14...mbx-cache-cargo-v0.3.15)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- updated the following local packages: mbx-cache-core\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-store`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.1.41](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-store-v0.1.40...mbx-cache-store-v0.1.41)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- updated the following local packages: mbx-cache-core\n</blockquote>\n\n\n</p></details>\n\n---\nThis PR was generated with\n[release-plz](https://github.com/release-plz/release-plz/).\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> This PR only bumps versions, lockfile entries, and changelogs;\nbehavioral risk comes from the already-merged features documented in\nthose changelogs (scheduler and cache), not from new code in the diff.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> **Release cut** for **mbx 1.25.0** and aligned workspace crates\n(`mbx-cache-core` / `mbx-cache-cc` / `mbx-cache-rustc` **0.20.3**,\n`mbx-cache-cargo` **0.3.15**, `mbx-cache-store` **0.1.41**). The diff\nupdates `Cargo.toml` versions, path dependency pins, `Cargo.lock`,\nper-crate **CHANGELOG** entries dated 2026-10-19, and the generated CLI\ndoc version in `docs/cli/index.md`.\n> \n> No application logic changes in this PR—it packages work already\nmerged. **1.25.0** changelog highlights include **incremental state\nseeding**, a **scheduler** overhaul (separate CPU/memory budgets,\ncalibrated memory estimates, shared queued capacity, observation logging\nand stats), **`remote.trust_writes`**, plus fixes for GC/incremental\nretention, MSVC/cc include handling, materialized build scripts, and\nWindows setup/CLI edges. **`mbx-cache-cc` 0.20.3** documents MSVC\ndetection and include-directory manifest fixes.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\nc5a2685d5292683eb4a577a0b2612e57a738f865. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"54170d2d1f8fa99bbd7f1bbce328cd69562e6305","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/trees/54170d2d1f8fa99bbd7f1bbce328cd69562e6305"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/git/commits/2ade2bc6b661f1b40a55cfa7083fa9a4efc9348d","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq1e6HCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQ8AQABUK5J+WEAO4YMEmxrgOx4Fj\n83gErL97hl8wv+udoNJMJNq51w9nyy042qfTeDok591MypqKd5eB30DbHNHKBG7Q\nyVkRvJCsXxj2yvAyrhmXdnX/uNacRby3nP4oeOwzRPIMJsmxP7tHIJ2RqxT3UfiD\nHAsjO93gAWxjHW4w3pFxhB08cNI3Yv8XfuTKEB43hdT2FtZuM/REiSg8JDNewAVV\ngHFrENonVWYU/w8wJusJRwvKdKLESIk31tJB5pFGg9UzbSrMkxk6Kk950TLjhMlv\n3B67YAT02HU+51tYHRdTMaZqPSgp1u3FmltA6n2cw+G0/i/ij9ikJNkt/DrBGVnX\n5aNUDBdCx18Mhul7JS4ves7usK+GKGbuasG8Fskxq8iMMJFgP3V5OqDhHBHmjcei\noJaQguNGdjVkJ5OxSbP5SIlId8hOXBoKAKd1Gua/ll+QzUMB6fkuSDohLIRWNk46\niVQsq3uZjN0eDOfCFISTvCbjoMLDnKtvEG5OwfM0fWDie6Hgmf2pFa9y5s+z3rK4\n57NxkZFVkoCLk4CnLqa8IlfxO2ZistuqwIPEdJE2SO1UFRy1hFRIj0L/HOeqvNW0\nUXQGZNK5EzwLVCa3U7QPueABz3YMWXaAzWfLqye7h1lAbfAYoTyvfGBCwl4tego7\nG5kAEpBnsDE+JKd8nx5t\n=mbja\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 54170d2d1f8fa99bbd7f1bbce328cd69562e6305\nparent edd03d047c30f47998529682af1a1b5d7f9e58f1\nauthor jdx <216188+jdx@users.noreply.github.com> 1792405127 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792405127 -0500\n\nchore: release (#723)\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/176\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n\n\n\n## 🤖 New release\n\n* `mbx-cache-core`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx-cache-cc`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx-cache-rustc`: 0.20.2 -> 0.20.3 (✓ API compatible changes)\n* `mbx`: 1.24.1 -> 1.25.0\n* `mbx-cache-cargo`: 0.3.14 -> 0.3.15\n* `mbx-cache-store`: 0.1.40 -> 0.1.41\n\n<details><summary><i><b>Changelog</b></i></summary><p>\n\n## `mbx-cache-core`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-core-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-core-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-cc`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-cc-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-cc-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Fixed\n\n- *(cc)* enumerate include directories on every manifest request\n([#738](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/738))\n- *(cc)* identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text\n([#735](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/735))\n\n### Other\n\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-rustc`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.20.3](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-rustc-v0.20.2...mbx-cache-rustc-v0.20.3)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Added\n\n- seed incremental state\n([#754](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/754))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[1.25.0](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/v1.24.1...v1.25.0)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Added\n\n- seed incremental state\n([#754](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/754))\n- *(scheduler)* estimate memory from a calibrated window of recent peaks\n([#755](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/755))\n- *(scheduler)* budget CPU and memory separately\n([#750](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/750))\n- *(scheduler)* let queued builds share capacity they cannot use yet\n([#748](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/748))\n- *(scheduler)* record build identity and reservations in the\nobservation log ([#749](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/749))\n- *(scheduler)* record estimate accuracy and summarize it in mbx stats\n([#746](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/746))\n- *(scheduler)* estimate compile memory for each build mode\n([#742](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/742))\n- *(cache)* add remote.trust_writes for CI systems mbx does not detect\n([#724](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/724))\n\n### Fixed\n\n- *(scheduler)* cap memory estimates at the previous rule until the\nspread is calibrated\n([#756](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/756))\n- *(gc)* cap incremental state at 10% of gc.max_total_size\n([#751](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/751))\n- *(gc)* prune incremental state after worktree removal\n([#752](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/752))\n- *(materialize)* stop restored build scripts from rerunning on the next\nunchanged build ([#745](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/745))\n- *(scheduler)* honor an explicit memory budget that fits the local\nmachine ([#741](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/741))\n- *(setup)* skip mise's mbx shim when the Windows cargo.exe shim\nforwards ([#736](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/736))\n- *(cc)* identify MSVC from preprocessor macros, not banner text\n([#735](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/735))\n- *(cli)* let cargo clean -p run when a managed target link dangles\n([#732](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/732))\n- *(cli)* report build.rustc-wrapper = \"mbx\" instead of failing with\n-Zscript ([#727](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/727))\n- *(scheduler)* prevent starvation across shared container builds\n([#721](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/721))\n\n### Other\n\n- *(setup)* keep the Windows end-to-end tests out of the developer's\nprofile ([#737](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/737))\n- fix clippy findings that only appear on Windows\n([#733](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/733))\n- *(seed)* run the access-time seeding test on Windows volumes that\ntrack access times\n([#730](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/730))\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-cargo`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.3.15](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-cargo-v0.3.14...mbx-cache-cargo-v0.3.15)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- updated the following local packages: mbx-cache-core\n</blockquote>\n\n## `mbx-cache-store`\n\n<blockquote>\n\n##\n[0.1.41](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/mbx-cache-store-v0.1.40...mbx-cache-store-v0.1.41)\n- 2026-10-19\n\n### Other\n\n- updated the following local packages: mbx-cache-core\n</blockquote>\n\n\n</p></details>\n\n---\nThis PR was generated with\n[release-plz](https://github.com/release-plz/release-plz/).\n\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Low Risk**\n> This PR only bumps versions, lockfile entries, and changelogs;\nbehavioral risk comes from the already-merged features documented in\nthose changelogs (scheduler and cache), not from new code in the diff.\n> \n> **Overview**\n> **Release cut** for **mbx 1.25.0** and aligned workspace crates\n(`mbx-cache-core` / `mbx-cache-cc` / `mbx-cache-rustc` **0.20.3**,\n`mbx-cache-cargo` **0.3.15**, `mbx-cache-store` **0.1.41**). The diff\nupdates `Cargo.toml` versions, path dependency pins, `Cargo.lock`,\nper-crate **CHANGELOG** entries dated 2026-10-19, and the generated CLI\ndoc version in `docs/cli/index.md`.\n> \n> No application logic changes in this PR—it packages work already\nmerged. **1.25.0** changelog highlights include **incremental state\nseeding**, a **scheduler** overhaul (separate CPU/memory budgets,\ncalibrated memory estimates, shared queued capacity, observation logging\nand stats), **`remote.trust_writes`**, plus fixes for GC/incremental\nretention, MSVC/cc include handling, materialized build scripts, and\nWindows setup/CLI edges. **`mbx-cache-cc` 0.20.3** documents MSVC\ndetection and include-directory manifest fixes.\n> \n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit\nc5a2685d5292683eb4a577a0b2612e57a738f865. Bugbot is set up for automated\ncode reviews on this repo. Configure\n[here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","verified_at":"2026-10-19T10:18:47Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2ade2bc6b661f1b40a55cfa7083fa9a4efc9348d","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/commit/2ade2bc6b661f1b40a55cfa7083fa9a4efc9348d","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/commits/2ade2bc6b661f1b40a55cfa7083fa9a4efc9348d/comments","author":{"login":"jdx","id":216188,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxNjE4OA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/216188?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx","html_url":"https://github.com/jdx","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jdx/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://